window.SHAKESPEARE_ENGLISH["2henryvi"] = {"id":"2henryvi","title":"Henry VI, part 2","kind":"play","source":"MIT Shakespeare (Complete Moby Shakespeare)","sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/2henryvi/full.html","sourceRevision":"6b82db852c7322dd33e95db347f0ddfc812c6409","sections":[{"id":"1.1","label":"Act 1 · SCENE I. London. The palace.","act":1,"sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/2henryvi/full.html","blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Flourish of trumpets: then hautboys. Enter KING HENRY VI, GLOUCESTER, SALISBURY, WARWICK, and CARDINAL, on the one side; QUEEN MARGARET, SUFFOLK, YORK, SOMERSET, and BUCKINGHAM, on the other","id":"2henryvi:1.1:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"As by your high imperial majesty\nI had in charge at my depart for France,\nAs procurator to your excellence,\nTo marry Princess Margaret for your grace,\nSo, in the famous ancient city, Tours,\nIn presence of the Kings of France and Sicil,\nThe Dukes of Orleans, Calaber, Bretagne and Alencon,\nSeven earls, twelve barons and twenty reverend bishops,\nI have perform'd my task and was espoused:\nAnd humbly now upon my bended knee,\nIn sight of England and her lordly peers,\nDeliver up my title in the queen\nTo your most gracious hands, that are the substance\nOf that great shadow I did represent;\nThe happiest gift that ever marquess gave,\nThe fairest queen that ever king received.","sourceLine":"1.1.1","speaker":"SUFFOLK","id":"2henryvi:1.1:2"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Suffolk, arise. Welcome, Queen Margaret:\nI can express no kinder sign of love\nThan this kind kiss. O Lord, that lends me life,\nLend me a heart replete with thankfulness!\nFor thou hast given me in this beauteous face\nA world of earthly blessings to my soul,\nIf sympathy of love unite our thoughts.","sourceLine":"1.1.17","speaker":"KING HENRY VI","id":"2henryvi:1.1:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Great King of England and my gracious lord,\nThe mutual conference that my mind hath had,\nBy day, by night, waking and in my dreams,\nIn courtly company or at my beads,\nWith you, mine alder-liefest sovereign,\nMakes me the bolder to salute my king\nWith ruder terms, such as my wit affords\nAnd over-joy of heart doth minister.","sourceLine":"1.1.24","speaker":"QUEEN MARGARET","id":"2henryvi:1.1:4"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Her sight did ravish; but her grace in speech,\nHer words y-clad with wisdom's majesty,\nMakes me from wondering fall to weeping joys;\nSuch is the fulness of my heart's content.\nLords, with one cheerful voice welcome my love.","sourceLine":"1.1.32","speaker":"KING HENRY VI","id":"2henryvi:1.1:5"},{"kind":"speech","text":"[Kneeling] Long live Queen Margaret, England's\nhappiness!","sourceLine":"1.1.37","speaker":"ALL","id":"2henryvi:1.1:6"},{"kind":"speech","text":"We thank you all.","sourceLine":"1.1.39","speaker":"QUEEN MARGARET","id":"2henryvi:1.1:7"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Flourish","id":"2henryvi:1.1:8"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My lord protector, so it please your grace,\nHere are the articles of contracted peace\nBetween our sovereign and the French king Charles,\nFor eighteen months concluded by consent.","sourceLine":"1.1.40","speaker":"SUFFOLK","id":"2henryvi:1.1:9"},{"kind":"speech","text":"[Reads] 'Imprimis, it is agreed between the French\nking Charles, and William de la Pole, Marquess of\nSuffolk, ambassador for Henry King of England, that\nthe said Henry shall espouse the Lady Margaret,\ndaughter unto Reignier King of Naples, Sicilia and\nJerusalem, and crown her Queen of England ere the\nthirtieth of May next ensuing. Item, that the duchy\nof Anjou and the county of Maine shall be released\nand delivered to the king her father'--","sourceLine":"1.1.44","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"2henryvi:1.1:10"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Lets the paper fall","id":"2henryvi:1.1:11"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Uncle, how now!","sourceLine":"1.1.53","speaker":"KING HENRY VI","id":"2henryvi:1.1:12"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Pardon me, gracious lord;\nSome sudden qualm hath struck me at the heart\nAnd dimm'd mine eyes, that I can read no further.","sourceLine":"1.1.54","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"2henryvi:1.1:13"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Uncle of Winchester, I pray, read on.","sourceLine":"1.1.57","speaker":"KING HENRY VI","id":"2henryvi:1.1:14"},{"kind":"speech","text":"[Reads] 'Item, It is further agreed between them,\nthat the duchies of Anjou and Maine shall be\nreleased and delivered over to the king her father,\nand she sent over of the King of England's own\nproper cost and charges, without having any dowry.'","sourceLine":"1.1.58","speaker":"CARDINAL","id":"2henryvi:1.1:15"},{"kind":"speech","text":"They please us well. Lord marquess, kneel down:\nWe here create thee the first duke of Suffolk,\nAnd gird thee with the sword. Cousin of York,\nWe here discharge your grace from being regent\nI' the parts of France, till term of eighteen months\nBe full expired. Thanks, uncle Winchester,\nGloucester, York, Buckingham, Somerset,\nSalisbury, and Warwick;\nWe thank you all for the great favour done,\nIn entertainment to my princely queen.\nCome, let us in, and with all speed provide\nTo see her coronation be perform'd.","sourceLine":"1.1.63","speaker":"KING HENRY VI","id":"2henryvi:1.1:16"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt KING HENRY VI, QUEEN MARGARET, and SUFFOLK","id":"2henryvi:1.1:17"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Brave peers of England, pillars of the state,\nTo you Duke Humphrey must unload his grief,\nYour grief, the common grief of all the land.\nWhat! did my brother Henry spend his youth,\nHis valour, coin and people, in the wars?\nDid he so often lodge in open field,\nIn winter's cold and summer's parching heat,\nTo conquer France, his true inheritance?\nAnd did my brother Bedford toil his wits,\nTo keep by policy what Henry got?\nHave you yourselves, Somerset, Buckingham,\nBrave York, Salisbury, and victorious Warwick,\nReceived deep scars in France and Normandy?\nOr hath mine uncle Beaufort and myself,\nWith all the learned council of the realm,\nStudied so long, sat in the council-house\nEarly and late, debating to and fro\nHow France and Frenchmen might be kept in awe,\nAnd had his highness in his infancy\nCrowned in Paris in despite of foes?\nAnd shall these labours and these honours die?\nShall Henry's conquest, Bedford's vigilance,\nYour deeds of war and all our counsel die?\nO peers of England, shameful is this league!\nFatal this marriage, cancelling your fame,\nBlotting your names from books of memory,\nRazing the characters of your renown,\nDefacing monuments of conquer'd France,\nUndoing all, as all had never been!","sourceLine":"1.1.75","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"2henryvi:1.1:18"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Nephew, what means this passionate discourse,\nThis peroration with such circumstance?\nFor France, 'tis ours; and we will keep it still.","sourceLine":"1.1.104","speaker":"CARDINAL","id":"2henryvi:1.1:19"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ay, uncle, we will keep it, if we can;\nBut now it is impossible we should:\nSuffolk, the new-made duke that rules the roast,\nHath given the duchy of Anjou and Maine\nUnto the poor King Reignier, whose large style\nAgrees not with the leanness of his purse.","sourceLine":"1.1.107","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"2henryvi:1.1:20"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Now, by the death of Him that died for all,\nThese counties were the keys of Normandy.\nBut wherefore weeps Warwick, my valiant son?","sourceLine":"1.1.113","speaker":"SALISBURY","id":"2henryvi:1.1:21"},{"kind":"speech","text":"For grief that they are past recovery:\nFor, were there hope to conquer them again,\nMy sword should shed hot blood, mine eyes no tears.\nAnjou and Maine! myself did win them both;\nThose provinces these arms of mine did conquer:\nAnd are the cities, that I got with wounds,\nDelivered up again with peaceful words?\nMort Dieu!","sourceLine":"1.1.116","speaker":"WARWICK","id":"2henryvi:1.1:22"},{"kind":"speech","text":"For Suffolk's duke, may he be suffocate,\nThat dims the honour of this warlike isle!\nFrance should have torn and rent my very heart,\nBefore I would have yielded to this league.\nI never read but England's kings have had\nLarge sums of gold and dowries with their wives:\nAnd our King Henry gives away his own,\nTo match with her that brings no vantages.","sourceLine":"1.1.124","speaker":"YORK","id":"2henryvi:1.1:23"},{"kind":"speech","text":"A proper jest, and never heard before,\nThat Suffolk should demand a whole fifteenth\nFor costs and charges in transporting her!\nShe should have stayed in France and starved\nin France, Before--","sourceLine":"1.1.132","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"2henryvi:1.1:24"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My Lord of Gloucester, now ye grow too hot:\nIt was the pleasure of my lord the King.","sourceLine":"1.1.137","speaker":"CARDINAL","id":"2henryvi:1.1:25"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My Lord of Winchester, I know your mind;\n'Tis not my speeches that you do mislike,\nBut 'tis my presence that doth trouble ye.\nRancour will out: proud prelate, in thy face\nI see thy fury: if I longer stay,\nWe shall begin our ancient bickerings.\nLordings, farewell; and say, when I am gone,\nI prophesied France will be lost ere long.","sourceLine":"1.1.139","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"2henryvi:1.1:26"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit","id":"2henryvi:1.1:27"},{"kind":"speech","text":"So, there goes our protector in a rage.\n'Tis known to you he is mine enemy,\nNay, more, an enemy unto you all,\nAnd no great friend, I fear me, to the king.\nConsider, lords, he is the next of blood,\nAnd heir apparent to the English crown:\nHad Henry got an empire by his marriage,\nAnd all the wealthy kingdoms of the west,\nThere's reason he should be displeased at it.\nLook to it, lords! let not his smoothing words\nBewitch your hearts; be wise and circumspect.\nWhat though the common people favour him,\nCalling him 'Humphrey, the good Duke of\nGloucester,'\nClapping their hands, and crying with loud voice,\n'Jesu maintain your royal excellence!'\nWith 'God preserve the good Duke Humphrey!'\nI fear me, lords, for all this flattering gloss,\nHe will be found a dangerous protector.","sourceLine":"1.1.147","speaker":"CARDINAL","id":"2henryvi:1.1:28"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Why should he, then, protect our sovereign,\nHe being of age to govern of himself?\nCousin of Somerset, join you with me,\nAnd all together, with the Duke of Suffolk,\nWe'll quickly hoise Duke Humphrey from his seat.","sourceLine":"1.1.166","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"2henryvi:1.1:29"},{"kind":"speech","text":"This weighty business will not brook delay:\nI'll to the Duke of Suffolk presently.","sourceLine":"1.1.171","speaker":"CARDINAL","id":"2henryvi:1.1:30"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit","id":"2henryvi:1.1:31"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Cousin of Buckingham, though Humphrey's pride\nAnd greatness of his place be grief to us,\nYet let us watch the haughty cardinal:\nHis insolence is more intolerable\nThan all the princes in the land beside:\nIf Gloucester be displaced, he'll be protector.","sourceLine":"1.1.173","speaker":"SOMERSET","id":"2henryvi:1.1:32"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Or thou or I, Somerset, will be protector,\nDespite Duke Humphrey or the cardinal.","sourceLine":"1.1.179","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"2henryvi:1.1:33"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt BUCKINGHAM and SOMERSET","id":"2henryvi:1.1:34"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Pride went before, ambition follows him.\nWhile these do labour for their own preferment,\nBehoves it us to labour for the realm.\nI never saw but Humphrey Duke of Gloucester\nDid bear him like a noble gentleman.\nOft have I seen the haughty cardinal,\nMore like a soldier than a man o' the church,\nAs stout and proud as he were lord of all,\nSwear like a ruffian and demean himself\nUnlike the ruler of a commonweal.\nWarwick, my son, the comfort of my age,\nThy deeds, thy plainness and thy housekeeping,\nHath won the greatest favour of the commons,\nExcepting none but good Duke Humphrey:\nAnd, brother York, thy acts in Ireland,\nIn bringing them to civil discipline,\nThy late exploits done in the heart of France,\nWhen thou wert regent for our sovereign,\nHave made thee fear'd and honour'd of the people:\nJoin we together, for the public good,\nIn what we can, to bridle and suppress\nThe pride of Suffolk and the cardinal,\nWith Somerset's and Buckingham's ambition;\nAnd, as we may, cherish Duke Humphrey's deeds,\nWhile they do tend the profit of the land.","sourceLine":"1.1.181","speaker":"SALISBURY","id":"2henryvi:1.1:35"},{"kind":"speech","text":"So God help Warwick, as he loves the land,\nAnd common profit of his country!","sourceLine":"1.1.206","speaker":"WARWICK","id":"2henryvi:1.1:36"},{"kind":"speech","text":"[Aside] And so says York, for he hath greatest cause.","sourceLine":"1.1.208","speaker":"YORK","id":"2henryvi:1.1:37"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Then let's make haste away, and look unto the main.","sourceLine":"1.1.209","speaker":"SALISBURY","id":"2henryvi:1.1:38"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Unto the main! O father, Maine is lost;\nThat Maine which by main force Warwick did win,\nAnd would have kept so long as breath did last!\nMain chance, father, you meant; but I meant Maine,\nWhich I will win from France, or else be slain,","sourceLine":"1.1.210","speaker":"WARWICK","id":"2henryvi:1.1:39"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt WARWICK and SALISBURY","id":"2henryvi:1.1:40"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Anjou and Maine are given to the French;\nParis is lost; the state of Normandy\nStands on a tickle point, now they are gone:\nSuffolk concluded on the articles,\nThe peers agreed, and Henry was well pleased\nTo change two dukedoms for a duke's fair daughter.\nI cannot blame them all: what is't to them?\n'Tis thine they give away, and not their own.\nPirates may make cheap pennyworths of their pillage\nAnd purchase friends and give to courtezans,\nStill revelling like lords till all be gone;\nWhile as the silly owner of the goods\nWeeps over them and wrings his hapless hands\nAnd shakes his head and trembling stands aloof,\nWhile all is shared and all is borne away,\nReady to starve and dare not touch his own:\nSo York must sit and fret and bite his tongue,\nWhile his own lands are bargain'd for and sold.\nMethinks the realms of England, France and Ireland\nBear that proportion to my flesh and blood\nAs did the fatal brand Althaea burn'd\nUnto the prince's heart of Calydon.\nAnjou and Maine both given unto the French!\nCold news for me, for I had hope of France,\nEven as I have of fertile England's soil.\nA day will come when York shall claim his own;\nAnd therefore I will take the Nevils' parts\nAnd make a show of love to proud Duke Humphrey,\nAnd, when I spy advantage, claim the crown,\nFor that's the golden mark I seek to hit:\nNor shall proud Lancaster usurp my right,\nNor hold the sceptre in his childish fist,\nNor wear the diadem upon his head,\nWhose church-like humours fits not for a crown.\nThen, York, be still awhile, till time do serve:\nWatch thou and wake when others be asleep,\nTo pry into the secrets of the state;\nTill Henry, surfeiting in joys of love,\nWith his new bride and England's dear-bought queen,\nAnd Humphrey with the peers be fall'n at jars:\nThen will I raise aloft the milk-white rose,\nWith whose sweet smell the air shall be perfumed;\nAnd in my standard bear the arms of York\nTo grapple with the house of Lancaster;\nAnd, force perforce, I'll make him yield the crown,\nWhose bookish rule hath pull'd fair England down.","sourceLine":"1.1.215","speaker":"YORK","id":"2henryvi:1.1:41"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit","id":"2henryvi:1.1:42"}],"scene":1},{"id":"1.2","label":"Act 1 · SCENE II. GLOUCESTER'S house.","act":1,"sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/2henryvi/full.html","blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter GLOUCESTER and his DUCHESS","id":"2henryvi:1.2:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Why droops my lord, like over-ripen'd corn,\nHanging the head at Ceres' plenteous load?\nWhy doth the great Duke Humphrey knit his brows,\nAs frowning at the favours of the world?\nWhy are thine eyes fixed to the sullen earth,\nGazing on that which seems to dim thy sight?\nWhat seest thou there? King Henry's diadem,\nEnchased with all the honours of the world?\nIf so, gaze on, and grovel on thy face,\nUntil thy head be circled with the same.\nPut forth thy hand, reach at the glorious gold.\nWhat, is't too short? I'll lengthen it with mine:\nAnd, having both together heaved it up,\nWe'll both together lift our heads to heaven,\nAnd never more abase our sight so low\nAs to vouchsafe one glance unto the ground.","sourceLine":"1.2.1","speaker":"DUCHESS","id":"2henryvi:1.2:2"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O Nell, sweet Nell, if thou dost love thy lord,\nBanish the canker of ambitious thoughts.\nAnd may that thought, when I imagine ill\nAgainst my king and nephew, virtuous Henry,\nBe my last breathing in this mortal world!\nMy troublous dream this night doth make me sad.","sourceLine":"1.2.17","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"2henryvi:1.2:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What dream'd my lord? tell me, and I'll requite it\nWith sweet rehearsal of my morning's dream.","sourceLine":"1.2.23","speaker":"DUCHESS","id":"2henryvi:1.2:4"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Methought this staff, mine office-badge in court,\nWas broke in twain; by whom I have forgot,\nBut, as I think, it was by the cardinal;\nAnd on the pieces of the broken wand\nWere placed the heads of Edmund Duke of Somerset,\nAnd William de la Pole, first duke of Suffolk.\nThis was my dream: what it doth bode, God knows.","sourceLine":"1.2.25","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"2henryvi:1.2:5"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Tut, this was nothing but an argument\nThat he that breaks a stick of Gloucester's grove\nShall lose his head for his presumption.\nBut list to me, my Humphrey, my sweet duke:\nMethought I sat in seat of majesty\nIn the cathedral church of Westminster,\nAnd in that chair where kings and queens are crown'd;\nWhere Henry and dame Margaret kneel'd to me\nAnd on my head did set the diadem.","sourceLine":"1.2.32","speaker":"DUCHESS","id":"2henryvi:1.2:6"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Nay, Eleanor, then must I chide outright:\nPresumptuous dame, ill-nurtured Eleanor,\nArt thou not second woman in the realm,\nAnd the protector's wife, beloved of him?\nHast thou not worldly pleasure at command,\nAbove the reach or compass of thy thought?\nAnd wilt thou still be hammering treachery,\nTo tumble down thy husband and thyself\nFrom top of honour to disgrace's feet?\nAway from me, and let me hear no more!","sourceLine":"1.2.41","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"2henryvi:1.2:7"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What, what, my lord! are you so choleric\nWith Eleanor, for telling but her dream?\nNext time I'll keep my dreams unto myself,\nAnd not be cheque'd.","sourceLine":"1.2.51","speaker":"DUCHESS","id":"2henryvi:1.2:8"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Nay, be not angry; I am pleased again.","sourceLine":"1.2.55","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"2henryvi:1.2:9"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter Messenger","id":"2henryvi:1.2:10"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My lord protector, 'tis his highness' pleasure\nYou do prepare to ride unto Saint Alban's,\nWhere as the king and queen do mean to hawk.","sourceLine":"1.2.56","speaker":"Messenger","id":"2henryvi:1.2:11"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I go. Come, Nell, thou wilt ride with us?","sourceLine":"1.2.59","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"2henryvi:1.2:12"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Yes, my good lord, I'll follow presently.","sourceLine":"1.2.60","speaker":"DUCHESS","id":"2henryvi:1.2:13"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt GLOUCESTER and Messenger","id":"2henryvi:1.2:14"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Follow I must; I cannot go before,\nWhile Gloucester bears this base and humble mind.\nWere I a man, a duke, and next of blood,\nI would remove these tedious stumbling-blocks\nAnd smooth my way upon their headless necks;\nAnd, being a woman, I will not be slack\nTo play my part in Fortune's pageant.\nWhere are you there? Sir John! nay, fear not, man,\nWe are alone; here's none but thee and I.","sourceLine":"1.2.61","speaker":"DUCHESS","id":"2henryvi:1.2:15"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter HUME","id":"2henryvi:1.2:16"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Jesus preserve your royal majesty!","sourceLine":"1.2.70","speaker":"HUME","id":"2henryvi:1.2:17"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What say'st thou? majesty! I am but grace.","sourceLine":"1.2.71","speaker":"DUCHESS","id":"2henryvi:1.2:18"},{"kind":"speech","text":"But, by the grace of God, and Hume's advice,\nYour grace's title shall be multiplied.","sourceLine":"1.2.72","speaker":"HUME","id":"2henryvi:1.2:19"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What say'st thou, man? hast thou as yet conferr'd\nWith Margery Jourdain, the cunning witch,\nWith Roger Bolingbroke, the conjurer?\nAnd will they undertake to do me good?","sourceLine":"1.2.74","speaker":"DUCHESS","id":"2henryvi:1.2:20"},{"kind":"speech","text":"This they have promised, to show your highness\nA spirit raised from depth of under-ground,\nThat shall make answer to such questions\nAs by your grace shall be propounded him.","sourceLine":"1.2.78","speaker":"HUME","id":"2henryvi:1.2:21"},{"kind":"speech","text":"It is enough; I'll think upon the questions:\nWhen from St. Alban's we do make return,\nWe'll see these things effected to the full.\nHere, Hume, take this reward; make merry, man,\nWith thy confederates in this weighty cause.","sourceLine":"1.2.82","speaker":"DUCHESS","id":"2henryvi:1.2:22"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit","id":"2henryvi:1.2:23"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Hume must make merry with the duchess' gold;\nMarry, and shall. But how now, Sir John Hume!\nSeal up your lips, and give no words but mum:\nThe business asketh silent secrecy.\nDame Eleanor gives gold to bring the witch:\nGold cannot come amiss, were she a devil.\nYet have I gold flies from another coast;\nI dare not say, from the rich cardinal\nAnd from the great and new-made Duke of Suffolk,\nYet I do find it so; for to be plain,\nThey, knowing Dame Eleanor's aspiring humour,\nHave hired me to undermine the duchess\nAnd buz these conjurations in her brain.\nThey say 'A crafty knave does need no broker;'\nYet am I Suffolk and the cardinal's broker.\nHume, if you take not heed, you shall go near\nTo call them both a pair of crafty knaves.\nWell, so it stands; and thus, I fear, at last\nHume's knavery will be the duchess' wreck,\nAnd her attainture will be Humphrey's fall:\nSort how it will, I shall have gold for all.","sourceLine":"1.2.87","speaker":"HUME","id":"2henryvi:1.2:24"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit","id":"2henryvi:1.2:25"}],"scene":2},{"id":"1.3","label":"Act 1 · SCENE III. The palace.","act":1,"sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/2henryvi/full.html","blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter three or four Petitioners, PETER, the Armourer's man, being one","id":"2henryvi:1.3:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My masters, let's stand close: my lord protector\nwill come this way by and by, and then we may deliver\nour supplications in the quill.","sourceLine":"1.3.1","speaker":"First Petitioner","id":"2henryvi:1.3:2"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Marry, the Lord protect him, for he's a good man!\nJesu bless him!","sourceLine":"1.3.4","speaker":"Second Petitioner","id":"2henryvi:1.3:3"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter SUFFOLK and QUEEN MARGARET","id":"2henryvi:1.3:4"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Here a' comes, methinks, and the queen with him.\nI'll be the first, sure.","sourceLine":"1.3.6","speaker":"PETER","id":"2henryvi:1.3:5"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Come back, fool; this is the Duke of Suffolk, and\nnot my lord protector.","sourceLine":"1.3.8","speaker":"Second Petitioner","id":"2henryvi:1.3:6"},{"kind":"speech","text":"How now, fellow! would'st anything with me?","sourceLine":"1.3.10","speaker":"SUFFOLK","id":"2henryvi:1.3:7"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I pray, my lord, pardon me; I took ye for my lord\nprotector.","sourceLine":"1.3.11","speaker":"First Petitioner","id":"2henryvi:1.3:8"},{"kind":"speech","text":"[Reading] 'To my Lord Protector!' Are your\nsupplications to his lordship? Let me see them:\nwhat is thine?","sourceLine":"1.3.13","speaker":"QUEEN MARGARET","id":"2henryvi:1.3:9"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Mine is, an't please your grace, against John\nGoodman, my lord cardinal's man, for keeping my\nhouse, and lands, and wife and all, from me.","sourceLine":"1.3.16","speaker":"First Petitioner","id":"2henryvi:1.3:10"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Thy wife, too! that's some wrong, indeed. What's\nyours? What's here!","sourceLine":"1.3.19","speaker":"SUFFOLK","id":"2henryvi:1.3:11"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Reads","id":"2henryvi:1.3:12"},{"kind":"speech","text":"'Against the Duke of Suffolk, for enclosing the\ncommons of Melford.' How now, sir knave!","sourceLine":"1.3.21","speaker":"SUFFOLK","id":"2henryvi:1.3:13"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Alas, sir, I am but a poor petitioner of our whole township.","sourceLine":"1.3.23","speaker":"Second Petitioner","id":"2henryvi:1.3:14"},{"kind":"speech","text":"[Giving his petition] Against my master, Thomas\nHorner, for saying that the Duke of York was rightful\nheir to the crown.","sourceLine":"1.3.24","speaker":"PETER","id":"2henryvi:1.3:15"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What sayst thou? did the Duke of York say he was\nrightful heir to the crown?","sourceLine":"1.3.27","speaker":"QUEEN MARGARET","id":"2henryvi:1.3:16"},{"kind":"speech","text":"That my master was? no, forsooth: my master said\nthat he was, and that the king was an usurper.","sourceLine":"1.3.29","speaker":"PETER","id":"2henryvi:1.3:17"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Who is there?","sourceLine":"1.3.31","speaker":"SUFFOLK","id":"2henryvi:1.3:18"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter Servant","id":"2henryvi:1.3:19"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Take this fellow in, and send for\nhis master with a pursuivant presently: we'll hear\nmore of your matter before the King.","sourceLine":"1.3.32","speaker":"SUFFOLK","id":"2henryvi:1.3:20"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit Servant with PETER","id":"2henryvi:1.3:21"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And as for you, that love to be protected\nUnder the wings of our protector's grace,\nBegin your suits anew, and sue to him.","sourceLine":"1.3.35","speaker":"QUEEN MARGARET","id":"2henryvi:1.3:22"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Tears the supplication","id":"2henryvi:1.3:23"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Away, base cullions! Suffolk, let them go.","sourceLine":"1.3.38","speaker":"QUEEN MARGARET","id":"2henryvi:1.3:24"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Come, let's be gone.","sourceLine":"1.3.39","speaker":"ALL","id":"2henryvi:1.3:25"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt","id":"2henryvi:1.3:26"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My Lord of Suffolk, say, is this the guise,\nIs this the fashion in the court of England?\nIs this the government of Britain's isle,\nAnd this the royalty of Albion's king?\nWhat shall King Henry be a pupil still\nUnder the surly Gloucester's governance?\nAm I a queen in title and in style,\nAnd must be made a subject to a duke?\nI tell thee, Pole, when in the city Tours\nThou ran'st a tilt in honour of my love\nAnd stolest away the ladies' hearts of France,\nI thought King Henry had resembled thee\nIn courage, courtship and proportion:\nBut all his mind is bent to holiness,\nTo number Ave-Maries on his beads;\nHis champions are the prophets and apostles,\nHis weapons holy saws of sacred writ,\nHis study is his tilt-yard, and his loves\nAre brazen images of canonized saints.\nI would the college of the cardinals\nWould choose him pope, and carry him to Rome,\nAnd set the triple crown upon his head:\nThat were a state fit for his holiness.","sourceLine":"1.3.40","speaker":"QUEEN MARGARET","id":"2henryvi:1.3:27"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Madam, be patient: as I was cause\nYour highness came to England, so will I\nIn England work your grace's full content.","sourceLine":"1.3.63","speaker":"SUFFOLK","id":"2henryvi:1.3:28"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Beside the haughty protector, have we Beaufort,\nThe imperious churchman, Somerset, Buckingham,\nAnd grumbling York: and not the least of these\nBut can do more in England than the king.","sourceLine":"1.3.66","speaker":"QUEEN MARGARET","id":"2henryvi:1.3:29"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And he of these that can do most of all\nCannot do more in England than the Nevils:\nSalisbury and Warwick are no simple peers.","sourceLine":"1.3.70","speaker":"SUFFOLK","id":"2henryvi:1.3:30"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Not all these lords do vex me half so much\nAs that proud dame, the lord protector's wife.\nShe sweeps it through the court with troops of ladies,\nMore like an empress than Duke Humphrey's wife:\nStrangers in court do take her for the queen:\nShe bears a duke's revenues on her back,\nAnd in her heart she scorns our poverty:\nShall I not live to be avenged on her?\nContemptuous base-born callet as she is,\nShe vaunted 'mongst her minions t'other day,\nThe very train of her worst wearing gown\nWas better worth than all my father's lands,\nTill Suffolk gave two dukedoms for his daughter.","sourceLine":"1.3.73","speaker":"QUEEN MARGARET","id":"2henryvi:1.3:31"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Madam, myself have limed a bush for her,\nAnd placed a quire of such enticing birds,\nThat she will light to listen to the lays,\nAnd never mount to trouble you again.\nSo, let her rest: and, madam, list to me;\nFor I am bold to counsel you in this.\nAlthough we fancy not the cardinal,\nYet must we join with him and with the lords,\nTill we have brought Duke Humphrey in disgrace.\nAs for the Duke of York, this late complaint\nWill make but little for his benefit.\nSo, one by one, we'll weed them all at last,\nAnd you yourself shall steer the happy helm.","sourceLine":"1.3.86","speaker":"SUFFOLK","id":"2henryvi:1.3:32"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Sound a sennet. Enter KING HENRY VI, GLOUCESTER, CARDINAL, BUCKINGHAM, YORK, SOMERSET, SALISBURY, WARWICK, and the DUCHESS","id":"2henryvi:1.3:33"},{"kind":"speech","text":"For my part, noble lords, I care not which;\nOr Somerset or York, all's one to me.","sourceLine":"1.3.99","speaker":"KING HENRY VI","id":"2henryvi:1.3:34"},{"kind":"speech","text":"If York have ill demean'd himself in France,\nThen let him be denay'd the regentship.","sourceLine":"1.3.101","speaker":"YORK","id":"2henryvi:1.3:35"},{"kind":"speech","text":"If Somerset be unworthy of the place,\nLet York be regent; I will yield to him.","sourceLine":"1.3.103","speaker":"SOMERSET","id":"2henryvi:1.3:36"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Whether your grace be worthy, yea or no,\nDispute not that: York is the worthier.","sourceLine":"1.3.105","speaker":"WARWICK","id":"2henryvi:1.3:37"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ambitious Warwick, let thy betters speak.","sourceLine":"1.3.107","speaker":"CARDINAL","id":"2henryvi:1.3:38"},{"kind":"speech","text":"The cardinal's not my better in the field.","sourceLine":"1.3.108","speaker":"WARWICK","id":"2henryvi:1.3:39"},{"kind":"speech","text":"All in this presence are thy betters, Warwick.","sourceLine":"1.3.109","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"2henryvi:1.3:40"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Warwick may live to be the best of all.","sourceLine":"1.3.110","speaker":"WARWICK","id":"2henryvi:1.3:41"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Peace, son! and show some reason, Buckingham,\nWhy Somerset should be preferred in this.","sourceLine":"1.3.111","speaker":"SALISBURY","id":"2henryvi:1.3:42"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Because the king, forsooth, will have it so.","sourceLine":"1.3.113","speaker":"QUEEN MARGARET","id":"2henryvi:1.3:43"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Madam, the king is old enough himself\nTo give his censure: these are no women's matters.","sourceLine":"1.3.114","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"2henryvi:1.3:44"},{"kind":"speech","text":"If he be old enough, what needs your grace\nTo be protector of his excellence?","sourceLine":"1.3.116","speaker":"QUEEN MARGARET","id":"2henryvi:1.3:45"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Madam, I am protector of the realm;\nAnd, at his pleasure, will resign my place.","sourceLine":"1.3.118","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"2henryvi:1.3:46"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Resign it then and leave thine insolence.\nSince thou wert king--as who is king but thou?--\nThe commonwealth hath daily run to wreck;\nThe Dauphin hath prevail'd beyond the seas;\nAnd all the peers and nobles of the realm\nHave been as bondmen to thy sovereignty.","sourceLine":"1.3.120","speaker":"SUFFOLK","id":"2henryvi:1.3:47"},{"kind":"speech","text":"The commons hast thou rack'd; the clergy's bags\nAre lank and lean with thy extortions.","sourceLine":"1.3.126","speaker":"CARDINAL","id":"2henryvi:1.3:48"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Thy sumptuous buildings and thy wife's attire\nHave cost a mass of public treasury.","sourceLine":"1.3.128","speaker":"SOMERSET","id":"2henryvi:1.3:49"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Thy cruelty in execution\nUpon offenders, hath exceeded law,\nAnd left thee to the mercy of the law.","sourceLine":"1.3.130","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"2henryvi:1.3:50"},{"kind":"speech","text":"They sale of offices and towns in France,\nIf they were known, as the suspect is great,\nWould make thee quickly hop without thy head.","sourceLine":"1.3.133","speaker":"QUEEN MARGARET","id":"2henryvi:1.3:51"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit GLOUCESTER. QUEEN MARGARET drops her fan","id":"2henryvi:1.3:52"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Give me my fan: what, minion! can ye not?","sourceLine":"1.3.136","speaker":"QUEEN MARGARET","id":"2henryvi:1.3:53"},{"kind":"stage","text":"She gives the DUCHESS a box on the ear","id":"2henryvi:1.3:54"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I cry you mercy, madam; was it you?","sourceLine":"1.3.137","speaker":"QUEEN MARGARET","id":"2henryvi:1.3:55"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Was't I! yea, I it was, proud Frenchwoman:\nCould I come near your beauty with my nails,\nI'd set my ten commandments in your face.","sourceLine":"1.3.138","speaker":"DUCHESS","id":"2henryvi:1.3:56"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Sweet aunt, be quiet; 'twas against her will.","sourceLine":"1.3.141","speaker":"KING HENRY VI","id":"2henryvi:1.3:57"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Against her will! good king, look to't in time;\nShe'll hamper thee, and dandle thee like a baby:\nThough in this place most master wear no breeches,\nShe shall not strike Dame Eleanor unrevenged.","sourceLine":"1.3.142","speaker":"DUCHESS","id":"2henryvi:1.3:58"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit","id":"2henryvi:1.3:59"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Lord cardinal, I will follow Eleanor,\nAnd listen after Humphrey, how he proceeds:\nShe's tickled now; her fume needs no spurs,\nShe'll gallop far enough to her destruction.","sourceLine":"1.3.146","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"2henryvi:1.3:60"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit","id":"2henryvi:1.3:61"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Re-enter GLOUCESTER","id":"2henryvi:1.3:62"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Now, lords, my choler being over-blown\nWith walking once about the quadrangle,\nI come to talk of commonwealth affairs.\nAs for your spiteful false objections,\nProve them, and I lie open to the law:\nBut God in mercy so deal with my soul,\nAs I in duty love my king and country!\nBut, to the matter that we have in hand:\nI say, my sovereign, York is meetest man\nTo be your regent in the realm of France.","sourceLine":"1.3.150","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"2henryvi:1.3:63"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Before we make election, give me leave\nTo show some reason, of no little force,\nThat York is most unmeet of any man.","sourceLine":"1.3.160","speaker":"SUFFOLK","id":"2henryvi:1.3:64"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I'll tell thee, Suffolk, why I am unmeet:\nFirst, for I cannot flatter thee in pride;\nNext, if I be appointed for the place,\nMy Lord of Somerset will keep me here,\nWithout discharge, money, or furniture,\nTill France be won into the Dauphin's hands:\nLast time, I danced attendance on his will\nTill Paris was besieged, famish'd, and lost.","sourceLine":"1.3.163","speaker":"YORK","id":"2henryvi:1.3:65"},{"kind":"speech","text":"That can I witness; and a fouler fact\nDid never traitor in the land commit.","sourceLine":"1.3.171","speaker":"WARWICK","id":"2henryvi:1.3:66"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Peace, headstrong Warwick!","sourceLine":"1.3.173","speaker":"SUFFOLK","id":"2henryvi:1.3:67"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Image of pride, why should I hold my peace?","sourceLine":"1.3.174","speaker":"WARWICK","id":"2henryvi:1.3:68"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter HORNER, the Armourer, and his man PETER, guarded","id":"2henryvi:1.3:69"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Because here is a man accused of treason:\nPray God the Duke of York excuse himself!","sourceLine":"1.3.175","speaker":"SUFFOLK","id":"2henryvi:1.3:70"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Doth any one accuse York for a traitor?","sourceLine":"1.3.177","speaker":"YORK","id":"2henryvi:1.3:71"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What mean'st thou, Suffolk; tell me, what are these?","sourceLine":"1.3.178","speaker":"KING HENRY VI","id":"2henryvi:1.3:72"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Please it your majesty, this is the man\nThat doth accuse his master of high treason:\nHis words were these: that Richard, Duke of York,\nWas rightful heir unto the English crown\nAnd that your majesty was a usurper.","sourceLine":"1.3.179","speaker":"SUFFOLK","id":"2henryvi:1.3:73"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Say, man, were these thy words?","sourceLine":"1.3.184","speaker":"KING HENRY VI","id":"2henryvi:1.3:74"},{"kind":"speech","text":"An't shall please your majesty, I never said nor\nthought any such matter: God is my witness, I am\nfalsely accused by the villain.","sourceLine":"1.3.185","speaker":"HORNER","id":"2henryvi:1.3:75"},{"kind":"speech","text":"By these ten bones, my lords, he did speak them to\nme in the garret one night, as we were scouring my\nLord of York's armour.","sourceLine":"1.3.188","speaker":"PETER","id":"2henryvi:1.3:76"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Base dunghill villain and mechanical,\nI'll have thy head for this thy traitor's speech.\nI do beseech your royal majesty,\nLet him have all the rigor of the law.","sourceLine":"1.3.191","speaker":"YORK","id":"2henryvi:1.3:77"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Alas, my lord, hang me, if ever I spake the words.\nMy accuser is my 'prentice; and when I did correct\nhim for his fault the other day, he did vow upon his\nknees he would be even with me: I have good\nwitness of this: therefore I beseech your majesty,\ndo not cast away an honest man for a villain's\naccusation.","sourceLine":"1.3.195","speaker":"HORNER","id":"2henryvi:1.3:78"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Uncle, what shall we say to this in law?","sourceLine":"1.3.202","speaker":"KING HENRY VI","id":"2henryvi:1.3:79"},{"kind":"speech","text":"This doom, my lord, if I may judge:\nLet Somerset be regent over the French,\nBecause in York this breeds suspicion:\nAnd let these have a day appointed them\nFor single combat in convenient place,\nFor he hath witness of his servant's malice:\nThis is the law, and this Duke Humphrey's doom.","sourceLine":"1.3.203","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"2henryvi:1.3:80"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I humbly thank your royal majesty.","sourceLine":"1.3.210","speaker":"SOMERSET","id":"2henryvi:1.3:81"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And I accept the combat willingly.","sourceLine":"1.3.211","speaker":"HORNER","id":"2henryvi:1.3:82"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Alas, my lord, I cannot fight; for God's sake, pity\nmy case. The spite of man prevaileth against me. O\nLord, have mercy upon me! I shall never be able to\nfight a blow. O Lord, my heart!","sourceLine":"1.3.212","speaker":"PETER","id":"2henryvi:1.3:83"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Sirrah, or you must fight, or else be hang'd.","sourceLine":"1.3.216","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"2henryvi:1.3:84"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Away with them to prison; and the day of combat\nshall be the last of the next month. Come,\nSomerset, we'll see thee sent away.","sourceLine":"1.3.217","speaker":"KING HENRY VI","id":"2henryvi:1.3:85"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Flourish. Exeunt","id":"2henryvi:1.3:86"}],"scene":3},{"id":"1.4","label":"Act 1 · SCENE IV. GLOUCESTER's garden.","act":1,"sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/2henryvi/full.html","blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter MARGARET JOURDAIN, HUME, SOUTHWELL, and BOLINGBROKE","id":"2henryvi:1.4:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Come, my masters; the duchess, I tell you, expects\nperformance of your promises.","sourceLine":"1.4.1","speaker":"HUME","id":"2henryvi:1.4:2"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Master Hume, we are therefore provided: will her\nladyship behold and hear our exorcisms?","sourceLine":"1.4.3","speaker":"BOLINGBROKE","id":"2henryvi:1.4:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ay, what else? fear you not her courage.","sourceLine":"1.4.5","speaker":"HUME","id":"2henryvi:1.4:4"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I have heard her reported to be a woman of an\ninvincible spirit: but it shall be convenient,\nMaster Hume, that you be by her aloft, while we be\nbusy below; and so, I pray you, go, in God's name,\nand leave us.","sourceLine":"1.4.6","speaker":"BOLINGBROKE","id":"2henryvi:1.4:5"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit HUME","id":"2henryvi:1.4:6"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Mother Jourdain, be you\nprostrate and grovel on the earth; John Southwell,\nread you; and let us to our work.","sourceLine":"1.4.11","speaker":"BOLINGBROKE","id":"2henryvi:1.4:7"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter the DUCHESS aloft, HUME following","id":"2henryvi:1.4:8"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Well said, my masters; and welcome all. To this\ngear the sooner the better.","sourceLine":"1.4.14","speaker":"DUCHESS","id":"2henryvi:1.4:9"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Patience, good lady; wizards know their times:\nDeep night, dark night, the silent of the night,\nThe time of night when Troy was set on fire;\nThe time when screech-owls cry and ban-dogs howl,\nAnd spirits walk and ghosts break up their graves,\nThat time best fits the work we have in hand.\nMadam, sit you and fear not: whom we raise,\nWe will make fast within a hallow'd verge.","sourceLine":"1.4.16","speaker":"BOLINGBROKE","id":"2henryvi:1.4:10"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Here they do the ceremonies belonging, and make the circle; BOLINGBROKE or SOUTHWELL reads, Conjuro te, & c. It thunders and lightens terribly; then the Spirit riseth","id":"2henryvi:1.4:11"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Adsum.","sourceLine":"1.4.24","speaker":"Spirit","id":"2henryvi:1.4:12"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Asmath,\nBy the eternal God, whose name and power\nThou tremblest at, answer that I shall ask;\nFor, till thou speak, thou shalt not pass from hence.","sourceLine":"1.4.25","speaker":"MARGARET JOURDAIN","id":"2henryvi:1.4:13"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ask what thou wilt. That I had said and done!","sourceLine":"1.4.29","speaker":"Spirit","id":"2henryvi:1.4:14"},{"kind":"speech","text":"'First of the king: what shall of him become?'","sourceLine":"1.4.30","speaker":"BOLINGBROKE","id":"2henryvi:1.4:15"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Reading out of a paper","id":"2henryvi:1.4:16"},{"kind":"speech","text":"The duke yet lives that Henry shall depose;\nBut him outlive, and die a violent death.","sourceLine":"1.4.31","speaker":"Spirit","id":"2henryvi:1.4:17"},{"kind":"stage","text":"As the Spirit speaks, SOUTHWELL writes the answer","id":"2henryvi:1.4:18"},{"kind":"speech","text":"'What fates await the Duke of Suffolk?'","sourceLine":"1.4.33","speaker":"BOLINGBROKE","id":"2henryvi:1.4:19"},{"kind":"speech","text":"By water shall he die, and take his end.","sourceLine":"1.4.34","speaker":"Spirit","id":"2henryvi:1.4:20"},{"kind":"speech","text":"'What shall befall the Duke of Somerset?'","sourceLine":"1.4.35","speaker":"BOLINGBROKE","id":"2henryvi:1.4:21"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Let him shun castles;\nSafer shall he be upon the sandy plains\nThan where castles mounted stand.\nHave done, for more I hardly can endure.","sourceLine":"1.4.36","speaker":"Spirit","id":"2henryvi:1.4:22"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Descend to darkness and the burning lake!\nFalse fiend, avoid!","sourceLine":"1.4.40","speaker":"BOLINGBROKE","id":"2henryvi:1.4:23"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Thunder and lightning. Exit Spirit","id":"2henryvi:1.4:24"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter YORK and BUCKINGHAM with their Guard and break in","id":"2henryvi:1.4:25"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Lay hands upon these traitors and their trash.\nBeldam, I think we watch'd you at an inch.\nWhat, madam, are you there? the king and commonweal\nAre deeply indebted for this piece of pains:\nMy lord protector will, I doubt it not,\nSee you well guerdon'd for these good deserts.","sourceLine":"1.4.42","speaker":"YORK","id":"2henryvi:1.4:26"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Not half so bad as thine to England's king,\nInjurious duke, that threatest where's no cause.","sourceLine":"1.4.48","speaker":"DUCHESS","id":"2henryvi:1.4:27"},{"kind":"speech","text":"True, madam, none at all: what call you this?\nAway with them! let them be clapp'd up close.\nAnd kept asunder. You, madam, shall with us.\nStafford, take her to thee.","sourceLine":"1.4.50","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"2henryvi:1.4:28"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt above DUCHESS and HUME, guarded","id":"2henryvi:1.4:29"},{"kind":"speech","text":"We'll see your trinkets here all forthcoming.\nAll, away!","sourceLine":"1.4.54","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"2henryvi:1.4:30"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt guard with MARGARET JOURDAIN, SOUTHWELL, & c","id":"2henryvi:1.4:31"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Lord Buckingham, methinks, you watch'd her well:\nA pretty plot, well chosen to build upon!\nNow, pray, my lord, let's see the devil's writ.\nWhat have we here?","sourceLine":"1.4.56","speaker":"YORK","id":"2henryvi:1.4:32"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Reads","id":"2henryvi:1.4:33"},{"kind":"speech","text":"'The duke yet lives, that Henry shall depose;\nBut him outlive, and die a violent death.'\nWhy, this is just\n'Aio te, AEacida, Romanos vincere posse.'\nWell, to the rest:\n'Tell me what fate awaits the Duke of Suffolk?\nBy water shall he die, and take his end.\nWhat shall betide the Duke of Somerset?\nLet him shun castles;\nSafer shall he be upon the sandy plains\nThan where castles mounted stand.'\nCome, come, my lords;\nThese oracles are hardly attain'd,\nAnd hardly understood.\nThe king is now in progress towards Saint Alban's,\nWith him the husband of this lovely lady:\nThither go these news, as fast as horse can\ncarry them:\nA sorry breakfast for my lord protector.","sourceLine":"1.4.60","speaker":"YORK","id":"2henryvi:1.4:34"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Your grace shall give me leave, my Lord of York,\nTo be the post, in hope of his reward.","sourceLine":"1.4.79","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"2henryvi:1.4:35"},{"kind":"speech","text":"At your pleasure, my good lord. Who's within\nthere, ho!","sourceLine":"1.4.81","speaker":"YORK","id":"2henryvi:1.4:36"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter a Servingman","id":"2henryvi:1.4:37"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Invite my Lords of Salisbury and Warwick\nTo sup with me to-morrow night. Away!","sourceLine":"1.4.83","speaker":"YORK","id":"2henryvi:1.4:38"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt","id":"2henryvi:1.4:39"}],"scene":4},{"id":"2.1","label":"Act 2 · SCENE I. Saint Alban's.","act":2,"sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/2henryvi/full.html","blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter KING HENRY VI, QUEEN MARGARET, GLOUCESTER, CARDINAL, and SUFFOLK, with Falconers halloing","id":"2henryvi:2.1:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Believe me, lords, for flying at the brook,\nI saw not better sport these seven years' day:\nYet, by your leave, the wind was very high;\nAnd, ten to one, old Joan had not gone out.","sourceLine":"2.1.1","speaker":"QUEEN MARGARET","id":"2henryvi:2.1:2"},{"kind":"speech","text":"But what a point, my lord, your falcon made,\nAnd what a pitch she flew above the rest!\nTo see how God in all his creatures works!\nYea, man and birds are fain of climbing high.","sourceLine":"2.1.5","speaker":"KING HENRY VI","id":"2henryvi:2.1:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"No marvel, an it like your majesty,\nMy lord protector's hawks do tower so well;\nThey know their master loves to be aloft,\nAnd bears his thoughts above his falcon's pitch.","sourceLine":"2.1.9","speaker":"SUFFOLK","id":"2henryvi:2.1:4"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My lord, 'tis but a base ignoble mind\nThat mounts no higher than a bird can soar.","sourceLine":"2.1.13","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"2henryvi:2.1:5"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I thought as much; he would be above the clouds.","sourceLine":"2.1.15","speaker":"CARDINAL","id":"2henryvi:2.1:6"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ay, my lord cardinal? how think you by that?\nWere it not good your grace could fly to heaven?","sourceLine":"2.1.16","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"2henryvi:2.1:7"},{"kind":"speech","text":"The treasury of everlasting joy.","sourceLine":"2.1.18","speaker":"KING HENRY VI","id":"2henryvi:2.1:8"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Thy heaven is on earth; thine eyes and thoughts\nBeat on a crown, the treasure of thy heart;\nPernicious protector, dangerous peer,\nThat smooth'st it so with king and commonweal!","sourceLine":"2.1.19","speaker":"CARDINAL","id":"2henryvi:2.1:9"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What, cardinal, is your priesthood grown peremptory?\nTantaene animis coelestibus irae?\nChurchmen so hot? good uncle, hide such malice;\nWith such holiness can you do it?","sourceLine":"2.1.23","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"2henryvi:2.1:10"},{"kind":"speech","text":"No malice, sir; no more than well becomes\nSo good a quarrel and so bad a peer.","sourceLine":"2.1.27","speaker":"SUFFOLK","id":"2henryvi:2.1:11"},{"kind":"speech","text":"As who, my lord?","sourceLine":"2.1.29","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"2henryvi:2.1:12"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Why, as you, my lord,\nAn't like your lordly lord-protectorship.","sourceLine":"2.1.30","speaker":"SUFFOLK","id":"2henryvi:2.1:13"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Why, Suffolk, England knows thine insolence.","sourceLine":"2.1.32","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"2henryvi:2.1:14"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And thy ambition, Gloucester.","sourceLine":"2.1.33","speaker":"QUEEN MARGARET","id":"2henryvi:2.1:15"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I prithee, peace, good queen,\nAnd whet not on these furious peers;\nFor blessed are the peacemakers on earth.","sourceLine":"2.1.34","speaker":"KING HENRY VI","id":"2henryvi:2.1:16"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Let me be blessed for the peace I make,\nAgainst this proud protector, with my sword!","sourceLine":"2.1.37","speaker":"CARDINAL","id":"2henryvi:2.1:17"},{"kind":"speech","text":"[Aside to CARDINAL] Faith, holy uncle, would\n'twere come to that!","sourceLine":"2.1.39","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"2henryvi:2.1:18"},{"kind":"speech","text":"[Aside to GLOUCESTER] Marry, when thou darest.","sourceLine":"2.1.41","speaker":"CARDINAL","id":"2henryvi:2.1:19"},{"kind":"speech","text":"[Aside to CARDINAL] Make up no factious\nnumbers for the matter;\nIn thine own person answer thy abuse.","sourceLine":"2.1.42","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"2henryvi:2.1:20"},{"kind":"speech","text":"[Aside to GLOUCESTER] Ay, where thou darest\nnot peep: an if thou darest,\nThis evening, on the east side of the grove.","sourceLine":"2.1.45","speaker":"CARDINAL","id":"2henryvi:2.1:21"},{"kind":"speech","text":"How now, my lords!","sourceLine":"2.1.48","speaker":"KING HENRY VI","id":"2henryvi:2.1:22"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Believe me, cousin Gloucester,\nHad not your man put up the fowl so suddenly,\nWe had had more sport.","sourceLine":"2.1.49","speaker":"CARDINAL","id":"2henryvi:2.1:23"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Aside to GLOUCESTER","id":"2henryvi:2.1:24"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Come with thy two-hand sword.","sourceLine":"2.1.52","speaker":"CARDINAL","id":"2henryvi:2.1:25"},{"kind":"speech","text":"True, uncle.","sourceLine":"2.1.53","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"2henryvi:2.1:26"},{"kind":"speech","text":"[Aside to GLOUCESTER] Are ye advised? the\neast side of the grove?","sourceLine":"2.1.54","speaker":"CARDINAL","id":"2henryvi:2.1:27"},{"kind":"speech","text":"[Aside to CARDINAL] Cardinal, I am with you.","sourceLine":"2.1.56","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"2henryvi:2.1:28"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Why, how now, uncle Gloucester!","sourceLine":"2.1.57","speaker":"KING HENRY VI","id":"2henryvi:2.1:29"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Talking of hawking; nothing else, my lord.","sourceLine":"2.1.58","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"2henryvi:2.1:30"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Aside to CARDINAL","id":"2henryvi:2.1:31"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Now, by God's mother, priest, I'll shave your crown for this,\nOr all my fence shall fail.","sourceLine":"2.1.59","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"2henryvi:2.1:32"},{"kind":"speech","text":"[Aside to GLOUCESTER] Medice, teipsum--\nProtector, see to't well, protect yourself.","sourceLine":"2.1.61","speaker":"CARDINAL","id":"2henryvi:2.1:33"},{"kind":"speech","text":"The winds grow high; so do your stomachs, lords.\nHow irksome is this music to my heart!\nWhen such strings jar, what hope of harmony?\nI pray, my lords, let me compound this strife.","sourceLine":"2.1.63","speaker":"KING HENRY VI","id":"2henryvi:2.1:34"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter a Townsman of Saint Alban's, crying 'A miracle!'","id":"2henryvi:2.1:35"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What means this noise?\nFellow, what miracle dost thou proclaim?","sourceLine":"2.1.67","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"2henryvi:2.1:36"},{"kind":"speech","text":"A miracle! a miracle!","sourceLine":"2.1.69","speaker":"Townsman","id":"2henryvi:2.1:37"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Come to the king and tell him what miracle.","sourceLine":"2.1.70","speaker":"SUFFOLK","id":"2henryvi:2.1:38"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Forsooth, a blind man at Saint Alban's shrine,\nWithin this half-hour, hath received his sight;\nA man that ne'er saw in his life before.","sourceLine":"2.1.71","speaker":"Townsman","id":"2henryvi:2.1:39"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Now, God be praised, that to believing souls\nGives light in darkness, comfort in despair!","sourceLine":"2.1.74","speaker":"KING HENRY VI","id":"2henryvi:2.1:40"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter the Mayor of Saint Alban's and his brethren, bearing SIMPCOX, between two in a chair, SIMPCOX's Wife following","id":"2henryvi:2.1:41"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Here comes the townsmen on procession,\nTo present your highness with the man.","sourceLine":"2.1.76","speaker":"CARDINAL","id":"2henryvi:2.1:42"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Great is his comfort in this earthly vale,\nAlthough by his sight his sin be multiplied.","sourceLine":"2.1.78","speaker":"KING HENRY VI","id":"2henryvi:2.1:43"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Stand by, my masters: bring him near the king;\nHis highness' pleasure is to talk with him.","sourceLine":"2.1.80","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"2henryvi:2.1:44"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Good fellow, tell us here the circumstance,\nThat we for thee may glorify the Lord.\nWhat, hast thou been long blind and now restored?","sourceLine":"2.1.82","speaker":"KING HENRY VI","id":"2henryvi:2.1:45"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Born blind, an't please your grace.","sourceLine":"2.1.85","speaker":"SIMPCOX","id":"2henryvi:2.1:46"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ay, indeed, was he.","sourceLine":"2.1.86","speaker":"Wife","id":"2henryvi:2.1:47"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What woman is this?","sourceLine":"2.1.87","speaker":"SUFFOLK","id":"2henryvi:2.1:48"},{"kind":"speech","text":"His wife, an't like your worship.","sourceLine":"2.1.88","speaker":"Wife","id":"2henryvi:2.1:49"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Hadst thou been his mother, thou couldst have\nbetter told.","sourceLine":"2.1.89","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"2henryvi:2.1:50"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Where wert thou born?","sourceLine":"2.1.91","speaker":"KING HENRY VI","id":"2henryvi:2.1:51"},{"kind":"speech","text":"At Berwick in the north, an't like your grace.","sourceLine":"2.1.92","speaker":"SIMPCOX","id":"2henryvi:2.1:52"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Poor soul, God's goodness hath been great to thee:\nLet never day nor night unhallow'd pass,\nBut still remember what the Lord hath done.","sourceLine":"2.1.93","speaker":"KING HENRY VI","id":"2henryvi:2.1:53"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Tell me, good fellow, camest thou here by chance,\nOr of devotion, to this holy shrine?","sourceLine":"2.1.96","speaker":"QUEEN MARGARET","id":"2henryvi:2.1:54"},{"kind":"speech","text":"God knows, of pure devotion; being call'd\nA hundred times and oftener, in my sleep,\nBy good Saint Alban; who said, 'Simpcox, come,\nCome, offer at my shrine, and I will help thee.'","sourceLine":"2.1.98","speaker":"SIMPCOX","id":"2henryvi:2.1:55"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Most true, forsooth; and many time and oft\nMyself have heard a voice to call him so.","sourceLine":"2.1.102","speaker":"Wife","id":"2henryvi:2.1:56"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What, art thou lame?","sourceLine":"2.1.104","speaker":"CARDINAL","id":"2henryvi:2.1:57"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ay, God Almighty help me!","sourceLine":"2.1.105","speaker":"SIMPCOX","id":"2henryvi:2.1:58"},{"kind":"speech","text":"How camest thou so?","sourceLine":"2.1.106","speaker":"SUFFOLK","id":"2henryvi:2.1:59"},{"kind":"speech","text":"A fall off of a tree.","sourceLine":"2.1.107","speaker":"SIMPCOX","id":"2henryvi:2.1:60"},{"kind":"speech","text":"A plum-tree, master.","sourceLine":"2.1.108","speaker":"Wife","id":"2henryvi:2.1:61"},{"kind":"speech","text":"How long hast thou been blind?","sourceLine":"2.1.109","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"2henryvi:2.1:62"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Born so, master.","sourceLine":"2.1.110","speaker":"SIMPCOX","id":"2henryvi:2.1:63"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What, and wouldst climb a tree?","sourceLine":"2.1.111","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"2henryvi:2.1:64"},{"kind":"speech","text":"But that in all my life, when I was a youth.","sourceLine":"2.1.112","speaker":"SIMPCOX","id":"2henryvi:2.1:65"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Too true; and bought his climbing very dear.","sourceLine":"2.1.113","speaker":"Wife","id":"2henryvi:2.1:66"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Mass, thou lovedst plums well, that wouldst\nventure so.","sourceLine":"2.1.114","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"2henryvi:2.1:67"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Alas, good master, my wife desired some damsons,\nAnd made me climb, with danger of my life.","sourceLine":"2.1.116","speaker":"SIMPCOX","id":"2henryvi:2.1:68"},{"kind":"speech","text":"A subtle knave! but yet it shall not serve.\nLet me see thine eyes: wink now: now open them:\nIn my opinion yet thou seest not well.","sourceLine":"2.1.118","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"2henryvi:2.1:69"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Yes, master, clear as day, I thank God and\nSaint Alban.","sourceLine":"2.1.121","speaker":"SIMPCOX","id":"2henryvi:2.1:70"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Say'st thou me so? What colour is this cloak of?","sourceLine":"2.1.123","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"2henryvi:2.1:71"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Red, master; red as blood.","sourceLine":"2.1.124","speaker":"SIMPCOX","id":"2henryvi:2.1:72"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Why, that's well said. What colour is my gown of?","sourceLine":"2.1.125","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"2henryvi:2.1:73"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Black, forsooth: coal-black as jet.","sourceLine":"2.1.126","speaker":"SIMPCOX","id":"2henryvi:2.1:74"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Why, then, thou know'st what colour jet is of?","sourceLine":"2.1.127","speaker":"KING HENRY VI","id":"2henryvi:2.1:75"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And yet, I think, jet did he never see.","sourceLine":"2.1.128","speaker":"SUFFOLK","id":"2henryvi:2.1:76"},{"kind":"speech","text":"But cloaks and gowns, before this day, a many.","sourceLine":"2.1.129","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"2henryvi:2.1:77"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Never, before this day, in all his life.","sourceLine":"2.1.130","speaker":"Wife","id":"2henryvi:2.1:78"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Tell me, sirrah, what's my name?","sourceLine":"2.1.131","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"2henryvi:2.1:79"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Alas, master, I know not.","sourceLine":"2.1.132","speaker":"SIMPCOX","id":"2henryvi:2.1:80"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What's his name?","sourceLine":"2.1.133","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"2henryvi:2.1:81"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I know not.","sourceLine":"2.1.134","speaker":"SIMPCOX","id":"2henryvi:2.1:82"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Nor his?","sourceLine":"2.1.135","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"2henryvi:2.1:83"},{"kind":"speech","text":"No, indeed, master.","sourceLine":"2.1.136","speaker":"SIMPCOX","id":"2henryvi:2.1:84"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What's thine own name?","sourceLine":"2.1.137","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"2henryvi:2.1:85"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Saunder Simpcox, an if it please you, master.","sourceLine":"2.1.138","speaker":"SIMPCOX","id":"2henryvi:2.1:86"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Then, Saunder, sit there, the lyingest knave in\nChristendom. If thou hadst been born blind, thou\nmightest as well have known all our names as thus to\nname the several colours we do wear. Sight may\ndistinguish of colours, but suddenly to nominate them\nall, it is impossible. My lords, Saint Alban here\nhath done a miracle; and would ye not think his\ncunning to be great, that could restore this cripple\nto his legs again?","sourceLine":"2.1.139","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"2henryvi:2.1:87"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O master, that you could!","sourceLine":"2.1.148","speaker":"SIMPCOX","id":"2henryvi:2.1:88"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My masters of Saint Alban's, have you not beadles in\nyour town, and things called whips?","sourceLine":"2.1.149","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"2henryvi:2.1:89"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Yes, my lord, if it please your grace.","sourceLine":"2.1.151","speaker":"Mayor","id":"2henryvi:2.1:90"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Then send for one presently.","sourceLine":"2.1.152","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"2henryvi:2.1:91"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Sirrah, go fetch the beadle hither straight.","sourceLine":"2.1.153","speaker":"Mayor","id":"2henryvi:2.1:92"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit an Attendant","id":"2henryvi:2.1:93"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Now fetch me a stool hither by and by. Now, sirrah,\nif you mean to save yourself from whipping, leap me\nover this stool and run away.","sourceLine":"2.1.154","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"2henryvi:2.1:94"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Alas, master, I am not able to stand alone:\nYou go about to torture me in vain.","sourceLine":"2.1.157","speaker":"SIMPCOX","id":"2henryvi:2.1:95"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter a Beadle with whips","id":"2henryvi:2.1:96"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Well, sir, we must have you find your legs. Sirrah\nbeadle, whip him till he leap over that same stool.","sourceLine":"2.1.159","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"2henryvi:2.1:97"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I will, my lord. Come on, sirrah; off with your\ndoublet quickly.","sourceLine":"2.1.161","speaker":"Beadle","id":"2henryvi:2.1:98"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Alas, master, what shall I do? I am not able to stand.","sourceLine":"2.1.163","speaker":"SIMPCOX","id":"2henryvi:2.1:99"},{"kind":"stage","text":"After the Beadle hath hit him once, he leaps over the stool and runs away; and they follow and cry, 'A miracle!'","id":"2henryvi:2.1:100"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O God, seest Thou this, and bearest so long?","sourceLine":"2.1.164","speaker":"KING HENRY VI","id":"2henryvi:2.1:101"},{"kind":"speech","text":"It made me laugh to see the villain run.","sourceLine":"2.1.165","speaker":"QUEEN MARGARET","id":"2henryvi:2.1:102"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Follow the knave; and take this drab away.","sourceLine":"2.1.166","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"2henryvi:2.1:103"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Alas, sir, we did it for pure need.","sourceLine":"2.1.167","speaker":"Wife","id":"2henryvi:2.1:104"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Let them be whipped through every market-town, till\nthey come to Berwick, from whence they came.","sourceLine":"2.1.168","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"2henryvi:2.1:105"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt Wife, Beadle, Mayor, & c","id":"2henryvi:2.1:106"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Duke Humphrey has done a miracle to-day.","sourceLine":"2.1.170","speaker":"CARDINAL","id":"2henryvi:2.1:107"},{"kind":"speech","text":"True; made the lame to leap and fly away.","sourceLine":"2.1.171","speaker":"SUFFOLK","id":"2henryvi:2.1:108"},{"kind":"speech","text":"But you have done more miracles than I;\nYou made in a day, my lord, whole towns to fly.","sourceLine":"2.1.172","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"2henryvi:2.1:109"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter BUCKINGHAM","id":"2henryvi:2.1:110"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What tidings with our cousin Buckingham?","sourceLine":"2.1.174","speaker":"KING HENRY VI","id":"2henryvi:2.1:111"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Such as my heart doth tremble to unfold.\nA sort of naughty persons, lewdly bent,\nUnder the countenance and confederacy\nOf Lady Eleanor, the protector's wife,\nThe ringleader and head of all this rout,\nHave practised dangerously against your state,\nDealing with witches and with conjurers:\nWhom we have apprehended in the fact;\nRaising up wicked spirits from under ground,\nDemanding of King Henry's life and death,\nAnd other of your highness' privy-council;\nAs more at large your grace shall understand.","sourceLine":"2.1.175","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"2henryvi:2.1:112"},{"kind":"speech","text":"[Aside to GLOUCESTER] And so, my lord protector,\nby this means\nYour lady is forthcoming yet at London.\nThis news, I think, hath turn'd your weapon's edge;\n'Tis like, my lord, you will not keep your hour.","sourceLine":"2.1.187","speaker":"CARDINAL","id":"2henryvi:2.1:113"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ambitious churchman, leave to afflict my heart:\nSorrow and grief have vanquish'd all my powers;\nAnd, vanquish'd as I am, I yield to thee,\nOr to the meanest groom.","sourceLine":"2.1.192","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"2henryvi:2.1:114"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O God, what mischiefs work the wicked ones,\nHeaping confusion on their own heads thereby!","sourceLine":"2.1.196","speaker":"KING HENRY VI","id":"2henryvi:2.1:115"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Gloucester, see here the tainture of thy nest.\nAnd look thyself be faultless, thou wert best.","sourceLine":"2.1.198","speaker":"QUEEN MARGARET","id":"2henryvi:2.1:116"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Madam, for myself, to heaven I do appeal,\nHow I have loved my king and commonweal:\nAnd, for my wife, I know not how it stands;\nSorry I am to hear what I have heard:\nNoble she is, but if she have forgot\nHonour and virtue and conversed with such\nAs, like to pitch, defile nobility,\nI banish her my bed and company\nAnd give her as a prey to law and shame,\nThat hath dishonour'd Gloucester's honest name.","sourceLine":"2.1.200","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"2henryvi:2.1:117"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Well, for this night we will repose us here:\nTo-morrow toward London back again,\nTo look into this business thoroughly\nAnd call these foul offenders to their answers\nAnd poise the cause in justice' equal scales,\nWhose beam stands sure, whose rightful cause prevails.","sourceLine":"2.1.210","speaker":"KING HENRY VI","id":"2henryvi:2.1:118"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Flourish. Exeunt","id":"2henryvi:2.1:119"}],"scene":1},{"id":"2.2","label":"Act 2 · SCENE II. London. YORK'S garden.","act":2,"sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/2henryvi/full.html","blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter YORK, SALISBURY, and WARWICK","id":"2henryvi:2.2:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Now, my good Lords of Salisbury and Warwick,\nOur simple supper ended, give me leave\nIn this close walk to satisfy myself,\nIn craving your opinion of my title,\nWhich is infallible, to England's crown.","sourceLine":"2.2.1","speaker":"YORK","id":"2henryvi:2.2:2"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My lord, I long to hear it at full.","sourceLine":"2.2.6","speaker":"SALISBURY","id":"2henryvi:2.2:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Sweet York, begin: and if thy claim be good,\nThe Nevils are thy subjects to command.","sourceLine":"2.2.7","speaker":"WARWICK","id":"2henryvi:2.2:4"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Then thus:\nEdward the Third, my lords, had seven sons:\nThe first, Edward the Black Prince, Prince of Wales;\nThe second, William of Hatfield, and the third,\nLionel Duke of Clarence: next to whom\nWas John of Gaunt, the Duke of Lancaster;\nThe fifth was Edmund Langley, Duke of York;\nThe sixth was Thomas of Woodstock, Duke of Gloucester;\nWilliam of Windsor was the seventh and last.\nEdward the Black Prince died before his father\nAnd left behind him Richard, his only son,\nWho after Edward the Third's death reign'd as king;\nTill Henry Bolingbroke, Duke of Lancaster,\nThe eldest son and heir of John of Gaunt,\nCrown'd by the name of Henry the Fourth,\nSeized on the realm, deposed the rightful king,\nSent his poor queen to France, from whence she came,\nAnd him to Pomfret; where, as all you know,\nHarmless Richard was murder'd traitorously.","sourceLine":"2.2.9","speaker":"YORK","id":"2henryvi:2.2:5"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Father, the duke hath told the truth:\nThus got the house of Lancaster the crown.","sourceLine":"2.2.28","speaker":"WARWICK","id":"2henryvi:2.2:6"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Which now they hold by force and not by right;\nFor Richard, the first son's heir, being dead,\nThe issue of the next son should have reign'd.","sourceLine":"2.2.30","speaker":"YORK","id":"2henryvi:2.2:7"},{"kind":"speech","text":"But William of Hatfield died without an heir.","sourceLine":"2.2.33","speaker":"SALISBURY","id":"2henryvi:2.2:8"},{"kind":"speech","text":"The third son, Duke of Clarence, from whose line\nI claimed the crown, had issue, Philippe, a daughter,\nWho married Edmund Mortimer, Earl of March:\nEdmund had issue, Roger Earl of March;\nRoger had issue, Edmund, Anne and Eleanor.","sourceLine":"2.2.34","speaker":"YORK","id":"2henryvi:2.2:9"},{"kind":"speech","text":"This Edmund, in the reign of Bolingbroke,\nAs I have read, laid claim unto the crown;\nAnd, but for Owen Glendower, had been king,\nWho kept him in captivity till he died.\nBut to the rest.","sourceLine":"2.2.39","speaker":"SALISBURY","id":"2henryvi:2.2:10"},{"kind":"speech","text":"His eldest sister, Anne,\nMy mother, being heir unto the crown\nMarried Richard Earl of Cambridge; who was son\nTo Edmund Langley, Edward the Third's fifth son.\nBy her I claim the kingdom: she was heir\nTo Roger Earl of March, who was the son\nOf Edmund Mortimer, who married Philippe,\nSole daughter unto Lionel Duke of Clarence:\nSo, if the issue of the elder son\nSucceed before the younger, I am king.","sourceLine":"2.2.44","speaker":"YORK","id":"2henryvi:2.2:11"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What plain proceeding is more plain than this?\nHenry doth claim the crown from John of Gaunt,\nThe fourth son; York claims it from the third.\nTill Lionel's issue fails, his should not reign:\nIt fails not yet, but flourishes in thee\nAnd in thy sons, fair slips of such a stock.\nThen, father Salisbury, kneel we together;\nAnd in this private plot be we the first\nThat shall salute our rightful sovereign\nWith honour of his birthright to the crown.","sourceLine":"2.2.54","speaker":"WARWICK","id":"2henryvi:2.2:12"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Long live our sovereign Richard, England's king!","sourceLine":"2.2.64","speaker":"BOTH","id":"2henryvi:2.2:13"},{"kind":"speech","text":"We thank you, lords. But I am not your king\nTill I be crown'd and that my sword be stain'd\nWith heart-blood of the house of Lancaster;\nAnd that's not suddenly to be perform'd,\nBut with advice and silent secrecy.\nDo you as I do in these dangerous days:\nWink at the Duke of Suffolk's insolence,\nAt Beaufort's pride, at Somerset's ambition,\nAt Buckingham and all the crew of them,\nTill they have snared the shepherd of the flock,\nThat virtuous prince, the good Duke Humphrey:\n'Tis that they seek, and they in seeking that\nShall find their deaths, if York can prophesy.","sourceLine":"2.2.65","speaker":"YORK","id":"2henryvi:2.2:14"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My lord, break we off; we know your mind at full.","sourceLine":"2.2.78","speaker":"SALISBURY","id":"2henryvi:2.2:15"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My heart assures me that the Earl of Warwick\nShall one day make the Duke of York a king.","sourceLine":"2.2.79","speaker":"WARWICK","id":"2henryvi:2.2:16"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And, Nevil, this I do assure myself:\nRichard shall live to make the Earl of Warwick\nThe greatest man in England but the king.","sourceLine":"2.2.81","speaker":"YORK","id":"2henryvi:2.2:17"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt","id":"2henryvi:2.2:18"}],"scene":2},{"id":"2.3","label":"Act 2 · SCENE III. A hall of justice.","act":2,"sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/2henryvi/full.html","blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Sound trumpets. Enter KING HENRY VI, QUEEN MARGARET, GLOUCESTER, YORK, SUFFOLK, and SALISBURY; the DUCHESS, MARGARET JOURDAIN, SOUTHWELL, HUME, and BOLINGBROKE, under guard","id":"2henryvi:2.3:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Stand forth, Dame Eleanor Cobham, Gloucester's wife:\nIn sight of God and us, your guilt is great:\nReceive the sentence of the law for sins\nSuch as by God's book are adjudged to death.\nYou four, from hence to prison back again;\nFrom thence unto the place of execution:\nThe witch in Smithfield shall be burn'd to ashes,\nAnd you three shall be strangled on the gallows.\nYou, madam, for you are more nobly born,\nDespoiled of your honour in your life,\nShall, after three days' open penance done,\nLive in your country here in banishment,\nWith Sir John Stanley, in the Isle of Man.","sourceLine":"2.3.1","speaker":"KING HENRY VI","id":"2henryvi:2.3:2"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Welcome is banishment; welcome were my death.","sourceLine":"2.3.14","speaker":"DUCHESS","id":"2henryvi:2.3:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Eleanor, the law, thou see'st, hath judged thee:\nI cannot justify whom the law condemns.","sourceLine":"2.3.15","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"2henryvi:2.3:4"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt DUCHESS and other prisoners, guarded","id":"2henryvi:2.3:5"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Mine eyes are full of tears, my heart of grief.\nAh, Humphrey, this dishonour in thine age\nWill bring thy head with sorrow to the ground!\nI beseech your majesty, give me leave to go;\nSorrow would solace and mine age would ease.","sourceLine":"2.3.17","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"2henryvi:2.3:6"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Stay, Humphrey Duke of Gloucester: ere thou go,\nGive up thy staff: Henry will to himself\nProtector be; and God shall be my hope,\nMy stay, my guide and lantern to my feet:\nAnd go in peace, Humphrey, no less beloved\nThan when thou wert protector to thy King.","sourceLine":"2.3.22","speaker":"KING HENRY VI","id":"2henryvi:2.3:7"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I see no reason why a king of years\nShould be to be protected like a child.\nGod and King Henry govern England's realm.\nGive up your staff, sir, and the king his realm.","sourceLine":"2.3.28","speaker":"QUEEN MARGARET","id":"2henryvi:2.3:8"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My staff? here, noble Henry, is my staff:\nAs willingly do I the same resign\nAs e'er thy father Henry made it mine;\nAnd even as willingly at thy feet I leave it\nAs others would ambitiously receive it.\nFarewell, good king: when I am dead and gone,\nMay honourable peace attend thy throne!","sourceLine":"2.3.32","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"2henryvi:2.3:9"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit","id":"2henryvi:2.3:10"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Why, now is Henry king, and Margaret queen;\nAnd Humphrey Duke of Gloucester scarce himself,\nThat bears so shrewd a maim; two pulls at once;\nHis lady banish'd, and a limb lopp'd off.\nThis staff of honour raught, there let it stand\nWhere it best fits to be, in Henry's hand.","sourceLine":"2.3.39","speaker":"QUEEN MARGARET","id":"2henryvi:2.3:11"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Thus droops this lofty pine and hangs his sprays;\nThus Eleanor's pride dies in her youngest days.","sourceLine":"2.3.45","speaker":"SUFFOLK","id":"2henryvi:2.3:12"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Lords, let him go. Please it your majesty,\nThis is the day appointed for the combat;\nAnd ready are the appellant and defendant,\nThe armourer and his man, to enter the lists,\nSo please your highness to behold the fight.","sourceLine":"2.3.47","speaker":"YORK","id":"2henryvi:2.3:13"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ay, good my lord; for purposely therefore\nLeft I the court, to see this quarrel tried.","sourceLine":"2.3.52","speaker":"QUEEN MARGARET","id":"2henryvi:2.3:14"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O God's name, see the lists and all things fit:\nHere let them end it; and God defend the right!","sourceLine":"2.3.54","speaker":"KING HENRY VI","id":"2henryvi:2.3:15"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I never saw a fellow worse bested,\nOr more afraid to fight, than is the appellant,\nThe servant of this armourer, my lords.","sourceLine":"2.3.56","speaker":"YORK","id":"2henryvi:2.3:16"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter at one door, HORNER, the Armourer, and his Neighbours, drinking to him so much that he is drunk; and he enters with a drum before him and his staff with a sand-bag fastened to it; and at the other door PETER, his man, with a drum and sand-bag, and 'Prentices drinking to him","id":"2henryvi:2.3:17"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Here, neighbour Horner, I drink to you in a cup of\nsack: and fear not, neighbour, you shall do well enough.","sourceLine":"2.3.59","speaker":"First Neighbour","id":"2henryvi:2.3:18"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And here, neighbour, here's a cup of charneco.","sourceLine":"2.3.61","speaker":"Second Neighbour","id":"2henryvi:2.3:19"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And here's a pot of good double beer, neighbour:\ndrink, and fear not your man.","sourceLine":"2.3.62","speaker":"Third Neighbour","id":"2henryvi:2.3:20"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Let it come, i' faith, and I'll pledge you all; and\na fig for Peter!\nFirst 'Prentice Here, Peter, I drink to thee: and be not afraid.\nSecond 'Prentice Be merry, Peter, and fear not thy master: fight\nfor credit of the 'prentices.","sourceLine":"2.3.64","speaker":"HORNER","id":"2henryvi:2.3:21"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I thank you all: drink, and pray for me, I pray\nyou; for I think I have taken my last draught in\nthis world. Here, Robin, an if I die, I give thee\nmy apron: and, Will, thou shalt have my hammer:\nand here, Tom, take all the money that I have. O\nLord bless me! I pray God! for I am never able to\ndeal with my master, he hath learnt me so much fence already.","sourceLine":"2.3.69","speaker":"PETER","id":"2henryvi:2.3:22"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Come, leave your drinking, and fall to blows.\nSirrah, what's thy name?","sourceLine":"2.3.76","speaker":"SALISBURY","id":"2henryvi:2.3:23"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Peter, forsooth.","sourceLine":"2.3.78","speaker":"PETER","id":"2henryvi:2.3:24"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Peter! what more?","sourceLine":"2.3.79","speaker":"SALISBURY","id":"2henryvi:2.3:25"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Thump.","sourceLine":"2.3.80","speaker":"PETER","id":"2henryvi:2.3:26"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Thump! then see thou thump thy master well.","sourceLine":"2.3.81","speaker":"SALISBURY","id":"2henryvi:2.3:27"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Masters, I am come hither, as it were, upon my man's\ninstigation, to prove him a knave and myself an\nhonest man: and touching the Duke of York, I will\ntake my death, I never meant him any ill, nor the\nking, nor the queen: and therefore, Peter, have at\nthee with a downright blow!","sourceLine":"2.3.82","speaker":"HORNER","id":"2henryvi:2.3:28"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Dispatch: this knave's tongue begins to double.\nSound, trumpets, alarum to the combatants!","sourceLine":"2.3.88","speaker":"YORK","id":"2henryvi:2.3:29"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Alarum. They fight, and PETER strikes him down","id":"2henryvi:2.3:30"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Hold, Peter, hold! I confess, I confess treason.","sourceLine":"2.3.90","speaker":"HORNER","id":"2henryvi:2.3:31"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Dies","id":"2henryvi:2.3:32"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Take away his weapon. Fellow, thank God, and the\ngood wine in thy master's way.","sourceLine":"2.3.91","speaker":"YORK","id":"2henryvi:2.3:33"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O God, have I overcome mine enemy in this presence?\nO Peter, thou hast prevailed in right!","sourceLine":"2.3.93","speaker":"PETER","id":"2henryvi:2.3:34"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Go, take hence that traitor from our sight;\nFor his death we do perceive his guilt:\nAnd God in justice hath revealed to us\nThe truth and innocence of this poor fellow,\nWhich he had thought to have murder'd wrongfully.\nCome, fellow, follow us for thy reward.","sourceLine":"2.3.95","speaker":"KING HENRY VI","id":"2henryvi:2.3:35"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Sound a flourish. Exeunt","id":"2henryvi:2.3:36"}],"scene":3},{"id":"2.4","label":"Act 2 · SCENE IV. A street.","act":2,"sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/2henryvi/full.html","blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter GLOUCESTER and his Servingmen, in mourning cloaks","id":"2henryvi:2.4:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Thus sometimes hath the brightest day a cloud;\nAnd after summer evermore succeeds\nBarren winter, with his wrathful nipping cold:\nSo cares and joys abound, as seasons fleet.\nSirs, what's o'clock?","sourceLine":"2.4.1","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"2henryvi:2.4:2"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ten, my lord.","sourceLine":"2.4.6","speaker":"Servants","id":"2henryvi:2.4:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ten is the hour that was appointed me\nTo watch the coming of my punish'd duchess:\nUneath may she endure the flinty streets,\nTo tread them with her tender-feeling feet.\nSweet Nell, ill can thy noble mind abrook\nThe abject people gazing on thy face,\nWith envious looks, laughing at thy shame,\nThat erst did follow thy proud chariot-wheels\nWhen thou didst ride in triumph through the streets.\nBut, soft! I think she comes; and I'll prepare\nMy tear-stain'd eyes to see her miseries.","sourceLine":"2.4.7","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"2henryvi:2.4:4"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter the DUCHESS in a white sheet, and a taper burning in her hand; with STANLEY, the Sheriff, and Officers","id":"2henryvi:2.4:5"},{"kind":"speech","text":"So please your grace, we'll take her from the sheriff.","sourceLine":"2.4.18","speaker":"Servant","id":"2henryvi:2.4:6"},{"kind":"speech","text":"No, stir not, for your lives; let her pass by.","sourceLine":"2.4.19","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"2henryvi:2.4:7"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Come you, my lord, to see my open shame?\nNow thou dost penance too. Look how they gaze!\nSee how the giddy multitude do point,\nAnd nod their heads, and throw their eyes on thee!\nAh, Gloucester, hide thee from their hateful looks,\nAnd, in thy closet pent up, rue my shame,\nAnd ban thine enemies, both mine and thine!","sourceLine":"2.4.20","speaker":"DUCHESS","id":"2henryvi:2.4:8"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Be patient, gentle Nell; forget this grief.","sourceLine":"2.4.27","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"2henryvi:2.4:9"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ah, Gloucester, teach me to forget myself!\nFor whilst I think I am thy married wife\nAnd thou a prince, protector of this land,\nMethinks I should not thus be led along,\nMail'd up in shame, with papers on my back,\nAnd followed with a rabble that rejoice\nTo see my tears and hear my deep-fet groans.\nThe ruthless flint doth cut my tender feet,\nAnd when I start, the envious people laugh\nAnd bid me be advised how I tread.\nAh, Humphrey, can I bear this shameful yoke?\nTrow'st thou that e'er I'll look upon the world,\nOr count them happy that enjoy the sun?\nNo; dark shall be my light and night my day;\nTo think upon my pomp shall be my hell.\nSometime I'll say, I am Duke Humphrey's wife,\nAnd he a prince and ruler of the land:\nYet so he ruled and such a prince he was\nAs he stood by whilst I, his forlorn duchess,\nWas made a wonder and a pointing-stock\nTo every idle rascal follower.\nBut be thou mild and blush not at my shame,\nNor stir at nothing till the axe of death\nHang over thee, as, sure, it shortly will;\nFor Suffolk, he that can do all in all\nWith her that hateth thee and hates us all,\nAnd York and impious Beaufort, that false priest,\nHave all limed bushes to betray thy wings,\nAnd, fly thou how thou canst, they'll tangle thee:\nBut fear not thou, until thy foot be snared,\nNor never seek prevention of thy foes.","sourceLine":"2.4.28","speaker":"DUCHESS","id":"2henryvi:2.4:10"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ah, Nell, forbear! thou aimest all awry;\nI must offend before I be attainted;\nAnd had I twenty times so many foes,\nAnd each of them had twenty times their power,\nAll these could not procure me any scathe,\nSo long as I am loyal, true and crimeless.\nWouldst have me rescue thee from this reproach?\nWhy, yet thy scandal were not wiped away\nBut I in danger for the breach of law.\nThy greatest help is quiet, gentle Nell:\nI pray thee, sort thy heart to patience;\nThese few days' wonder will be quickly worn.","sourceLine":"2.4.59","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"2henryvi:2.4:11"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter a Herald","id":"2henryvi:2.4:12"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I summon your grace to his majesty's parliament,\nHolden at Bury the first of this next month.","sourceLine":"2.4.71","speaker":"Herald","id":"2henryvi:2.4:13"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And my consent ne'er ask'd herein before!\nThis is close dealing. Well, I will be there.","sourceLine":"2.4.73","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"2henryvi:2.4:14"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit Herald","id":"2henryvi:2.4:15"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My Nell, I take my leave: and, master sheriff,\nLet not her penance exceed the king's commission.","sourceLine":"2.4.75","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"2henryvi:2.4:16"},{"kind":"speech","text":"An't please your grace, here my commission stays,\nAnd Sir John Stanley is appointed now\nTo take her with him to the Isle of Man.","sourceLine":"2.4.77","speaker":"Sheriff","id":"2henryvi:2.4:17"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Must you, Sir John, protect my lady here?","sourceLine":"2.4.80","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"2henryvi:2.4:18"},{"kind":"speech","text":"So am I given in charge, may't please your grace.","sourceLine":"2.4.81","speaker":"STANLEY","id":"2henryvi:2.4:19"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Entreat her not the worse in that I pray\nYou use her well: the world may laugh again;\nAnd I may live to do you kindness if\nYou do it her: and so, Sir John, farewell!","sourceLine":"2.4.82","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"2henryvi:2.4:20"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What, gone, my lord, and bid me not farewell!","sourceLine":"2.4.86","speaker":"DUCHESS","id":"2henryvi:2.4:21"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Witness my tears, I cannot stay to speak.","sourceLine":"2.4.87","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"2henryvi:2.4:22"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt GLOUCESTER and Servingmen","id":"2henryvi:2.4:23"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Art thou gone too? all comfort go with thee!\nFor none abides with me: my joy is death;\nDeath, at whose name I oft have been afear'd,\nBecause I wish'd this world's eternity.\nStanley, I prithee, go, and take me hence;\nI care not whither, for I beg no favour,\nOnly convey me where thou art commanded.","sourceLine":"2.4.88","speaker":"DUCHESS","id":"2henryvi:2.4:24"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Why, madam, that is to the Isle of Man;\nThere to be used according to your state.","sourceLine":"2.4.95","speaker":"STANLEY","id":"2henryvi:2.4:25"},{"kind":"speech","text":"That's bad enough, for I am but reproach:\nAnd shall I then be used reproachfully?","sourceLine":"2.4.97","speaker":"DUCHESS","id":"2henryvi:2.4:26"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Like to a duchess, and Duke Humphrey's lady;\nAccording to that state you shall be used.","sourceLine":"2.4.99","speaker":"STANLEY","id":"2henryvi:2.4:27"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Sheriff, farewell, and better than I fare,\nAlthough thou hast been conduct of my shame.","sourceLine":"2.4.101","speaker":"DUCHESS","id":"2henryvi:2.4:28"},{"kind":"speech","text":"It is my office; and, madam, pardon me.","sourceLine":"2.4.103","speaker":"Sheriff","id":"2henryvi:2.4:29"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ay, ay, farewell; thy office is discharged.\nCome, Stanley, shall we go?","sourceLine":"2.4.104","speaker":"DUCHESS","id":"2henryvi:2.4:30"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Madam, your penance done, throw off this sheet,\nAnd go we to attire you for our journey.","sourceLine":"2.4.106","speaker":"STANLEY","id":"2henryvi:2.4:31"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My shame will not be shifted with my sheet:\nNo, it will hang upon my richest robes\nAnd show itself, attire me how I can.\nGo, lead the way; I long to see my prison.","sourceLine":"2.4.108","speaker":"DUCHESS","id":"2henryvi:2.4:32"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt","id":"2henryvi:2.4:33"}],"scene":4},{"id":"3.1","label":"Act 3 · SCENE I. The Abbey at Bury St. Edmund's.","act":3,"sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/2henryvi/full.html","blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Sound a sennet. Enter KING HENRY VI, QUEEN MARGARET, CARDINAL, SUFFOLK, YORK, BUCKINGHAM, SALISBURY and WARWICK to the Parliament","id":"2henryvi:3.1:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I muse my Lord of Gloucester is not come:\n'Tis not his wont to be the hindmost man,\nWhate'er occasion keeps him from us now.","sourceLine":"3.1.1","speaker":"KING HENRY VI","id":"2henryvi:3.1:2"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Can you not see? or will ye not observe\nThe strangeness of his alter'd countenance?\nWith what a majesty he bears himself,\nHow insolent of late he is become,\nHow proud, how peremptory, and unlike himself?\nWe know the time since he was mild and affable,\nAnd if we did but glance a far-off look,\nImmediately he was upon his knee,\nThat all the court admired him for submission:\nBut meet him now, and, be it in the morn,\nWhen every one will give the time of day,\nHe knits his brow and shows an angry eye,\nAnd passeth by with stiff unbowed knee,\nDisdaining duty that to us belongs.\nSmall curs are not regarded when they grin;\nBut great men tremble when the lion roars;\nAnd Humphrey is no little man in England.\nFirst note that he is near you in descent,\nAnd should you fall, he as the next will mount.\nMe seemeth then it is no policy,\nRespecting what a rancorous mind he bears\nAnd his advantage following your decease,\nThat he should come about your royal person\nOr be admitted to your highness' council.\nBy flattery hath he won the commons' hearts,\nAnd when he please to make commotion,\n'Tis to be fear'd they all will follow him.\nNow 'tis the spring, and weeds are shallow-rooted;\nSuffer them now, and they'll o'ergrow the garden\nAnd choke the herbs for want of husbandry.\nThe reverent care I bear unto my lord\nMade me collect these dangers in the duke.\nIf it be fond, call it a woman's fear;\nWhich fear if better reasons can supplant,\nI will subscribe and say I wrong'd the duke.\nMy Lord of Suffolk, Buckingham, and York,\nReprove my allegation, if you can;\nOr else conclude my words effectual.","sourceLine":"3.1.4","speaker":"QUEEN MARGARET","id":"2henryvi:3.1:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Well hath your highness seen into this duke;\nAnd, had I first been put to speak my mind,\nI think I should have told your grace's tale.\nThe duchess, by his subornation,\nUpon my life, began her devilish practises:\nOr, if he were not privy to those faults,\nYet, by reputing of his high descent,\nAs next the king he was successive heir,\nAnd such high vaunts of his nobility,\nDid instigate the bedlam brain-sick duchess\nBy wicked means to frame our sovereign's fall.\nSmooth runs the water where the brook is deep;\nAnd in his simple show he harbours treason.\nThe fox barks not when he would steal the lamb.\nNo, no, my sovereign; Gloucester is a man\nUnsounded yet and full of deep deceit.","sourceLine":"3.1.42","speaker":"SUFFOLK","id":"2henryvi:3.1:4"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Did he not, contrary to form of law,\nDevise strange deaths for small offences done?","sourceLine":"3.1.58","speaker":"CARDINAL","id":"2henryvi:3.1:5"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And did he not, in his protectorship,\nLevy great sums of money through the realm\nFor soldiers' pay in France, and never sent it?\nBy means whereof the towns each day revolted.","sourceLine":"3.1.60","speaker":"YORK","id":"2henryvi:3.1:6"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Tut, these are petty faults to faults unknown.\nWhich time will bring to light in smooth\nDuke Humphrey.","sourceLine":"3.1.64","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"2henryvi:3.1:7"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My lords, at once: the care you have of us,\nTo mow down thorns that would annoy our foot,\nIs worthy praise: but, shall I speak my conscience,\nOur kinsman Gloucester is as innocent\nFrom meaning treason to our royal person\nAs is the sucking lamb or harmless dove:\nThe duke is virtuous, mild and too well given\nTo dream on evil or to work my downfall.","sourceLine":"3.1.67","speaker":"KING HENRY VI","id":"2henryvi:3.1:8"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ah, what's more dangerous than this fond affiance!\nSeems he a dove? his feathers are but borrowed,\nFor he's disposed as the hateful raven:\nIs he a lamb? his skin is surely lent him,\nFor he's inclined as is the ravenous wolf.\nWho cannot steal a shape that means deceit?\nTake heed, my lord; the welfare of us all\nHangs on the cutting short that fraudful man.","sourceLine":"3.1.75","speaker":"QUEEN MARGARET","id":"2henryvi:3.1:9"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter SOMERSET","id":"2henryvi:3.1:10"},{"kind":"speech","text":"All health unto my gracious sovereign!","sourceLine":"3.1.83","speaker":"SOMERSET","id":"2henryvi:3.1:11"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Welcome, Lord Somerset. What news from France?","sourceLine":"3.1.84","speaker":"KING HENRY VI","id":"2henryvi:3.1:12"},{"kind":"speech","text":"That all your interest in those territories\nIs utterly bereft you; all is lost.","sourceLine":"3.1.85","speaker":"SOMERSET","id":"2henryvi:3.1:13"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Cold news, Lord Somerset: but God's will be done!","sourceLine":"3.1.87","speaker":"KING HENRY VI","id":"2henryvi:3.1:14"},{"kind":"speech","text":"[Aside] Cold news for me; for I had hope of France\nAs firmly as I hope for fertile England.\nThus are my blossoms blasted in the bud\nAnd caterpillars eat my leaves away;\nBut I will remedy this gear ere long,\nOr sell my title for a glorious grave.","sourceLine":"3.1.88","speaker":"YORK","id":"2henryvi:3.1:15"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter GLOUCESTER","id":"2henryvi:3.1:16"},{"kind":"speech","text":"All happiness unto my lord the king!\nPardon, my liege, that I have stay'd so long.","sourceLine":"3.1.94","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"2henryvi:3.1:17"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Nay, Gloucester, know that thou art come too soon,\nUnless thou wert more loyal than thou art:\nI do arrest thee of high treason here.","sourceLine":"3.1.96","speaker":"SUFFOLK","id":"2henryvi:3.1:18"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Well, Suffolk, thou shalt not see me blush\nNor change my countenance for this arrest:\nA heart unspotted is not easily daunted.\nThe purest spring is not so free from mud\nAs I am clear from treason to my sovereign:\nWho can accuse me? wherein am I guilty?","sourceLine":"3.1.99","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"2henryvi:3.1:19"},{"kind":"speech","text":"'Tis thought, my lord, that you took bribes of France,\nAnd, being protector, stayed the soldiers' pay;\nBy means whereof his highness hath lost France.","sourceLine":"3.1.105","speaker":"YORK","id":"2henryvi:3.1:20"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Is it but thought so? what are they that think it?\nI never robb'd the soldiers of their pay,\nNor ever had one penny bribe from France.\nSo help me God, as I have watch'd the night,\nAy, night by night, in studying good for England,\nThat doit that e'er I wrested from the king,\nOr any groat I hoarded to my use,\nBe brought against me at my trial-day!\nNo; many a pound of mine own proper store,\nBecause I would not tax the needy commons,\nHave I disbursed to the garrisons,\nAnd never ask'd for restitution.","sourceLine":"3.1.108","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"2henryvi:3.1:21"},{"kind":"speech","text":"It serves you well, my lord, to say so much.","sourceLine":"3.1.120","speaker":"CARDINAL","id":"2henryvi:3.1:22"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I say no more than truth, so help me God!","sourceLine":"3.1.121","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"2henryvi:3.1:23"},{"kind":"speech","text":"In your protectorship you did devise\nStrange tortures for offenders never heard of,\nThat England was defamed by tyranny.","sourceLine":"3.1.122","speaker":"YORK","id":"2henryvi:3.1:24"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Why, 'tis well known that, whiles I was\nprotector,\nPity was all the fault that was in me;\nFor I should melt at an offender's tears,\nAnd lowly words were ransom for their fault.\nUnless it were a bloody murderer,\nOr foul felonious thief that fleeced poor passengers,\nI never gave them condign punishment:\nMurder indeed, that bloody sin, I tortured\nAbove the felon or what trespass else.","sourceLine":"3.1.125","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"2henryvi:3.1:25"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My lord, these faults are easy, quickly answered:\nBut mightier crimes are laid unto your charge,\nWhereof you cannot easily purge yourself.\nI do arrest you in his highness' name;\nAnd here commit you to my lord cardinal\nTo keep, until your further time of trial.","sourceLine":"3.1.135","speaker":"SUFFOLK","id":"2henryvi:3.1:26"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My lord of Gloucester, 'tis my special hope\nThat you will clear yourself from all suspect:\nMy conscience tells me you are innocent.","sourceLine":"3.1.141","speaker":"KING HENRY VI","id":"2henryvi:3.1:27"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ah, gracious lord, these days are dangerous:\nVirtue is choked with foul ambition\nAnd charity chased hence by rancour's hand;\nFoul subornation is predominant\nAnd equity exiled your highness' land.\nI know their complot is to have my life,\nAnd if my death might make this island happy,\nAnd prove the period of their tyranny,\nI would expend it with all willingness:\nBut mine is made the prologue to their play;\nFor thousands more, that yet suspect no peril,\nWill not conclude their plotted tragedy.\nBeaufort's red sparkling eyes blab his heart's malice,\nAnd Suffolk's cloudy brow his stormy hate;\nSharp Buckingham unburthens with his tongue\nThe envious load that lies upon his heart;\nAnd dogged York, that reaches at the moon,\nWhose overweening arm I have pluck'd back,\nBy false accuse doth level at my life:\nAnd you, my sovereign lady, with the rest,\nCauseless have laid disgraces on my head,\nAnd with your best endeavour have stirr'd up\nMy liefest liege to be mine enemy:\nAy, all you have laid your heads together--\nMyself had notice of your conventicles--\nAnd all to make away my guiltless life.\nI shall not want false witness to condemn me,\nNor store of treasons to augment my guilt;\nThe ancient proverb will be well effected:\n'A staff is quickly found to beat a dog.'","sourceLine":"3.1.144","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"2henryvi:3.1:28"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My liege, his railing is intolerable:\nIf those that care to keep your royal person\nFrom treason's secret knife and traitors' rage\nBe thus upbraided, chid and rated at,\nAnd the offender granted scope of speech,\n'Twill make them cool in zeal unto your grace.","sourceLine":"3.1.174","speaker":"CARDINAL","id":"2henryvi:3.1:29"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Hath he not twit our sovereign lady here\nWith ignominious words, though clerkly couch'd,\nAs if she had suborned some to swear\nFalse allegations to o'erthrow his state?","sourceLine":"3.1.180","speaker":"SUFFOLK","id":"2henryvi:3.1:30"},{"kind":"speech","text":"But I can give the loser leave to chide.","sourceLine":"3.1.184","speaker":"QUEEN MARGARET","id":"2henryvi:3.1:31"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Far truer spoke than meant: I lose, indeed;\nBeshrew the winners, for they play'd me false!\nAnd well such losers may have leave to speak.","sourceLine":"3.1.185","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"2henryvi:3.1:32"},{"kind":"speech","text":"He'll wrest the sense and hold us here all day:\nLord cardinal, he is your prisoner.","sourceLine":"3.1.188","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"2henryvi:3.1:33"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Sirs, take away the duke, and guard him sure.","sourceLine":"3.1.190","speaker":"CARDINAL","id":"2henryvi:3.1:34"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ah! thus King Henry throws away his crutch\nBefore his legs be firm to bear his body.\nThus is the shepherd beaten from thy side,\nAnd wolves are gnarling who shall gnaw thee first.\nAh, that my fear were false! ah, that it were!\nFor, good King Henry, thy decay I fear.","sourceLine":"3.1.191","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"2henryvi:3.1:35"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit, guarded","id":"2henryvi:3.1:36"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My lords, what to your wisdoms seemeth best,\nDo or undo, as if ourself were here.","sourceLine":"3.1.197","speaker":"KING HENRY VI","id":"2henryvi:3.1:37"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What, will your highness leave the parliament?","sourceLine":"3.1.199","speaker":"QUEEN MARGARET","id":"2henryvi:3.1:38"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ay, Margaret; my heart is drown'd with grief,\nWhose flood begins to flow within mine eyes,\nMy body round engirt with misery,\nFor what's more miserable than discontent?\nAh, uncle Humphrey! in thy face I see\nThe map of honour, truth and loyalty:\nAnd yet, good Humphrey, is the hour to come\nThat e'er I proved thee false or fear'd thy faith.\nWhat louring star now envies thy estate,\nThat these great lords and Margaret our queen\nDo seek subversion of thy harmless life?\nThou never didst them wrong, nor no man wrong;\nAnd as the butcher takes away the calf\nAnd binds the wretch, and beats it when it strays,\nBearing it to the bloody slaughter-house,\nEven so remorseless have they borne him hence;\nAnd as the dam runs lowing up and down,\nLooking the way her harmless young one went,\nAnd can do nought but wail her darling's loss,\nEven so myself bewails good Gloucester's case\nWith sad unhelpful tears, and with dimm'd eyes\nLook after him and cannot do him good,\nSo mighty are his vowed enemies.\nHis fortunes I will weep; and, 'twixt each groan\nSay 'Who's a traitor? Gloucester he is none.'","sourceLine":"3.1.200","speaker":"KING HENRY VI","id":"2henryvi:3.1:39"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt all but QUEEN MARGARET, CARDINAL, SUFFOLK, and YORK; SOMERSET remains apart","id":"2henryvi:3.1:40"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Free lords, cold snow melts with the sun's hot beams.\nHenry my lord is cold in great affairs,\nToo full of foolish pity, and Gloucester's show\nBeguiles him as the mournful crocodile\nWith sorrow snares relenting passengers,\nOr as the snake roll'd in a flowering bank,\nWith shining chequer'd slough, doth sting a child\nThat for the beauty thinks it excellent.\nBelieve me, lords, were none more wise than I--\nAnd yet herein I judge mine own wit good--\nThis Gloucester should be quickly rid the world,\nTo rid us of the fear we have of him.","sourceLine":"3.1.225","speaker":"QUEEN MARGARET","id":"2henryvi:3.1:41"},{"kind":"speech","text":"That he should die is worthy policy;\nBut yet we want a colour for his death:\n'Tis meet he be condemn'd by course of law.","sourceLine":"3.1.237","speaker":"CARDINAL","id":"2henryvi:3.1:42"},{"kind":"speech","text":"But, in my mind, that were no policy:\nThe king will labour still to save his life,\nThe commons haply rise, to save his life;\nAnd yet we have but trivial argument,\nMore than mistrust, that shows him worthy death.","sourceLine":"3.1.240","speaker":"SUFFOLK","id":"2henryvi:3.1:43"},{"kind":"speech","text":"So that, by this, you would not have him die.","sourceLine":"3.1.245","speaker":"YORK","id":"2henryvi:3.1:44"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ah, York, no man alive so fain as I!","sourceLine":"3.1.246","speaker":"SUFFOLK","id":"2henryvi:3.1:45"},{"kind":"speech","text":"'Tis York that hath more reason for his death.\nBut, my lord cardinal, and you, my Lord of Suffolk,\nSay as you think, and speak it from your souls,\nWere't not all one, an empty eagle were set\nTo guard the chicken from a hungry kite,\nAs place Duke Humphrey for the king's protector?","sourceLine":"3.1.247","speaker":"YORK","id":"2henryvi:3.1:46"},{"kind":"speech","text":"So the poor chicken should be sure of death.","sourceLine":"3.1.253","speaker":"QUEEN MARGARET","id":"2henryvi:3.1:47"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Madam, 'tis true; and were't not madness, then,\nTo make the fox surveyor of the fold?\nWho being accused a crafty murderer,\nHis guilt should be but idly posted over,\nBecause his purpose is not executed.\nNo; let him die, in that he is a fox,\nBy nature proved an enemy to the flock,\nBefore his chaps be stain'd with crimson blood,\nAs Humphrey, proved by reasons, to my liege.\nAnd do not stand on quillets how to slay him:\nBe it by gins, by snares, by subtlety,\nSleeping or waking, 'tis no matter how,\nSo he be dead; for that is good deceit\nWhich mates him first that first intends deceit.","sourceLine":"3.1.254","speaker":"SUFFOLK","id":"2henryvi:3.1:48"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Thrice-noble Suffolk, 'tis resolutely spoke.","sourceLine":"3.1.268","speaker":"QUEEN MARGARET","id":"2henryvi:3.1:49"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Not resolute, except so much were done;\nFor things are often spoke and seldom meant:\nBut that my heart accordeth with my tongue,\nSeeing the deed is meritorious,\nAnd to preserve my sovereign from his foe,\nSay but the word, and I will be his priest.","sourceLine":"3.1.269","speaker":"SUFFOLK","id":"2henryvi:3.1:50"},{"kind":"speech","text":"But I would have him dead, my Lord of Suffolk,\nEre you can take due orders for a priest:\nSay you consent and censure well the deed,\nAnd I'll provide his executioner,\nI tender so the safety of my liege.","sourceLine":"3.1.275","speaker":"CARDINAL","id":"2henryvi:3.1:51"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Here is my hand, the deed is worthy doing.","sourceLine":"3.1.280","speaker":"SUFFOLK","id":"2henryvi:3.1:52"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And so say I.","sourceLine":"3.1.281","speaker":"QUEEN MARGARET","id":"2henryvi:3.1:53"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And I and now we three have spoke it,\nIt skills not greatly who impugns our doom.","sourceLine":"3.1.282","speaker":"YORK","id":"2henryvi:3.1:54"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter a Post","id":"2henryvi:3.1:55"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Great lords, from Ireland am I come amain,\nTo signify that rebels there are up\nAnd put the Englishmen unto the sword:\nSend succors, lords, and stop the rage betime,\nBefore the wound do grow uncurable;\nFor, being green, there is great hope of help.","sourceLine":"3.1.284","speaker":"Post","id":"2henryvi:3.1:56"},{"kind":"speech","text":"A breach that craves a quick expedient stop!\nWhat counsel give you in this weighty cause?","sourceLine":"3.1.290","speaker":"CARDINAL","id":"2henryvi:3.1:57"},{"kind":"speech","text":"That Somerset be sent as regent thither:\n'Tis meet that lucky ruler be employ'd;\nWitness the fortune he hath had in France.","sourceLine":"3.1.292","speaker":"YORK","id":"2henryvi:3.1:58"},{"kind":"speech","text":"If York, with all his far-fet policy,\nHad been the regent there instead of me,\nHe never would have stay'd in France so long.","sourceLine":"3.1.295","speaker":"SOMERSET","id":"2henryvi:3.1:59"},{"kind":"speech","text":"No, not to lose it all, as thou hast done:\nI rather would have lost my life betimes\nThan bring a burthen of dishonour home\nBy staying there so long till all were lost.\nShow me one scar character'd on thy skin:\nMen's flesh preserved so whole do seldom win.","sourceLine":"3.1.298","speaker":"YORK","id":"2henryvi:3.1:60"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Nay, then, this spark will prove a raging fire,\nIf wind and fuel be brought to feed it with:\nNo more, good York; sweet Somerset, be still:\nThy fortune, York, hadst thou been regent there,\nMight happily have proved far worse than his.","sourceLine":"3.1.304","speaker":"QUEEN MARGARET","id":"2henryvi:3.1:61"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What, worse than nought? nay, then, a shame take all!","sourceLine":"3.1.309","speaker":"YORK","id":"2henryvi:3.1:62"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And, in the number, thee that wishest shame!","sourceLine":"3.1.310","speaker":"SOMERSET","id":"2henryvi:3.1:63"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My Lord of York, try what your fortune is.\nThe uncivil kerns of Ireland are in arms\nAnd temper clay with blood of Englishmen:\nTo Ireland will you lead a band of men,\nCollected choicely, from each county some,\nAnd try your hap against the Irishmen?","sourceLine":"3.1.311","speaker":"CARDINAL","id":"2henryvi:3.1:64"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I will, my lord, so please his majesty.","sourceLine":"3.1.317","speaker":"YORK","id":"2henryvi:3.1:65"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Why, our authority is his consent,\nAnd what we do establish he confirms:\nThen, noble York, take thou this task in hand.","sourceLine":"3.1.318","speaker":"SUFFOLK","id":"2henryvi:3.1:66"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I am content: provide me soldiers, lords,\nWhiles I take order for mine own affairs.","sourceLine":"3.1.321","speaker":"YORK","id":"2henryvi:3.1:67"},{"kind":"speech","text":"A charge, Lord York, that I will see perform'd.\nBut now return we to the false Duke Humphrey.","sourceLine":"3.1.323","speaker":"SUFFOLK","id":"2henryvi:3.1:68"},{"kind":"speech","text":"No more of him; for I will deal with him\nThat henceforth he shall trouble us no more.\nAnd so break off; the day is almost spent:\nLord Suffolk, you and I must talk of that event.","sourceLine":"3.1.325","speaker":"CARDINAL","id":"2henryvi:3.1:69"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My Lord of Suffolk, within fourteen days\nAt Bristol I expect my soldiers;\nFor there I'll ship them all for Ireland.","sourceLine":"3.1.329","speaker":"YORK","id":"2henryvi:3.1:70"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I'll see it truly done, my Lord of York.","sourceLine":"3.1.332","speaker":"SUFFOLK","id":"2henryvi:3.1:71"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt all but YORK","id":"2henryvi:3.1:72"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Now, York, or never, steel thy fearful thoughts,\nAnd change misdoubt to resolution:\nBe that thou hopest to be, or what thou art\nResign to death; it is not worth the enjoying:\nLet pale-faced fear keep with the mean-born man,\nAnd find no harbour in a royal heart.\nFaster than spring-time showers comes thought\non thought,\nAnd not a thought but thinks on dignity.\nMy brain more busy than the labouring spider\nWeaves tedious snares to trap mine enemies.\nWell, nobles, well, 'tis politicly done,\nTo send me packing with an host of men:\nI fear me you but warm the starved snake,\nWho, cherish'd in your breasts, will sting\nyour hearts.\n'Twas men I lack'd and you will give them me:\nI take it kindly; and yet be well assured\nYou put sharp weapons in a madman's hands.\nWhiles I in Ireland nourish a mighty band,\nI will stir up in England some black storm\nShall blow ten thousand souls to heaven or hell;\nAnd this fell tempest shall not cease to rage\nUntil the golden circuit on my head,\nLike to the glorious sun's transparent beams,\nDo calm the fury of this mad-bred flaw.\nAnd, for a minister of my intent,\nI have seduced a headstrong Kentishman,\nJohn Cade of Ashford,\nTo make commotion, as full well he can,\nUnder the title of John Mortimer.\nIn Ireland have I seen this stubborn Cade\nOppose himself against a troop of kerns,\nAnd fought so long, till that his thighs with darts\nWere almost like a sharp-quill'd porpentine;\nAnd, in the end being rescued, I have seen\nHim caper upright like a wild Morisco,\nShaking the bloody darts as he his bells.\nFull often, like a shag-hair'd crafty kern,\nHath he conversed with the enemy,\nAnd undiscover'd come to me again\nAnd given me notice of their villanies.\nThis devil here shall be my substitute;\nFor that John Mortimer, which now is dead,\nIn face, in gait, in speech, he doth resemble:\nBy this I shall perceive the commons' mind,\nHow they affect the house and claim of York.\nSay he be taken, rack'd and tortured,\nI know no pain they can inflict upon him\nWill make him say I moved him to those arms.\nSay that he thrive, as 'tis great like he will,\nWhy, then from Ireland come I with my strength\nAnd reap the harvest which that rascal sow'd;\nFor Humphrey being dead, as he shall be,\nAnd Henry put apart, the next for me.","sourceLine":"3.1.333","speaker":"YORK","id":"2henryvi:3.1:73"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit","id":"2henryvi:3.1:74"}],"scene":1},{"id":"3.2","label":"Act 3 · SCENE II. Bury St. Edmund's. A room of state.","act":3,"sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/2henryvi/full.html","blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter certain Murderers, hastily","id":"2henryvi:3.2:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Run to my Lord of Suffolk; let him know\nWe have dispatch'd the duke, as he commanded.","sourceLine":"3.2.1","speaker":"First Murderer","id":"2henryvi:3.2:2"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O that it were to do! What have we done?\nDidst ever hear a man so penitent?","sourceLine":"3.2.3","speaker":"Second Murderer","id":"2henryvi:3.2:3"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter SUFFOLK","id":"2henryvi:3.2:4"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Here comes my lord.","sourceLine":"3.2.5","speaker":"First Murder","id":"2henryvi:3.2:5"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Now, sirs, have you dispatch'd this thing?","sourceLine":"3.2.6","speaker":"SUFFOLK","id":"2henryvi:3.2:6"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ay, my good lord, he's dead.","sourceLine":"3.2.7","speaker":"First Murderer","id":"2henryvi:3.2:7"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Why, that's well said. Go, get you to my house;\nI will reward you for this venturous deed.\nThe king and all the peers are here at hand.\nHave you laid fair the bed? Is all things well,\nAccording as I gave directions?","sourceLine":"3.2.8","speaker":"SUFFOLK","id":"2henryvi:3.2:8"},{"kind":"speech","text":"'Tis, my good lord.","sourceLine":"3.2.13","speaker":"First Murderer","id":"2henryvi:3.2:9"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Away! be gone.","sourceLine":"3.2.14","speaker":"SUFFOLK","id":"2henryvi:3.2:10"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt Murderers","id":"2henryvi:3.2:11"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Sound trumpets. Enter KING HENRY VI, QUEEN MARGARET, CARDINAL, SOMERSET, with Attendants","id":"2henryvi:3.2:12"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Go, call our uncle to our presence straight;\nSay we intend to try his grace to-day.\nIf he be guilty, as 'tis published.","sourceLine":"3.2.15","speaker":"KING HENRY VI","id":"2henryvi:3.2:13"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I'll call him presently, my noble lord.","sourceLine":"3.2.18","speaker":"SUFFOLK","id":"2henryvi:3.2:14"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit","id":"2henryvi:3.2:15"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Lords, take your places; and, I pray you all,\nProceed no straiter 'gainst our uncle Gloucester\nThan from true evidence of good esteem\nHe be approved in practise culpable.","sourceLine":"3.2.19","speaker":"KING HENRY VI","id":"2henryvi:3.2:16"},{"kind":"speech","text":"God forbid any malice should prevail,\nThat faultless may condemn a nobleman!\nPray God he may acquit him of suspicion!","sourceLine":"3.2.23","speaker":"QUEEN MARGARET","id":"2henryvi:3.2:17"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I thank thee, Meg; these words content me much.","sourceLine":"3.2.26","speaker":"KING HENRY VI","id":"2henryvi:3.2:18"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Re-enter SUFFOLK","id":"2henryvi:3.2:19"},{"kind":"speech","text":"How now! why look'st thou pale? why tremblest thou?\nWhere is our uncle? what's the matter, Suffolk?","sourceLine":"3.2.27","speaker":"KING HENRY VI","id":"2henryvi:3.2:20"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Dead in his bed, my lord; Gloucester is dead.","sourceLine":"3.2.29","speaker":"SUFFOLK","id":"2henryvi:3.2:21"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Marry, God forfend!","sourceLine":"3.2.30","speaker":"QUEEN MARGARET","id":"2henryvi:3.2:22"},{"kind":"speech","text":"God's secret judgment: I did dream to-night\nThe duke was dumb and could not speak a word.","sourceLine":"3.2.31","speaker":"CARDINAL","id":"2henryvi:3.2:23"},{"kind":"stage","text":"KING HENRY VI swoons","id":"2henryvi:3.2:24"},{"kind":"speech","text":"How fares my lord? Help, lords! the king is dead.","sourceLine":"3.2.33","speaker":"QUEEN MARGARET","id":"2henryvi:3.2:25"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Rear up his body; wring him by the nose.","sourceLine":"3.2.34","speaker":"SOMERSET","id":"2henryvi:3.2:26"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Run, go, help, help! O Henry, ope thine eyes!","sourceLine":"3.2.35","speaker":"QUEEN MARGARET","id":"2henryvi:3.2:27"},{"kind":"speech","text":"He doth revive again: madam, be patient.","sourceLine":"3.2.36","speaker":"SUFFOLK","id":"2henryvi:3.2:28"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O heavenly God!","sourceLine":"3.2.37","speaker":"KING HENRY VI","id":"2henryvi:3.2:29"},{"kind":"speech","text":"How fares my gracious lord?","sourceLine":"3.2.38","speaker":"QUEEN MARGARET","id":"2henryvi:3.2:30"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Comfort, my sovereign! gracious Henry, comfort!","sourceLine":"3.2.39","speaker":"SUFFOLK","id":"2henryvi:3.2:31"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What, doth my Lord of Suffolk comfort me?\nCame he right now to sing a raven's note,\nWhose dismal tune bereft my vital powers;\nAnd thinks he that the chirping of a wren,\nBy crying comfort from a hollow breast,\nCan chase away the first-conceived sound?\nHide not thy poison with such sugar'd words;\nLay not thy hands on me; forbear, I say;\nTheir touch affrights me as a serpent's sting.\nThou baleful messenger, out of my sight!\nUpon thy eye-balls murderous tyranny\nSits in grim majesty, to fright the world.\nLook not upon me, for thine eyes are wounding:\nYet do not go away: come, basilisk,\nAnd kill the innocent gazer with thy sight;\nFor in the shade of death I shall find joy;\nIn life but double death, now Gloucester's dead.","sourceLine":"3.2.40","speaker":"KING HENRY VI","id":"2henryvi:3.2:32"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Why do you rate my Lord of Suffolk thus?\nAlthough the duke was enemy to him,\nYet he most Christian-like laments his death:\nAnd for myself, foe as he was to me,\nMight liquid tears or heart-offending groans\nOr blood-consuming sighs recall his life,\nI would be blind with weeping, sick with groans,\nLook pale as primrose with blood-drinking sighs,\nAnd all to have the noble duke alive.\nWhat know I how the world may deem of me?\nFor it is known we were but hollow friends:\nIt may be judged I made the duke away;\nSo shall my name with slander's tongue be wounded,\nAnd princes' courts be fill'd with my reproach.\nThis get I by his death: ay me, unhappy!\nTo be a queen, and crown'd with infamy!","sourceLine":"3.2.57","speaker":"QUEEN MARGARET","id":"2henryvi:3.2:33"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ah, woe is me for Gloucester, wretched man!","sourceLine":"3.2.73","speaker":"KING HENRY VI","id":"2henryvi:3.2:34"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Be woe for me, more wretched than he is.\nWhat, dost thou turn away and hide thy face?\nI am no loathsome leper; look on me.\nWhat! art thou, like the adder, waxen deaf?\nBe poisonous too and kill thy forlorn queen.\nIs all thy comfort shut in Gloucester's tomb?\nWhy, then, dame Margaret was ne'er thy joy.\nErect his statue and worship it,\nAnd make my image but an alehouse sign.\nWas I for this nigh wreck'd upon the sea\nAnd twice by awkward wind from England's bank\nDrove back again unto my native clime?\nWhat boded this, but well forewarning wind\nDid seem to say 'Seek not a scorpion's nest,\nNor set no footing on this unkind shore'?\nWhat did I then, but cursed the gentle gusts\nAnd he that loosed them forth their brazen caves:\nAnd bid them blow towards England's blessed shore,\nOr turn our stern upon a dreadful rock\nYet AEolus would not be a murderer,\nBut left that hateful office unto thee:\nThe pretty-vaulting sea refused to drown me,\nKnowing that thou wouldst have me drown'd on shore,\nWith tears as salt as sea, through thy unkindness:\nThe splitting rocks cower'd in the sinking sands\nAnd would not dash me with their ragged sides,\nBecause thy flinty heart, more hard than they,\nMight in thy palace perish Margaret.\nAs far as I could ken thy chalky cliffs,\nWhen from thy shore the tempest beat us back,\nI stood upon the hatches in the storm,\nAnd when the dusky sky began to rob\nMy earnest-gaping sight of thy land's view,\nI took a costly jewel from my neck,\nA heart it was, bound in with diamonds,\nAnd threw it towards thy land: the sea received it,\nAnd so I wish'd thy body might my heart:\nAnd even with this I lost fair England's view\nAnd bid mine eyes be packing with my heart\nAnd call'd them blind and dusky spectacles,\nFor losing ken of Albion's wished coast.\nHow often have I tempted Suffolk's tongue,\nThe agent of thy foul inconstancy,\nTo sit and witch me, as Ascanius did\nWhen he to madding Dido would unfold\nHis father's acts commenced in burning Troy!\nAm I not witch'd like her? or thou not false like him?\nAy me, I can no more! die, Margaret!\nFor Henry weeps that thou dost live so long.","sourceLine":"3.2.74","speaker":"QUEEN MARGARET","id":"2henryvi:3.2:35"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Noise within. Enter WARWICK, SALISBURY, and many Commons","id":"2henryvi:3.2:36"},{"kind":"speech","text":"It is reported, mighty sovereign,\nThat good Duke Humphrey traitorously is murder'd\nBy Suffolk and the Cardinal Beaufort's means.\nThe commons, like an angry hive of bees\nThat want their leader, scatter up and down\nAnd care not who they sting in his revenge.\nMyself have calm'd their spleenful mutiny,\nUntil they hear the order of his death.","sourceLine":"3.2.123","speaker":"WARWICK","id":"2henryvi:3.2:37"},{"kind":"speech","text":"That he is dead, good Warwick, 'tis too true;\nBut how he died God knows, not Henry:\nEnter his chamber, view his breathless corpse,\nAnd comment then upon his sudden death.","sourceLine":"3.2.131","speaker":"KING HENRY VI","id":"2henryvi:3.2:38"},{"kind":"speech","text":"That shall I do, my liege. Stay, Salisbury,\nWith the rude multitude till I return.","sourceLine":"3.2.135","speaker":"WARWICK","id":"2henryvi:3.2:39"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit","id":"2henryvi:3.2:40"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O Thou that judgest all things, stay my thoughts,\nMy thoughts, that labour to persuade my soul\nSome violent hands were laid on Humphrey's life!\nIf my suspect be false, forgive me, God,\nFor judgment only doth belong to thee.\nFain would I go to chafe his paly lips\nWith twenty thousand kisses, and to drain\nUpon his face an ocean of salt tears,\nTo tell my love unto his dumb deaf trunk,\nAnd with my fingers feel his hand unfeeling:\nBut all in vain are these mean obsequies;\nAnd to survey his dead and earthly image,\nWhat were it but to make my sorrow greater?","sourceLine":"3.2.137","speaker":"KING HENRY VI","id":"2henryvi:3.2:41"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Re-enter WARWICK and others, bearing GLOUCESTER'S body on a bed","id":"2henryvi:3.2:42"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Come hither, gracious sovereign, view this body.","sourceLine":"3.2.150","speaker":"WARWICK","id":"2henryvi:3.2:43"},{"kind":"speech","text":"That is to see how deep my grave is made;\nFor with his soul fled all my worldly solace,\nFor seeing him I see my life in death.","sourceLine":"3.2.151","speaker":"KING HENRY VI","id":"2henryvi:3.2:44"},{"kind":"speech","text":"As surely as my soul intends to live\nWith that dread King that took our state upon him\nTo free us from his father's wrathful curse,\nI do believe that violent hands were laid\nUpon the life of this thrice-famed duke.","sourceLine":"3.2.154","speaker":"WARWICK","id":"2henryvi:3.2:45"},{"kind":"speech","text":"A dreadful oath, sworn with a solemn tongue!\nWhat instance gives Lord Warwick for his vow?","sourceLine":"3.2.159","speaker":"SUFFOLK","id":"2henryvi:3.2:46"},{"kind":"speech","text":"See how the blood is settled in his face.\nOft have I seen a timely-parted ghost,\nOf ashy semblance, meagre, pale and bloodless,\nBeing all descended to the labouring heart;\nWho, in the conflict that it holds with death,\nAttracts the same for aidance 'gainst the enemy;\nWhich with the heart there cools and ne'er returneth\nTo blush and beautify the cheek again.\nBut see, his face is black and full of blood,\nHis eye-balls further out than when he lived,\nStaring full ghastly like a strangled man;\nHis hair uprear'd, his nostrils stretched with struggling;\nHis hands abroad display'd, as one that grasp'd\nAnd tugg'd for life and was by strength subdued:\nLook, on the sheets his hair you see, is sticking;\nHis well-proportion'd beard made rough and rugged,\nLike to the summer's corn by tempest lodged.\nIt cannot be but he was murder'd here;\nThe least of all these signs were probable.","sourceLine":"3.2.161","speaker":"WARWICK","id":"2henryvi:3.2:47"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Why, Warwick, who should do the duke to death?\nMyself and Beaufort had him in protection;\nAnd we, I hope, sir, are no murderers.","sourceLine":"3.2.180","speaker":"SUFFOLK","id":"2henryvi:3.2:48"},{"kind":"speech","text":"But both of you were vow'd Duke Humphrey's foes,\nAnd you, forsooth, had the good duke to keep:\n'Tis like you would not feast him like a friend;\nAnd 'tis well seen he found an enemy.","sourceLine":"3.2.183","speaker":"WARWICK","id":"2henryvi:3.2:49"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Then you, belike, suspect these noblemen\nAs guilty of Duke Humphrey's timeless death.","sourceLine":"3.2.187","speaker":"QUEEN MARGARET","id":"2henryvi:3.2:50"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Who finds the heifer dead and bleeding fresh\nAnd sees fast by a butcher with an axe,\nBut will suspect 'twas he that made the slaughter?\nWho finds the partridge in the puttock's nest,\nBut may imagine how the bird was dead,\nAlthough the kite soar with unbloodied beak?\nEven so suspicious is this tragedy.","sourceLine":"3.2.189","speaker":"WARWICK","id":"2henryvi:3.2:51"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Are you the butcher, Suffolk? Where's your knife?\nIs Beaufort term'd a kite? Where are his talons?","sourceLine":"3.2.196","speaker":"QUEEN MARGARET","id":"2henryvi:3.2:52"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I wear no knife to slaughter sleeping men;\nBut here's a vengeful sword, rusted with ease,\nThat shall be scoured in his rancorous heart\nThat slanders me with murder's crimson badge.\nSay, if thou darest, proud Lord of Warwick-shire,\nThat I am faulty in Duke Humphrey's death.","sourceLine":"3.2.198","speaker":"SUFFOLK","id":"2henryvi:3.2:53"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt CARDINAL, SOMERSET, and others","id":"2henryvi:3.2:54"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What dares not Warwick, if false Suffolk dare him?","sourceLine":"3.2.204","speaker":"WARWICK","id":"2henryvi:3.2:55"},{"kind":"speech","text":"He dares not calm his contumelious spirit\nNor cease to be an arrogant controller,\nThough Suffolk dare him twenty thousand times.","sourceLine":"3.2.205","speaker":"QUEEN MARGARET","id":"2henryvi:3.2:56"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Madam, be still; with reverence may I say;\nFor every word you speak in his behalf\nIs slander to your royal dignity.","sourceLine":"3.2.208","speaker":"WARWICK","id":"2henryvi:3.2:57"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Blunt-witted lord, ignoble in demeanor!\nIf ever lady wrong'd her lord so much,\nThy mother took into her blameful bed\nSome stern untutor'd churl, and noble stock\nWas graft with crab-tree slip; whose fruit thou art,\nAnd never of the Nevils' noble race.","sourceLine":"3.2.211","speaker":"SUFFOLK","id":"2henryvi:3.2:58"},{"kind":"speech","text":"But that the guilt of murder bucklers thee\nAnd I should rob the deathsman of his fee,\nQuitting thee thereby of ten thousand shames,\nAnd that my sovereign's presence makes me mild,\nI would, false murderous coward, on thy knee\nMake thee beg pardon for thy passed speech,\nAnd say it was thy mother that thou meant'st\nThat thou thyself was born in bastardy;\nAnd after all this fearful homage done,\nGive thee thy hire and send thy soul to hell,\nPernicious blood-sucker of sleeping men!","sourceLine":"3.2.217","speaker":"WARWICK","id":"2henryvi:3.2:59"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Thou shall be waking well I shed thy blood,\nIf from this presence thou darest go with me.","sourceLine":"3.2.228","speaker":"SUFFOLK","id":"2henryvi:3.2:60"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Away even now, or I will drag thee hence:\nUnworthy though thou art, I'll cope with thee\nAnd do some service to Duke Humphrey's ghost.","sourceLine":"3.2.230","speaker":"WARWICK","id":"2henryvi:3.2:61"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt SUFFOLK and WARWICK","id":"2henryvi:3.2:62"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What stronger breastplate than a heart untainted!\nThrice is he armed that hath his quarrel just,\nAnd he but naked, though lock'd up in steel\nWhose conscience with injustice is corrupted.","sourceLine":"3.2.233","speaker":"KING HENRY VI","id":"2henryvi:3.2:63"},{"kind":"stage","text":"A noise within","id":"2henryvi:3.2:64"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What noise is this?","sourceLine":"3.2.237","speaker":"QUEEN MARGARET","id":"2henryvi:3.2:65"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Re-enter SUFFOLK and WARWICK, with their weapons drawn","id":"2henryvi:3.2:66"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Why, how now, lords! your wrathful weapons drawn\nHere in our presence! dare you be so bold?\nWhy, what tumultuous clamour have we here?","sourceLine":"3.2.238","speaker":"KING HENRY VI","id":"2henryvi:3.2:67"},{"kind":"speech","text":"The traitorous Warwick with the men of Bury\nSet all upon me, mighty sovereign.","sourceLine":"3.2.241","speaker":"SUFFOLK","id":"2henryvi:3.2:68"},{"kind":"speech","text":"[To the Commons, entering] Sirs, stand apart;\nthe king shall know your mind.\nDread lord, the commons send you word by me,\nUnless Lord Suffolk straight be done to death,\nOr banished fair England's territories,\nThey will by violence tear him from your palace\nAnd torture him with grievous lingering death.\nThey say, by him the good Duke Humphrey died;\nThey say, in him they fear your highness' death;\nAnd mere instinct of love and loyalty,\nFree from a stubborn opposite intent,\nAs being thought to contradict your liking,\nMakes them thus forward in his banishment.\nThey say, in care of your most royal person,\nThat if your highness should intend to sleep\nAnd charge that no man should disturb your rest\nIn pain of your dislike or pain of death,\nYet, notwithstanding such a strait edict,\nWere there a serpent seen, with forked tongue,\nThat slily glided towards your majesty,\nIt were but necessary you were waked,\nLest, being suffer'd in that harmful slumber,\nThe mortal worm might make the sleep eternal;\nAnd therefore do they cry, though you forbid,\nThat they will guard you, whether you will or no,\nFrom such fell serpents as false Suffolk is,\nWith whose envenomed and fatal sting,\nYour loving uncle, twenty times his worth,\nThey say, is shamefully bereft of life.","sourceLine":"3.2.243","speaker":"SALISBURY","id":"2henryvi:3.2:69"},{"kind":"speech","text":"[Within] An answer from the king, my\nLord of Salisbury!","sourceLine":"3.2.272","speaker":"Commons","id":"2henryvi:3.2:70"},{"kind":"speech","text":"'Tis like the commons, rude unpolish'd hinds,\nCould send such message to their sovereign:\nBut you, my lord, were glad to be employ'd,\nTo show how quaint an orator you are:\nBut all the honour Salisbury hath won\nIs, that he was the lord ambassador\nSent from a sort of tinkers to the king.","sourceLine":"3.2.274","speaker":"SUFFOLK","id":"2henryvi:3.2:71"},{"kind":"speech","text":"[Within] An answer from the king, or we will all break in!","sourceLine":"3.2.281","speaker":"Commons","id":"2henryvi:3.2:72"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Go, Salisbury, and tell them all from me.\nI thank them for their tender loving care;\nAnd had I not been cited so by them,\nYet did I purpose as they do entreat;\nFor, sure, my thoughts do hourly prophesy\nMischance unto my state by Suffolk's means:\nAnd therefore, by His majesty I swear,\nWhose far unworthy deputy I am,\nHe shall not breathe infection in this air\nBut three days longer, on the pain of death.","sourceLine":"3.2.282","speaker":"KING HENRY VI","id":"2henryvi:3.2:73"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit SALISBURY","id":"2henryvi:3.2:74"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O Henry, let me plead for gentle Suffolk!","sourceLine":"3.2.292","speaker":"QUEEN MARGARET","id":"2henryvi:3.2:75"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ungentle queen, to call him gentle Suffolk!\nNo more, I say: if thou dost plead for him,\nThou wilt but add increase unto my wrath.\nHad I but said, I would have kept my word,\nBut when I swear, it is irrevocable.\nIf, after three days' space, thou here be'st found\nOn any ground that I am ruler of,\nThe world shall not be ransom for thy life.\nCome, Warwick, come, good Warwick, go with me;\nI have great matters to impart to thee.","sourceLine":"3.2.293","speaker":"KING HENRY VI","id":"2henryvi:3.2:76"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt all but QUEEN MARGARET and SUFFOLK","id":"2henryvi:3.2:77"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Mischance and sorrow go along with you!\nHeart's discontent and sour affliction\nBe playfellows to keep you company!\nThere's two of you; the devil make a third!\nAnd threefold vengeance tend upon your steps!","sourceLine":"3.2.303","speaker":"QUEEN MARGARET","id":"2henryvi:3.2:78"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Cease, gentle queen, these execrations,\nAnd let thy Suffolk take his heavy leave.","sourceLine":"3.2.308","speaker":"SUFFOLK","id":"2henryvi:3.2:79"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Fie, coward woman and soft-hearted wretch!\nHast thou not spirit to curse thine enemy?","sourceLine":"3.2.310","speaker":"QUEEN MARGARET","id":"2henryvi:3.2:80"},{"kind":"speech","text":"A plague upon them! wherefore should I curse them?\nWould curses kill, as doth the mandrake's groan,\nI would invent as bitter-searching terms,\nAs curst, as harsh and horrible to hear,\nDeliver'd strongly through my fixed teeth,\nWith full as many signs of deadly hate,\nAs lean-faced Envy in her loathsome cave:\nMy tongue should stumble in mine earnest words;\nMine eyes should sparkle like the beaten flint;\nMine hair be fixed on end, as one distract;\nAy, every joint should seem to curse and ban:\nAnd even now my burthen'd heart would break,\nShould I not curse them. Poison be their drink!\nGall, worse than gall, the daintiest that they taste!\nTheir sweetest shade a grove of cypress trees!\nTheir chiefest prospect murdering basilisks!\nTheir softest touch as smart as lizards' sting!\nTheir music frightful as the serpent's hiss,\nAnd boding screech-owls make the concert full!\nAll the foul terrors in dark-seated hell--","sourceLine":"3.2.312","speaker":"SUFFOLK","id":"2henryvi:3.2:81"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Enough, sweet Suffolk; thou torment'st thyself;\nAnd these dread curses, like the sun 'gainst glass,\nOr like an overcharged gun, recoil,\nAnd turn the force of them upon thyself.","sourceLine":"3.2.332","speaker":"QUEEN MARGARET","id":"2henryvi:3.2:82"},{"kind":"speech","text":"You bade me ban, and will you bid me leave?\nNow, by the ground that I am banish'd from,\nWell could I curse away a winter's night,\nThough standing naked on a mountain top,\nWhere biting cold would never let grass grow,\nAnd think it but a minute spent in sport.","sourceLine":"3.2.336","speaker":"SUFFOLK","id":"2henryvi:3.2:83"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O, let me entreat thee cease. Give me thy hand,\nThat I may dew it with my mournful tears;\nNor let the rain of heaven wet this place,\nTo wash away my woful monuments.\nO, could this kiss be printed in thy hand,\nThat thou mightst think upon these by the seal,\nThrough whom a thousand sighs are breathed for thee!\nSo, get thee gone, that I may know my grief;\n'Tis but surmised whiles thou art standing by,\nAs one that surfeits thinking on a want.\nI will repeal thee, or, be well assured,\nAdventure to be banished myself:\nAnd banished I am, if but from thee.\nGo; speak not to me; even now be gone.\nO, go not yet! Even thus two friends condemn'd\nEmbrace and kiss and take ten thousand leaves,\nLoather a hundred times to part than die.\nYet now farewell; and farewell life with thee!","sourceLine":"3.2.342","speaker":"QUEEN MARGARET","id":"2henryvi:3.2:84"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Thus is poor Suffolk ten times banished;\nOnce by the king, and three times thrice by thee.\n'Tis not the land I care for, wert thou thence;\nA wilderness is populous enough,\nSo Suffolk had thy heavenly company:\nFor where thou art, there is the world itself,\nWith every several pleasure in the world,\nAnd where thou art not, desolation.\nI can no more: live thou to joy thy life;\nMyself no joy in nought but that thou livest.","sourceLine":"3.2.360","speaker":"SUFFOLK","id":"2henryvi:3.2:85"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter VAUX","id":"2henryvi:3.2:86"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Wither goes Vaux so fast? what news, I prithee?","sourceLine":"3.2.370","speaker":"QUEEN MARGARET","id":"2henryvi:3.2:87"},{"kind":"speech","text":"To signify unto his majesty\nThat Cardinal Beaufort is at point of death;\nFor suddenly a grievous sickness took him,\nThat makes him gasp and stare and catch the air,\nBlaspheming God and cursing men on earth.\nSometimes he talks as if Duke Humphrey's ghost\nWere by his side; sometime he calls the king,\nAnd whispers to his pillow, as to him,\nThe secrets of his overcharged soul;\nAnd I am sent to tell his majesty\nThat even now he cries aloud for him.","sourceLine":"3.2.371","speaker":"VAUX","id":"2henryvi:3.2:88"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Go tell this heavy message to the king.","sourceLine":"3.2.382","speaker":"QUEEN MARGARET","id":"2henryvi:3.2:89"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit VAUX","id":"2henryvi:3.2:90"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ay me! what is this world! what news are these!\nBut wherefore grieve I at an hour's poor loss,\nOmitting Suffolk's exile, my soul's treasure?\nWhy only, Suffolk, mourn I not for thee,\nAnd with the southern clouds contend in tears,\nTheirs for the earth's increase, mine for my sorrows?\nNow get thee hence: the king, thou know'st, is coming;\nIf thou be found by me, thou art but dead.","sourceLine":"3.2.383","speaker":"QUEEN MARGARET","id":"2henryvi:3.2:91"},{"kind":"speech","text":"If I depart from thee, I cannot live;\nAnd in thy sight to die, what were it else\nBut like a pleasant slumber in thy lap?\nHere could I breathe my soul into the air,\nAs mild and gentle as the cradle-babe\nDying with mother's dug between its lips:\nWhere, from thy sight, I should be raging mad,\nAnd cry out for thee to close up mine eyes,\nTo have thee with thy lips to stop my mouth;\nSo shouldst thou either turn my flying soul,\nOr I should breathe it so into thy body,\nAnd then it lived in sweet Elysium.\nTo die by thee were but to die in jest;\nFrom thee to die were torture more than death:\nO, let me stay, befall what may befall!","sourceLine":"3.2.391","speaker":"SUFFOLK","id":"2henryvi:3.2:92"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Away! though parting be a fretful corrosive,\nIt is applied to a deathful wound.\nTo France, sweet Suffolk: let me hear from thee;\nFor wheresoe'er thou art in this world's globe,\nI'll have an Iris that shall find thee out.","sourceLine":"3.2.406","speaker":"QUEEN MARGARET","id":"2henryvi:3.2:93"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I go.","sourceLine":"3.2.411","speaker":"SUFFOLK","id":"2henryvi:3.2:94"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And take my heart with thee.","sourceLine":"3.2.412","speaker":"QUEEN MARGARET","id":"2henryvi:3.2:95"},{"kind":"speech","text":"A jewel, lock'd into the wofull'st cask\nThat ever did contain a thing of worth.\nEven as a splitted bark, so sunder we\nThis way fall I to death.","sourceLine":"3.2.413","speaker":"SUFFOLK","id":"2henryvi:3.2:96"},{"kind":"speech","text":"This way for me.","sourceLine":"3.2.417","speaker":"QUEEN MARGARET","id":"2henryvi:3.2:97"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt severally","id":"2henryvi:3.2:98"}],"scene":2},{"id":"3.3","label":"Act 3 · SCENE III. A bedchamber.","act":3,"sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/2henryvi/full.html","blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter the KING, SALISBURY, WARWICK, to the CARDINAL in bed","id":"2henryvi:3.3:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"How fares my lord? speak, Beaufort, to\nthy sovereign.","sourceLine":"3.3.1","speaker":"KING HENRY VI","id":"2henryvi:3.3:2"},{"kind":"speech","text":"If thou be'st death, I'll give thee England's treasure,\nEnough to purchase such another island,\nSo thou wilt let me live, and feel no pain.","sourceLine":"3.3.3","speaker":"CARDINAL","id":"2henryvi:3.3:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ah, what a sign it is of evil life,\nWhere death's approach is seen so terrible!","sourceLine":"3.3.6","speaker":"KING HENRY VI","id":"2henryvi:3.3:4"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Beaufort, it is thy sovereign speaks to thee.","sourceLine":"3.3.8","speaker":"WARWICK","id":"2henryvi:3.3:5"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Bring me unto my trial when you will.\nDied he not in his bed? where should he die?\nCan I make men live, whether they will or no?\nO, torture me no more! I will confess.\nAlive again? then show me where he is:\nI'll give a thousand pound to look upon him.\nHe hath no eyes, the dust hath blinded them.\nComb down his hair; look, look! it stands upright,\nLike lime-twigs set to catch my winged soul.\nGive me some drink; and bid the apothecary\nBring the strong poison that I bought of him.","sourceLine":"3.3.9","speaker":"CARDINAL","id":"2henryvi:3.3:6"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O thou eternal Mover of the heavens.\nLook with a gentle eye upon this wretch!\nO, beat away the busy meddling fiend\nThat lays strong siege unto this wretch's soul.\nAnd from his bosom purge this black despair!","sourceLine":"3.3.20","speaker":"KING HENRY VI","id":"2henryvi:3.3:7"},{"kind":"speech","text":"See, how the pangs of death do make him grin!","sourceLine":"3.3.25","speaker":"WARWICK","id":"2henryvi:3.3:8"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Disturb him not; let him pass peaceably.","sourceLine":"3.3.26","speaker":"SALISBURY","id":"2henryvi:3.3:9"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Peace to his soul, if God's good pleasure be!\nLord cardinal, if thou think'st on heaven's bliss,\nHold up thy hand, make signal of thy hope.\nHe dies, and makes no sign. O God, forgive him!","sourceLine":"3.3.27","speaker":"KING HENRY VI","id":"2henryvi:3.3:10"},{"kind":"speech","text":"So bad a death argues a monstrous life.","sourceLine":"3.3.31","speaker":"WARWICK","id":"2henryvi:3.3:11"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Forbear to judge, for we are sinners all.\nClose up his eyes and draw the curtain close;\nAnd let us all to meditation.","sourceLine":"3.3.32","speaker":"KING HENRY VI","id":"2henryvi:3.3:12"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt","id":"2henryvi:3.3:13"}],"scene":3},{"id":"4.1","label":"Act 4 · SCENE I. The coast of Kent.","act":4,"sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/2henryvi/full.html","blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Alarum. Fight at sea. Ordnance goes off. Enter a Captain, a Master, a Master's-mate, WALTER WHITMORE, and others; with them SUFFOLK, and others, prisoners","id":"2henryvi:4.1:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"The gaudy, blabbing and remorseful day\nIs crept into the bosom of the sea;\nAnd now loud-howling wolves arouse the jades\nThat drag the tragic melancholy night;\nWho, with their drowsy, slow and flagging wings,\nClip dead men's graves and from their misty jaws\nBreathe foul contagious darkness in the air.\nTherefore bring forth the soldiers of our prize;\nFor, whilst our pinnace anchors in the Downs,\nHere shall they make their ransom on the sand,\nOr with their blood stain this discolour'd shore.\nMaster, this prisoner freely give I thee;\nAnd thou that art his mate, make boot of this;\nThe other, Walter Whitmore, is thy share.","sourceLine":"4.1.1","speaker":"Captain","id":"2henryvi:4.1:2"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What is my ransom, master? let me know.","sourceLine":"4.1.15","speaker":"First Gentleman","id":"2henryvi:4.1:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"A thousand crowns, or else lay down your head.\nMaster's-Mate And so much shall you give, or off goes yours.","sourceLine":"4.1.16","speaker":"Master","id":"2henryvi:4.1:4"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What, think you much to pay two thousand crowns,\nAnd bear the name and port of gentlemen?\nCut both the villains' throats; for die you shall:\nThe lives of those which we have lost in fight\nBe counterpoised with such a petty sum!","sourceLine":"4.1.18","speaker":"Captain","id":"2henryvi:4.1:5"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I'll give it, sir; and therefore spare my life.","sourceLine":"4.1.23","speaker":"First Gentleman","id":"2henryvi:4.1:6"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And so will I and write home for it straight.","sourceLine":"4.1.24","speaker":"Second Gentleman","id":"2henryvi:4.1:7"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I lost mine eye in laying the prize aboard,\nAnd therefore to revenge it, shalt thou die;","sourceLine":"4.1.25","speaker":"WHITMORE","id":"2henryvi:4.1:8"},{"kind":"stage","text":"To SUFFOLK","id":"2henryvi:4.1:9"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And so should these, if I might have my will.","sourceLine":"4.1.27","speaker":"WHITMORE","id":"2henryvi:4.1:10"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Be not so rash; take ransom, let him live.","sourceLine":"4.1.28","speaker":"Captain","id":"2henryvi:4.1:11"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Look on my George; I am a gentleman:\nRate me at what thou wilt, thou shalt be paid.","sourceLine":"4.1.29","speaker":"SUFFOLK","id":"2henryvi:4.1:12"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And so am I; my name is Walter Whitmore.\nHow now! why start'st thou? what, doth\ndeath affright?","sourceLine":"4.1.31","speaker":"WHITMORE","id":"2henryvi:4.1:13"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Thy name affrights me, in whose sound is death.\nA cunning man did calculate my birth\nAnd told me that by water I should die:\nYet let not this make thee be bloody-minded;\nThy name is Gaultier, being rightly sounded.","sourceLine":"4.1.34","speaker":"SUFFOLK","id":"2henryvi:4.1:14"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Gaultier or Walter, which it is, I care not:\nNever yet did base dishonour blur our name,\nBut with our sword we wiped away the blot;\nTherefore, when merchant-like I sell revenge,\nBroke be my sword, my arms torn and defaced,\nAnd I proclaim'd a coward through the world!","sourceLine":"4.1.39","speaker":"WHITMORE","id":"2henryvi:4.1:15"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Stay, Whitmore; for thy prisoner is a prince,\nThe Duke of Suffolk, William de la Pole.","sourceLine":"4.1.45","speaker":"SUFFOLK","id":"2henryvi:4.1:16"},{"kind":"speech","text":"The Duke of Suffolk muffled up in rags!","sourceLine":"4.1.47","speaker":"WHITMORE","id":"2henryvi:4.1:17"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ay, but these rags are no part of the duke:\nJove sometimes went disguised, and why not I?","sourceLine":"4.1.48","speaker":"SUFFOLK","id":"2henryvi:4.1:18"},{"kind":"speech","text":"But Jove was never slain, as thou shalt be.","sourceLine":"4.1.50","speaker":"Captain","id":"2henryvi:4.1:19"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Obscure and lowly swain, King Henry's blood,\nThe honourable blood of Lancaster,\nMust not be shed by such a jaded groom.\nHast thou not kiss'd thy hand and held my stirrup?\nBare-headed plodded by my foot-cloth mule\nAnd thought thee happy when I shook my head?\nHow often hast thou waited at my cup,\nFed from my trencher, kneel'd down at the board.\nWhen I have feasted with Queen Margaret?\nRemember it and let it make thee crest-fall'n,\nAy, and allay this thy abortive pride;\nHow in our voiding lobby hast thou stood\nAnd duly waited for my coming forth?\nThis hand of mine hath writ in thy behalf,\nAnd therefore shall it charm thy riotous tongue.","sourceLine":"4.1.51","speaker":"SUFFOLK","id":"2henryvi:4.1:20"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Speak, captain, shall I stab the forlorn swain?","sourceLine":"4.1.66","speaker":"WHITMORE","id":"2henryvi:4.1:21"},{"kind":"speech","text":"First let my words stab him, as he hath me.","sourceLine":"4.1.67","speaker":"Captain","id":"2henryvi:4.1:22"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Base slave, thy words are blunt and so art thou.","sourceLine":"4.1.68","speaker":"SUFFOLK","id":"2henryvi:4.1:23"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Convey him hence and on our longboat's side\nStrike off his head.","sourceLine":"4.1.69","speaker":"Captain","id":"2henryvi:4.1:24"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Thou darest not, for thy own.","sourceLine":"4.1.71","speaker":"SUFFOLK","id":"2henryvi:4.1:25"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Yes, Pole.","sourceLine":"4.1.72","speaker":"Captain","id":"2henryvi:4.1:26"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Pole!","sourceLine":"4.1.73","speaker":"SUFFOLK","id":"2henryvi:4.1:27"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Pool! Sir Pool! lord!\nAy, kennel, puddle, sink; whose filth and dirt\nTroubles the silver spring where England drinks.\nNow will I dam up this thy yawning mouth\nFor swallowing the treasure of the realm:\nThy lips that kiss'd the queen shall sweep the ground;\nAnd thou that smiledst at good Duke Humphrey's death,\nAgainst the senseless winds shalt grin in vain,\nWho in contempt shall hiss at thee again:\nAnd wedded be thou to the hags of hell,\nFor daring to affy a mighty lord\nUnto the daughter of a worthless king,\nHaving neither subject, wealth, nor diadem.\nBy devilish policy art thou grown great,\nAnd, like ambitious Sylla, overgorged\nWith gobbets of thy mother's bleeding heart.\nBy thee Anjou and Maine were sold to France,\nThe false revolting Normans thorough thee\nDisdain to call us lord, and Picardy\nHath slain their governors, surprised our forts,\nAnd sent the ragged soldiers wounded home.\nThe princely Warwick, and the Nevils all,\nWhose dreadful swords were never drawn in vain,\nAs hating thee, are rising up in arms:\nAnd now the house of York, thrust from the crown\nBy shameful murder of a guiltless king\nAnd lofty proud encroaching tyranny,\nBurns with revenging fire; whose hopeful colours\nAdvance our half-faced sun, striving to shine,\nUnder the which is writ 'Invitis nubibus.'\nThe commons here in Kent are up in arms:\nAnd, to conclude, reproach and beggary\nIs crept into the palace of our king.\nAnd all by thee. Away! convey him hence.","sourceLine":"4.1.74","speaker":"Captain","id":"2henryvi:4.1:28"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O that I were a god, to shoot forth thunder\nUpon these paltry, servile, abject drudges!\nSmall things make base men proud: this villain here,\nBeing captain of a pinnace, threatens more\nThan Bargulus the strong Illyrian pirate.\nDrones suck not eagles' blood but rob beehives:\nIt is impossible that I should die\nBy such a lowly vassal as thyself.\nThy words move rage and not remorse in me:\nI go of message from the queen to France;\nI charge thee waft me safely cross the Channel.","sourceLine":"4.1.108","speaker":"SUFFOLK","id":"2henryvi:4.1:29"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Walter,--","sourceLine":"4.1.119","speaker":"Captain","id":"2henryvi:4.1:30"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Come, Suffolk, I must waft thee to thy death.","sourceLine":"4.1.120","speaker":"WHITMORE","id":"2henryvi:4.1:31"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Gelidus timor occupat artus it is thee I fear.","sourceLine":"4.1.121","speaker":"SUFFOLK","id":"2henryvi:4.1:32"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Thou shalt have cause to fear before I leave thee.\nWhat, are ye daunted now? now will ye stoop?","sourceLine":"4.1.122","speaker":"WHITMORE","id":"2henryvi:4.1:33"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My gracious lord, entreat him, speak him fair.","sourceLine":"4.1.124","speaker":"First Gentleman","id":"2henryvi:4.1:34"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Suffolk's imperial tongue is stern and rough,\nUsed to command, untaught to plead for favour.\nFar be it we should honour such as these\nWith humble suit: no, rather let my head\nStoop to the block than these knees bow to any\nSave to the God of heaven and to my king;\nAnd sooner dance upon a bloody pole\nThan stand uncover'd to the vulgar groom.\nTrue nobility is exempt from fear:\nMore can I bear than you dare execute.","sourceLine":"4.1.125","speaker":"SUFFOLK","id":"2henryvi:4.1:35"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Hale him away, and let him talk no more.","sourceLine":"4.1.135","speaker":"Captain","id":"2henryvi:4.1:36"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Come, soldiers, show what cruelty ye can,\nThat this my death may never be forgot!\nGreat men oft die by vile bezonians:\nA Roman sworder and banditto slave\nMurder'd sweet Tully; Brutus' bastard hand\nStabb'd Julius Caesar; savage islanders\nPompey the Great; and Suffolk dies by pirates.","sourceLine":"4.1.136","speaker":"SUFFOLK","id":"2henryvi:4.1:37"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt Whitmore and others with Suffolk","id":"2henryvi:4.1:38"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And as for these whose ransom we have set,\nIt is our pleasure one of them depart;\nTherefore come you with us and let him go.","sourceLine":"4.1.143","speaker":"Captain","id":"2henryvi:4.1:39"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt all but the First Gentleman","id":"2henryvi:4.1:40"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Re-enter WHITMORE with SUFFOLK's body","id":"2henryvi:4.1:41"},{"kind":"speech","text":"There let his head and lifeless body lie,\nUntil the queen his mistress bury it.","sourceLine":"4.1.146","speaker":"WHITMORE","id":"2henryvi:4.1:42"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit","id":"2henryvi:4.1:43"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O barbarous and bloody spectacle!\nHis body will I bear unto the king:\nIf he revenge it not, yet will his friends;\nSo will the queen, that living held him dear.","sourceLine":"4.1.148","speaker":"First Gentleman","id":"2henryvi:4.1:44"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit with the body","id":"2henryvi:4.1:45"}],"scene":1},{"id":"4.2","label":"Act 4 · SCENE II. Blackheath.","act":4,"sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/2henryvi/full.html","blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter GEORGE BEVIS and JOHN HOLLAND","id":"2henryvi:4.2:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Come, and get thee a sword, though made of a lath;\nthey have been up these two days.","sourceLine":"4.2.1","speaker":"BEVIS","id":"2henryvi:4.2:2"},{"kind":"speech","text":"They have the more need to sleep now, then.","sourceLine":"4.2.3","speaker":"HOLLAND","id":"2henryvi:4.2:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I tell thee, Jack Cade the clothier means to dress\nthe commonwealth, and turn it, and set a new nap upon it.","sourceLine":"4.2.4","speaker":"BEVIS","id":"2henryvi:4.2:4"},{"kind":"speech","text":"So he had need, for 'tis threadbare. Well, I say it\nwas never merry world in England since gentlemen came up.","sourceLine":"4.2.6","speaker":"HOLLAND","id":"2henryvi:4.2:5"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O miserable age! virtue is not regarded in handicrafts-men.","sourceLine":"4.2.8","speaker":"BEVIS","id":"2henryvi:4.2:6"},{"kind":"speech","text":"The nobility think scorn to go in leather aprons.","sourceLine":"4.2.9","speaker":"HOLLAND","id":"2henryvi:4.2:7"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Nay, more, the king's council are no good workmen.","sourceLine":"4.2.10","speaker":"BEVIS","id":"2henryvi:4.2:8"},{"kind":"speech","text":"True; and yet it is said, labour in thy vocation;\nwhich is as much to say as, let the magistrates be\nlabouring men; and therefore should we be\nmagistrates.","sourceLine":"4.2.11","speaker":"HOLLAND","id":"2henryvi:4.2:9"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Thou hast hit it; for there's no better sign of a\nbrave mind than a hard hand.","sourceLine":"4.2.15","speaker":"BEVIS","id":"2henryvi:4.2:10"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I see them! I see them! there's Best's son, the\ntanner of Wingham,--","sourceLine":"4.2.17","speaker":"HOLLAND","id":"2henryvi:4.2:11"},{"kind":"speech","text":"He shall have the skin of our enemies, to make\ndog's-leather of.","sourceLine":"4.2.19","speaker":"BEVIS","id":"2henryvi:4.2:12"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And Dick the Butcher,--","sourceLine":"4.2.21","speaker":"HOLLAND","id":"2henryvi:4.2:13"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Then is sin struck down like an ox, and iniquity's\nthroat cut like a calf.","sourceLine":"4.2.22","speaker":"BEVIS","id":"2henryvi:4.2:14"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And Smith the weaver,--","sourceLine":"4.2.24","speaker":"HOLLAND","id":"2henryvi:4.2:15"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Argo, their thread of life is spun.","sourceLine":"4.2.25","speaker":"BEVIS","id":"2henryvi:4.2:16"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Come, come, let's fall in with them.","sourceLine":"4.2.26","speaker":"HOLLAND","id":"2henryvi:4.2:17"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Drum. Enter CADE, DICK the Butcher, SMITH the Weaver, and a Sawyer, with infinite numbers","id":"2henryvi:4.2:18"},{"kind":"speech","text":"We John Cade, so termed of our supposed father,--","sourceLine":"4.2.27","speaker":"CADE","id":"2henryvi:4.2:19"},{"kind":"speech","text":"[Aside] Or rather, of stealing a cade of herrings.","sourceLine":"4.2.28","speaker":"DICK","id":"2henryvi:4.2:20"},{"kind":"speech","text":"For our enemies shall fall before us, inspired with\nthe spirit of putting down kings and princes,\n--Command silence.","sourceLine":"4.2.29","speaker":"CADE","id":"2henryvi:4.2:21"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Silence!","sourceLine":"4.2.32","speaker":"DICK","id":"2henryvi:4.2:22"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My father was a Mortimer,--","sourceLine":"4.2.33","speaker":"CADE","id":"2henryvi:4.2:23"},{"kind":"speech","text":"[Aside] He was an honest man, and a good\nbricklayer.","sourceLine":"4.2.34","speaker":"DICK","id":"2henryvi:4.2:24"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My mother a Plantagenet,--","sourceLine":"4.2.36","speaker":"CADE","id":"2henryvi:4.2:25"},{"kind":"speech","text":"[Aside] I knew her well; she was a midwife.","sourceLine":"4.2.37","speaker":"DICK","id":"2henryvi:4.2:26"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My wife descended of the Lacies,--","sourceLine":"4.2.38","speaker":"CADE","id":"2henryvi:4.2:27"},{"kind":"speech","text":"[Aside] She was, indeed, a pedler's daughter, and\nsold many laces.","sourceLine":"4.2.39","speaker":"DICK","id":"2henryvi:4.2:28"},{"kind":"speech","text":"[Aside] But now of late, notable to travel with her\nfurred pack, she washes bucks here at home.","sourceLine":"4.2.41","speaker":"SMITH","id":"2henryvi:4.2:29"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Therefore am I of an honourable house.","sourceLine":"4.2.43","speaker":"CADE","id":"2henryvi:4.2:30"},{"kind":"speech","text":"[Aside] Ay, by my faith, the field is honourable;\nand there was he borne, under a hedge, for his\nfather had never a house but the cage.","sourceLine":"4.2.44","speaker":"DICK","id":"2henryvi:4.2:31"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Valiant I am.","sourceLine":"4.2.47","speaker":"CADE","id":"2henryvi:4.2:32"},{"kind":"speech","text":"[Aside] A' must needs; for beggary is valiant.","sourceLine":"4.2.48","speaker":"SMITH","id":"2henryvi:4.2:33"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I am able to endure much.","sourceLine":"4.2.49","speaker":"CADE","id":"2henryvi:4.2:34"},{"kind":"speech","text":"[Aside] No question of that; for I have seen him\nwhipped three market-days together.","sourceLine":"4.2.50","speaker":"DICK","id":"2henryvi:4.2:35"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I fear neither sword nor fire.","sourceLine":"4.2.52","speaker":"CADE","id":"2henryvi:4.2:36"},{"kind":"speech","text":"[Aside] He need not fear the sword; for his coat is of proof.","sourceLine":"4.2.53","speaker":"SMITH","id":"2henryvi:4.2:37"},{"kind":"speech","text":"[Aside] But methinks he should stand in fear of\nfire, being burnt i' the hand for stealing of sheep.","sourceLine":"4.2.54","speaker":"DICK","id":"2henryvi:4.2:38"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Be brave, then; for your captain is brave, and vows\nreformation. There shall be in England seven\nhalfpenny loaves sold for a penny: the three-hooped\npot; shall have ten hoops and I will make it felony\nto drink small beer: all the realm shall be in\ncommon; and in Cheapside shall my palfrey go to\ngrass: and when I am king, as king I will be,--","sourceLine":"4.2.56","speaker":"CADE","id":"2henryvi:4.2:39"},{"kind":"speech","text":"God save your majesty!","sourceLine":"4.2.63","speaker":"ALL","id":"2henryvi:4.2:40"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I thank you, good people: there shall be no money;\nall shall eat and drink on my score; and I will\napparel them all in one livery, that they may agree\nlike brothers and worship me their lord.","sourceLine":"4.2.64","speaker":"CADE","id":"2henryvi:4.2:41"},{"kind":"speech","text":"The first thing we do, let's kill all the lawyers.","sourceLine":"4.2.68","speaker":"DICK","id":"2henryvi:4.2:42"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Nay, that I mean to do. Is not this a lamentable\nthing, that of the skin of an innocent lamb should\nbe made parchment? that parchment, being scribbled\no'er, should undo a man? Some say the bee stings:\nbut I say, 'tis the bee's wax; for I did but seal\nonce to a thing, and I was never mine own man\nsince. How now! who's there?","sourceLine":"4.2.69","speaker":"CADE","id":"2henryvi:4.2:43"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter some, bringing forward the Clerk of Chatham","id":"2henryvi:4.2:44"},{"kind":"speech","text":"The clerk of Chatham: he can write and read and\ncast accompt.","sourceLine":"4.2.76","speaker":"SMITH","id":"2henryvi:4.2:45"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O monstrous!","sourceLine":"4.2.78","speaker":"CADE","id":"2henryvi:4.2:46"},{"kind":"speech","text":"We took him setting of boys' copies.","sourceLine":"4.2.79","speaker":"SMITH","id":"2henryvi:4.2:47"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Here's a villain!","sourceLine":"4.2.80","speaker":"CADE","id":"2henryvi:4.2:48"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Has a book in his pocket with red letters in't.","sourceLine":"4.2.81","speaker":"SMITH","id":"2henryvi:4.2:49"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Nay, then, he is a conjurer.","sourceLine":"4.2.82","speaker":"CADE","id":"2henryvi:4.2:50"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Nay, he can make obligations, and write court-hand.","sourceLine":"4.2.83","speaker":"DICK","id":"2henryvi:4.2:51"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I am sorry for't: the man is a proper man, of mine\nhonour; unless I find him guilty, he shall not die.\nCome hither, sirrah, I must examine thee: what is thy name?","sourceLine":"4.2.84","speaker":"CADE","id":"2henryvi:4.2:52"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Emmanuel.","sourceLine":"4.2.87","speaker":"Clerk","id":"2henryvi:4.2:53"},{"kind":"speech","text":"They use to write it on the top of letters: 'twill\ngo hard with you.","sourceLine":"4.2.88","speaker":"DICK","id":"2henryvi:4.2:54"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Let me alone. Dost thou use to write thy name? or\nhast thou a mark to thyself, like an honest\nplain-dealing man?","sourceLine":"4.2.90","speaker":"CADE","id":"2henryvi:4.2:55"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Sir, I thank God, I have been so well brought up\nthat I can write my name.","sourceLine":"4.2.93","speaker":"CLERK","id":"2henryvi:4.2:56"},{"kind":"speech","text":"He hath confessed: away with him! he's a villain\nand a traitor.","sourceLine":"4.2.95","speaker":"ALL","id":"2henryvi:4.2:57"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Away with him, I say! hang him with his pen and\nink-horn about his neck.","sourceLine":"4.2.97","speaker":"CADE","id":"2henryvi:4.2:58"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit one with the Clerk","id":"2henryvi:4.2:59"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter MICHAEL","id":"2henryvi:4.2:60"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Where's our general?","sourceLine":"4.2.99","speaker":"MICHAEL","id":"2henryvi:4.2:61"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Here I am, thou particular fellow.","sourceLine":"4.2.100","speaker":"CADE","id":"2henryvi:4.2:62"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Fly, fly, fly! Sir Humphrey Stafford and his\nbrother are hard by, with the king's forces.","sourceLine":"4.2.101","speaker":"MICHAEL","id":"2henryvi:4.2:63"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Stand, villain, stand, or I'll fell thee down. He\nshall be encountered with a man as good as himself:\nhe is but a knight, is a'?","sourceLine":"4.2.103","speaker":"CADE","id":"2henryvi:4.2:64"},{"kind":"speech","text":"No.","sourceLine":"4.2.106","speaker":"MICHAEL","id":"2henryvi:4.2:65"},{"kind":"speech","text":"To equal him, I will make myself a knight presently.","sourceLine":"4.2.107","speaker":"CADE","id":"2henryvi:4.2:66"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Kneels","id":"2henryvi:4.2:67"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Rise up Sir John Mortimer.","sourceLine":"4.2.108","speaker":"CADE","id":"2henryvi:4.2:68"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Rises","id":"2henryvi:4.2:69"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Now have at him!","sourceLine":"4.2.109","speaker":"CADE","id":"2henryvi:4.2:70"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter SIR HUMPHREY and WILLIAM STAFFORD, with drum and soldiers","id":"2henryvi:4.2:71"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Rebellious hinds, the filth and scum of Kent,\nMark'd for the gallows, lay your weapons down;\nHome to your cottages, forsake this groom:\nThe king is merciful, if you revolt.","sourceLine":"4.2.110","speaker":"SIR HUMPHREY","id":"2henryvi:4.2:72"},{"kind":"speech","text":"But angry, wrathful, and inclined to blood,\nIf you go forward; therefore yield, or die.","sourceLine":"4.2.114","speaker":"WILLIAM STAFFORD","id":"2henryvi:4.2:73"},{"kind":"speech","text":"As for these silken-coated slaves, I pass not:\nIt is to you, good people, that I speak,\nOver whom, in time to come, I hope to reign;\nFor I am rightful heir unto the crown.","sourceLine":"4.2.116","speaker":"CADE","id":"2henryvi:4.2:74"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Villain, thy father was a plasterer;\nAnd thou thyself a shearman, art thou not?","sourceLine":"4.2.120","speaker":"SIR HUMPHREY","id":"2henryvi:4.2:75"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And Adam was a gardener.","sourceLine":"4.2.122","speaker":"CADE","id":"2henryvi:4.2:76"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And what of that?","sourceLine":"4.2.123","speaker":"WILLIAM STAFFORD","id":"2henryvi:4.2:77"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Marry, this: Edmund Mortimer, Earl of March.\nMarried the Duke of Clarence' daughter, did he not?","sourceLine":"4.2.124","speaker":"CADE","id":"2henryvi:4.2:78"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ay, sir.","sourceLine":"4.2.126","speaker":"SIR HUMPHREY","id":"2henryvi:4.2:79"},{"kind":"speech","text":"By her he had two children at one birth.","sourceLine":"4.2.127","speaker":"CADE","id":"2henryvi:4.2:80"},{"kind":"speech","text":"That's false.","sourceLine":"4.2.128","speaker":"WILLIAM STAFFORD","id":"2henryvi:4.2:81"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ay, there's the question; but I say, 'tis true:\nThe elder of them, being put to nurse,\nWas by a beggar-woman stolen away;\nAnd, ignorant of his birth and parentage,\nBecame a bricklayer when he came to age:\nHis son am I; deny it, if you can.","sourceLine":"4.2.129","speaker":"CADE","id":"2henryvi:4.2:82"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Nay, 'tis too true; therefore he shall be king.","sourceLine":"4.2.135","speaker":"DICK","id":"2henryvi:4.2:83"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Sir, he made a chimney in my father's house, and\nthe bricks are alive at this day to testify it;\ntherefore deny it not.","sourceLine":"4.2.136","speaker":"SMITH","id":"2henryvi:4.2:84"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And will you credit this base drudge's words,\nThat speaks he knows not what?","sourceLine":"4.2.139","speaker":"SIR HUMPHREY","id":"2henryvi:4.2:85"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ay, marry, will we; therefore get ye gone.","sourceLine":"4.2.141","speaker":"ALL","id":"2henryvi:4.2:86"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Jack Cade, the Duke of York hath taught you this.","sourceLine":"4.2.142","speaker":"WILLIAM STAFFORD","id":"2henryvi:4.2:87"},{"kind":"speech","text":"[Aside] He lies, for I invented it myself.\nGo to, sirrah, tell the king from me, that, for his\nfather's sake, Henry the Fifth, in whose time boys\nwent to span-counter for French crowns, I am content\nhe shall reign; but I'll be protector over him.","sourceLine":"4.2.143","speaker":"CADE","id":"2henryvi:4.2:88"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And furthermore, well have the Lord Say's head for\nselling the dukedom of Maine.","sourceLine":"4.2.148","speaker":"DICK","id":"2henryvi:4.2:89"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And good reason; for thereby is England mained, and\nfain to go with a staff, but that my puissance holds\nit up. Fellow kings, I tell you that that Lord Say\nhath gelded the commonwealth, and made it an eunuch:\nand more than that, he can speak French; and\ntherefore he is a traitor.","sourceLine":"4.2.150","speaker":"CADE","id":"2henryvi:4.2:90"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O gross and miserable ignorance!","sourceLine":"4.2.156","speaker":"SIR HUMPHREY","id":"2henryvi:4.2:91"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Nay, answer, if you can: the Frenchmen are our\nenemies; go to, then, I ask but this: can he that\nspeaks with the tongue of an enemy be a good\ncounsellor, or no?","sourceLine":"4.2.157","speaker":"CADE","id":"2henryvi:4.2:92"},{"kind":"speech","text":"No, no; and therefore we'll have his head.","sourceLine":"4.2.161","speaker":"ALL","id":"2henryvi:4.2:93"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Well, seeing gentle words will not prevail,\nAssail them with the army of the king.","sourceLine":"4.2.162","speaker":"WILLIAM STAFFORD","id":"2henryvi:4.2:94"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Herald, away; and throughout every town\nProclaim them traitors that are up with Cade;\nThat those which fly before the battle ends\nMay, even in their wives' and children's sight,\nBe hang'd up for example at their doors:\nAnd you that be the king's friends, follow me.","sourceLine":"4.2.164","speaker":"SIR HUMPHREY","id":"2henryvi:4.2:95"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt WILLIAM STAFFORD and SIR HUMPHREY, and soldiers","id":"2henryvi:4.2:96"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And you that love the commons, follow me.\nNow show yourselves men; 'tis for liberty.\nWe will not leave one lord, one gentleman:\nSpare none but such as go in clouted shoon;\nFor they are thrifty honest men, and such\nAs would, but that they dare not, take our parts.","sourceLine":"4.2.170","speaker":"CADE","id":"2henryvi:4.2:97"},{"kind":"speech","text":"They are all in order and march toward us.","sourceLine":"4.2.176","speaker":"DICK","id":"2henryvi:4.2:98"},{"kind":"speech","text":"But then are we in order when we are most\nout of order. Come, march forward.","sourceLine":"4.2.177","speaker":"CADE","id":"2henryvi:4.2:99"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt","id":"2henryvi:4.2:100"}],"scene":2},{"id":"4.3","label":"Act 4 · SCENE III. Another part of Blackheath.","act":4,"sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/2henryvi/full.html","blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Alarums to the fight, wherein SIR HUMPHREY and WILLIAM STAFFORD are slain. Enter CADE and the rest","id":"2henryvi:4.3:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Where's Dick, the butcher of Ashford?","sourceLine":"4.3.1","speaker":"CADE","id":"2henryvi:4.3:2"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Here, sir.","sourceLine":"4.3.2","speaker":"DICK","id":"2henryvi:4.3:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"They fell before thee like sheep and oxen, and thou\nbehavedst thyself as if thou hadst been in thine own\nslaughter-house: therefore thus will I reward thee,\nthe Lent shall be as long again as it is; and thou\nshalt have a licence to kill for a hundred lacking\none.","sourceLine":"4.3.3","speaker":"CADE","id":"2henryvi:4.3:4"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I desire no more.","sourceLine":"4.3.9","speaker":"DICK","id":"2henryvi:4.3:5"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And, to speak truth, thou deservest no less. This\nmonument of the victory will I bear;","sourceLine":"4.3.10","speaker":"CADE","id":"2henryvi:4.3:6"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Putting on SIR HUMPHREY'S brigandine","id":"2henryvi:4.3:7"},{"kind":"speech","text":"and the bodies shall be dragged at my horse' heels\ntill I do come to London, where we will have the\nmayor's sword borne before us.","sourceLine":"4.3.12","speaker":"CADE","id":"2henryvi:4.3:8"},{"kind":"speech","text":"If we mean to thrive and do good, break open the\ngaols and let out the prisoners.","sourceLine":"4.3.15","speaker":"DICK","id":"2henryvi:4.3:9"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Fear not that, I warrant thee. Come, let's march\ntowards London.","sourceLine":"4.3.17","speaker":"CADE","id":"2henryvi:4.3:10"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt","id":"2henryvi:4.3:11"}],"scene":3},{"id":"4.4","label":"Act 4 · SCENE IV. London. The palace.","act":4,"sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/2henryvi/full.html","blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter KING HENRY VI with a supplication, and the QUEEN with SUFFOLK'S head, BUCKINGHAM and Lord SAY","id":"2henryvi:4.4:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Oft have I heard that grief softens the mind,\nAnd makes it fearful and degenerate;\nThink therefore on revenge and cease to weep.\nBut who can cease to weep and look on this?\nHere may his head lie on my throbbing breast:\nBut where's the body that I should embrace?","sourceLine":"4.4.1","speaker":"QUEEN MARGARET","id":"2henryvi:4.4:2"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What answer makes your grace to the rebels'\nsupplication?","sourceLine":"4.4.7","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"2henryvi:4.4:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I'll send some holy bishop to entreat;\nFor God forbid so many simple souls\nShould perish by the sword! And I myself,\nRather than bloody war shall cut them short,\nWill parley with Jack Cade their general:\nBut stay, I'll read it over once again.","sourceLine":"4.4.9","speaker":"KING HENRY VI","id":"2henryvi:4.4:4"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ah, barbarous villains! hath this lovely face\nRuled, like a wandering planet, over me,\nAnd could it not enforce them to relent,\nThat were unworthy to behold the same?","sourceLine":"4.4.15","speaker":"QUEEN MARGARET","id":"2henryvi:4.4:5"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Lord Say, Jack Cade hath sworn to have thy head.","sourceLine":"4.4.19","speaker":"KING HENRY VI","id":"2henryvi:4.4:6"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ay, but I hope your highness shall have his.","sourceLine":"4.4.20","speaker":"SAY","id":"2henryvi:4.4:7"},{"kind":"speech","text":"How now, madam!\nStill lamenting and mourning for Suffolk's death?\nI fear me, love, if that I had been dead,\nThou wouldst not have mourn'd so much for me.","sourceLine":"4.4.21","speaker":"KING HENRY VI","id":"2henryvi:4.4:8"},{"kind":"speech","text":"No, my love, I should not mourn, but die for thee.","sourceLine":"4.4.25","speaker":"QUEEN MARGARET","id":"2henryvi:4.4:9"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter a Messenger","id":"2henryvi:4.4:10"},{"kind":"speech","text":"How now! what news? why comest thou in such haste?","sourceLine":"4.4.26","speaker":"KING HENRY VI","id":"2henryvi:4.4:11"},{"kind":"speech","text":"The rebels are in Southwark; fly, my lord!\nJack Cade proclaims himself Lord Mortimer,\nDescended from the Duke of Clarence' house,\nAnd calls your grace usurper openly\nAnd vows to crown himself in Westminster.\nHis army is a ragged multitude\nOf hinds and peasants, rude and merciless:\nSir Humphrey Stafford and h is brother's death\nHath given them heart and courage to proceed:\nAll scholars, lawyers, courtiers, gentlemen,\nThey call false caterpillars, and intend their death.","sourceLine":"4.4.27","speaker":"Messenger","id":"2henryvi:4.4:12"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O graceless men! they know not what they do.","sourceLine":"4.4.38","speaker":"KING HENRY VI","id":"2henryvi:4.4:13"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My gracious lord, return to Killingworth,\nUntil a power be raised to put them down.","sourceLine":"4.4.39","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"2henryvi:4.4:14"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ah, were the Duke of Suffolk now alive,\nThese Kentish rebels would be soon appeased!","sourceLine":"4.4.41","speaker":"QUEEN MARGARET","id":"2henryvi:4.4:15"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Lord Say, the traitors hate thee;\nTherefore away with us to Killingworth.","sourceLine":"4.4.43","speaker":"KING HENRY VI","id":"2henryvi:4.4:16"},{"kind":"speech","text":"So might your grace's person be in danger.\nThe sight of me is odious in their eyes;\nAnd therefore in this city will I stay\nAnd live alone as secret as I may.","sourceLine":"4.4.45","speaker":"SAY","id":"2henryvi:4.4:17"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter another Messenger","id":"2henryvi:4.4:18"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Jack Cade hath gotten London bridge:\nThe citizens fly and forsake their houses:\nThe rascal people, thirsting after prey,\nJoin with the traitor, and they jointly swear\nTo spoil the city and your royal court.","sourceLine":"4.4.49","speaker":"Messenger","id":"2henryvi:4.4:19"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Then linger not, my lord, away, take horse.","sourceLine":"4.4.54","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"2henryvi:4.4:20"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Come, Margaret; God, our hope, will succor us.","sourceLine":"4.4.55","speaker":"KING HENRY VI","id":"2henryvi:4.4:21"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My hope is gone, now Suffolk is deceased.","sourceLine":"4.4.56","speaker":"QUEEN MARGARET","id":"2henryvi:4.4:22"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Farewell, my lord: trust not the Kentish rebels.","sourceLine":"4.4.57","speaker":"KING HENRY VI","id":"2henryvi:4.4:23"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Trust nobody, for fear you be betray'd.","sourceLine":"4.4.58","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"2henryvi:4.4:24"},{"kind":"speech","text":"The trust I have is in mine innocence,\nAnd therefore am I bold and resolute.","sourceLine":"4.4.59","speaker":"SAY","id":"2henryvi:4.4:25"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt","id":"2henryvi:4.4:26"}],"scene":4},{"id":"4.5","label":"Act 4 · SCENE V. London. The Tower.","act":4,"sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/2henryvi/full.html","blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter SCALES upon the Tower, walking. Then enter two or three Citizens below","id":"2henryvi:4.5:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"How now! is Jack Cade slain?","sourceLine":"4.5.1","speaker":"SCALES","id":"2henryvi:4.5:2"},{"kind":"speech","text":"No, my lord, nor likely to be slain; for they have\nwon the bridge, killing all those that withstand\nthem: the lord mayor craves aid of your honour from\nthe Tower, to defend the city from the rebels.","sourceLine":"4.5.2","speaker":"First Citizen","id":"2henryvi:4.5:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Such aid as I can spare you shall command;\nBut I am troubled here with them myself;\nThe rebels have assay'd to win the Tower.\nBut get you to Smithfield, and gather head,\nAnd thither I will send you Matthew Goffe;\nFight for your king, your country and your lives;\nAnd so, farewell, for I must hence again.","sourceLine":"4.5.6","speaker":"SCALES","id":"2henryvi:4.5:4"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt","id":"2henryvi:4.5:5"}],"scene":5},{"id":"4.6","label":"Act 4 · SCENE VI. London. Cannon Street.","act":4,"sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/2henryvi/full.html","blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter CADE and the rest, and strikes his staff on London-stone","id":"2henryvi:4.6:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Now is Mortimer lord of this city. And here, sitting\nupon London-stone, I charge and command that, of the\ncity's cost, the pissing-conduit run nothing but\nclaret wine this first year of our reign. And now\nhenceforward it shall be treason for any that calls\nme other than Lord Mortimer.","sourceLine":"4.6.1","speaker":"CADE","id":"2henryvi:4.6:2"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter a Soldier, running","id":"2henryvi:4.6:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Jack Cade! Jack Cade!","sourceLine":"4.6.7","speaker":"Soldier","id":"2henryvi:4.6:4"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Knock him down there.","sourceLine":"4.6.8","speaker":"CADE","id":"2henryvi:4.6:5"},{"kind":"stage","text":"They kill him","id":"2henryvi:4.6:6"},{"kind":"speech","text":"If this fellow be wise, he'll never call ye Jack\nCade more: I think he hath a very fair warning.","sourceLine":"4.6.9","speaker":"SMITH","id":"2henryvi:4.6:7"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My lord, there's an army gathered together in\nSmithfield.","sourceLine":"4.6.11","speaker":"DICK","id":"2henryvi:4.6:8"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Come, then, let's go fight with them; but first, go\nand set London bridge on fire; and, if you can, burn\ndown the Tower too. Come, let's away.","sourceLine":"4.6.13","speaker":"CADE","id":"2henryvi:4.6:9"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt","id":"2henryvi:4.6:10"}],"scene":6},{"id":"4.7","label":"Act 4 · SCENE VII. London. Smithfield.","act":4,"sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/2henryvi/full.html","blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Alarums. MATTHEW GOFFE is slain, and all the rest. Then enter CADE, with his company.","id":"2henryvi:4.7:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"So, sirs: now go some and pull down the Savoy;\nothers to the inns of court; down with them all.","sourceLine":"4.7.1","speaker":"CADE","id":"2henryvi:4.7:2"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I have a suit unto your lordship.","sourceLine":"4.7.3","speaker":"DICK","id":"2henryvi:4.7:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Be it a lordship, thou shalt have it for that word.","sourceLine":"4.7.4","speaker":"CADE","id":"2henryvi:4.7:4"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Only that the laws of England may come out of your mouth.","sourceLine":"4.7.5","speaker":"DICK","id":"2henryvi:4.7:5"},{"kind":"speech","text":"[Aside] Mass, 'twill be sore law, then; for he was\nthrust in the mouth with a spear, and 'tis not whole\nyet.","sourceLine":"4.7.6","speaker":"HOLLAND","id":"2henryvi:4.7:6"},{"kind":"speech","text":"[Aside] Nay, John, it will be stinking law for his\nbreath stinks with eating toasted cheese.","sourceLine":"4.7.9","speaker":"SMITH","id":"2henryvi:4.7:7"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I have thought upon it, it shall be so. Away, burn\nall the records of the realm: my mouth shall be\nthe parliament of England.","sourceLine":"4.7.11","speaker":"CADE","id":"2henryvi:4.7:8"},{"kind":"speech","text":"[Aside] Then we are like to have biting statutes,\nunless his teeth be pulled out.","sourceLine":"4.7.14","speaker":"HOLLAND","id":"2henryvi:4.7:9"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And henceforward all things shall be in common.","sourceLine":"4.7.16","speaker":"CADE","id":"2henryvi:4.7:10"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter a Messenger","id":"2henryvi:4.7:11"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My lord, a prize, a prize! here's the Lord Say,\nwhich sold the towns in France; he that made us pay\none and twenty fifteens, and one shilling to the\npound, the last subsidy.","sourceLine":"4.7.17","speaker":"Messenger","id":"2henryvi:4.7:12"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter BEVIS, with Lord SAY","id":"2henryvi:4.7:13"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Well, he shall be beheaded for it ten times. Ah,\nthou say, thou serge, nay, thou buckram lord! now\nart thou within point-blank of our jurisdiction\nregal. What canst thou answer to my majesty for\ngiving up of Normandy unto Mounsieur Basimecu, the\ndauphin of France? Be it known unto thee by these\npresence, even the presence of Lord Mortimer, that I\nam the besom that must sweep the court clean of such\nfilth as thou art. Thou hast most traitorously\ncorrupted the youth of the realm in erecting a\ngrammar school; and whereas, before, our forefathers\nhad no other books but the score and the tally, thou\nhast caused printing to be used, and, contrary to\nthe king, his crown and dignity, thou hast built a\npaper-mill. It will be proved to thy face that thou\nhast men about thee that usually talk of a noun and\na verb, and such abominable words as no Christian\near can endure to hear. Thou hast appointed\njustices of peace, to call poor men before them\nabout matters they were not able to answer.\nMoreover, thou hast put them in prison; and because\nthey could not read, thou hast hanged them; when,\nindeed, only for that cause they have been most\nworthy to live. Thou dost ride in a foot-cloth, dost thou not?","sourceLine":"4.7.21","speaker":"CADE","id":"2henryvi:4.7:14"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What of that?","sourceLine":"4.7.45","speaker":"SAY","id":"2henryvi:4.7:15"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Marry, thou oughtest not to let thy horse wear a\ncloak, when honester men than thou go in their hose\nand doublets.","sourceLine":"4.7.46","speaker":"CADE","id":"2henryvi:4.7:16"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And work in their shirt too; as myself, for example,\nthat am a butcher.","sourceLine":"4.7.49","speaker":"DICK","id":"2henryvi:4.7:17"},{"kind":"speech","text":"You men of Kent,--","sourceLine":"4.7.51","speaker":"SAY","id":"2henryvi:4.7:18"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What say you of Kent?","sourceLine":"4.7.52","speaker":"DICK","id":"2henryvi:4.7:19"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Nothing but this; 'tis 'bona terra, mala gens.'","sourceLine":"4.7.53","speaker":"SAY","id":"2henryvi:4.7:20"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Away with him, away with him! he speaks Latin.","sourceLine":"4.7.54","speaker":"CADE","id":"2henryvi:4.7:21"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Hear me but speak, and bear me where you will.\nKent, in the Commentaries Caesar writ,\nIs term'd the civil'st place of this isle:\nSweet is the country, because full of riches;\nThe people liberal, valiant, active, wealthy;\nWhich makes me hope you are not void of pity.\nI sold not Maine, I lost not Normandy,\nYet, to recover them, would lose my life.\nJustice with favour have I always done;\nPrayers and tears have moved me, gifts could never.\nWhen have I aught exacted at your hands,\nBut to maintain the king, the realm and you?\nLarge gifts have I bestow'd on learned clerks,\nBecause my book preferr'd me to the king,\nAnd seeing ignorance is the curse of God,\nKnowledge the wing wherewith we fly to heaven,\nUnless you be possess'd with devilish spirits,\nYou cannot but forbear to murder me:\nThis tongue hath parley'd unto foreign kings\nFor your behoof,--","sourceLine":"4.7.55","speaker":"SAY","id":"2henryvi:4.7:22"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Tut, when struck'st thou one blow in the field?","sourceLine":"4.7.75","speaker":"CADE","id":"2henryvi:4.7:23"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Great men have reaching hands: oft have I struck\nThose that I never saw and struck them dead.","sourceLine":"4.7.76","speaker":"SAY","id":"2henryvi:4.7:24"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O monstrous coward! what, to come behind folks?","sourceLine":"4.7.78","speaker":"BEVIS","id":"2henryvi:4.7:25"},{"kind":"speech","text":"These cheeks are pale for watching for your good.","sourceLine":"4.7.79","speaker":"SAY","id":"2henryvi:4.7:26"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Give him a box o' the ear and that will make 'em red again.","sourceLine":"4.7.80","speaker":"CADE","id":"2henryvi:4.7:27"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Long sitting to determine poor men's causes\nHath made me full of sickness and diseases.","sourceLine":"4.7.81","speaker":"SAY","id":"2henryvi:4.7:28"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ye shall have a hempen caudle, then, and the help of hatchet.","sourceLine":"4.7.83","speaker":"CADE","id":"2henryvi:4.7:29"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Why dost thou quiver, man?","sourceLine":"4.7.84","speaker":"DICK","id":"2henryvi:4.7:30"},{"kind":"speech","text":"The palsy, and not fear, provokes me.","sourceLine":"4.7.85","speaker":"SAY","id":"2henryvi:4.7:31"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Nay, he nods at us, as who should say, I'll be even\nwith you: I'll see if his head will stand steadier\non a pole, or no. Take him away, and behead him.","sourceLine":"4.7.86","speaker":"CADE","id":"2henryvi:4.7:32"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Tell me wherein have I offended most?\nHave I affected wealth or honour? speak.\nAre my chests fill'd up with extorted gold?\nIs my apparel sumptuous to behold?\nWhom have I injured, that ye seek my death?\nThese hands are free from guiltless bloodshedding,\nThis breast from harbouring foul deceitful thoughts.\nO, let me live!","sourceLine":"4.7.89","speaker":"SAY","id":"2henryvi:4.7:33"},{"kind":"speech","text":"[Aside] I feel remorse in myself with his words;\nbut I'll bridle it: he shall die, an it be but for\npleading so well for his life. Away with him! he\nhas a familiar under his tongue; he speaks not o'\nGod's name. Go, take him away, I say, and strike\noff his head presently; and then break into his\nson-in-law's house, Sir James Cromer, and strike off\nhis head, and bring them both upon two poles hither.","sourceLine":"4.7.97","speaker":"CADE","id":"2henryvi:4.7:34"},{"kind":"speech","text":"It shall be done.","sourceLine":"4.7.105","speaker":"ALL","id":"2henryvi:4.7:35"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ah, countrymen! if when you make your prayers,\nGod should be so obdurate as yourselves,\nHow would it fare with your departed souls?\nAnd therefore yet relent, and save my life.","sourceLine":"4.7.106","speaker":"SAY","id":"2henryvi:4.7:36"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Away with him! and do as I command ye.","sourceLine":"4.7.110","speaker":"CADE","id":"2henryvi:4.7:37"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt some with Lord SAY","id":"2henryvi:4.7:38"},{"kind":"speech","text":"The proudest peer in the realm shall not wear a head\non his shoulders, unless he pay me tribute; there\nshall not a maid be married, but she shall pay to me\nher maidenhead ere they have it: men shall hold of\nme in capite; and we charge and command that their\nwives be as free as heart can wish or tongue can tell.","sourceLine":"4.7.111","speaker":"CADE","id":"2henryvi:4.7:39"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My lord, when shall we go to Cheapside and take up\ncommodities upon our bills?","sourceLine":"4.7.117","speaker":"DICK","id":"2henryvi:4.7:40"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Marry, presently.","sourceLine":"4.7.119","speaker":"CADE","id":"2henryvi:4.7:41"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O, brave!","sourceLine":"4.7.120","speaker":"ALL","id":"2henryvi:4.7:42"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Re-enter one with the heads","id":"2henryvi:4.7:43"},{"kind":"speech","text":"But is not this braver? Let them kiss one another,\nfor they loved well when they were alive. Now part\nthem again, lest they consult about the giving up of\nsome more towns in France. Soldiers, defer the\nspoil of the city until night: for with these borne\nbefore us, instead of maces, will we ride through\nthe streets, and at every corner have them kiss. Away!","sourceLine":"4.7.121","speaker":"CADE","id":"2henryvi:4.7:44"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt","id":"2henryvi:4.7:45"}],"scene":7},{"id":"4.8","label":"Act 4 · SCENE VIII. Southwark.","act":4,"sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/2henryvi/full.html","blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Alarum and retreat. Enter CADE and all his rabblement","id":"2henryvi:4.8:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Up Fish Street! down Saint Magnus' Corner! Kill\nand knock down! throw them into Thames!","sourceLine":"4.8.1","speaker":"CADE","id":"2henryvi:4.8:2"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Sound a parley","id":"2henryvi:4.8:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What noise is this I hear? Dare any be so bold to\nsound retreat or parley, when I command them kill?","sourceLine":"4.8.3","speaker":"CADE","id":"2henryvi:4.8:4"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter BUCKINGHAM and CLIFFORD, attended","id":"2henryvi:4.8:5"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ay, here they be that dare and will disturb thee:\nKnow, Cade, we come ambassadors from the king\nUnto the commons whom thou hast misled;\nAnd here pronounce free pardon to them all\nThat will forsake thee and go home in peace.","sourceLine":"4.8.5","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"2henryvi:4.8:6"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What say ye, countrymen? will ye relent,\nAnd yield to mercy whilst 'tis offer'd you;\nOr let a rebel lead you to your deaths?\nWho loves the king and will embrace his pardon,\nFling up his cap, and say 'God save his majesty!'\nWho hateth him and honours not his father,\nHenry the Fifth, that made all France to quake,\nShake he his weapon at us and pass by.","sourceLine":"4.8.10","speaker":"CLIFFORD","id":"2henryvi:4.8:7"},{"kind":"speech","text":"God save the king! God save the king!","sourceLine":"4.8.18","speaker":"ALL","id":"2henryvi:4.8:8"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What, Buckingham and Clifford, are ye so brave? And\nyou, base peasants, do ye believe him? will you\nneeds be hanged with your pardons about your necks?\nHath my sword therefore broke through London gates,\nthat you should leave me at the White Hart in\nSouthwark? I thought ye would never have given out\nthese arms till you had recovered your ancient\nfreedom: but you are all recreants and dastards,\nand delight to live in slavery to the nobility. Let\nthem break your backs with burthens, take your\nhouses over your heads, ravish your wives and\ndaughters before your faces: for me, I will make\nshift for one; and so, God's curse light upon you\nall!","sourceLine":"4.8.19","speaker":"CADE","id":"2henryvi:4.8:9"},{"kind":"speech","text":"We'll follow Cade, we'll follow Cade!","sourceLine":"4.8.33","speaker":"ALL","id":"2henryvi:4.8:10"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Is Cade the son of Henry the Fifth,\nThat thus you do exclaim you'll go with him?\nWill he conduct you through the heart of France,\nAnd make the meanest of you earls and dukes?\nAlas, he hath no home, no place to fly to;\nNor knows he how to live but by the spoil,\nUnless by robbing of your friends and us.\nWere't not a shame, that whilst you live at jar,\nThe fearful French, whom you late vanquished,\nShould make a start o'er seas and vanquish you?\nMethinks already in this civil broil\nI see them lording it in London streets,\nCrying 'Villiago!' unto all they meet.\nBetter ten thousand base-born Cades miscarry\nThan you should stoop unto a Frenchman's mercy.\nTo France, to France, and get what you have lost;\nSpare England, for it is your native coast;\nHenry hath money, you are strong and manly;\nGod on our side, doubt not of victory.","sourceLine":"4.8.34","speaker":"CLIFFORD","id":"2henryvi:4.8:11"},{"kind":"speech","text":"A Clifford! a Clifford! we'll follow the king and Clifford.","sourceLine":"4.8.53","speaker":"ALL","id":"2henryvi:4.8:12"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Was ever feather so lightly blown to and fro as this\nmultitude? The name of Henry the Fifth hales them\nto an hundred mischiefs, and makes them leave me\ndesolate. I see them lay their heads together to\nsurprise me. My sword make way for me, for here is\nno staying. In despite of the devils and hell, have\nthrough the very middest of you? and heavens and\nhonour be witness, that no want of resolution in me.\nbut only my followers' base and ignominious\ntreasons, makes me betake me to my heels.","sourceLine":"4.8.54","speaker":"CADE","id":"2henryvi:4.8:13"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit","id":"2henryvi:4.8:14"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What, is he fled? Go some, and follow him;\nAnd he that brings his head unto the king\nShall have a thousand crowns for his reward.","sourceLine":"4.8.64","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"2henryvi:4.8:15"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt some of them","id":"2henryvi:4.8:16"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Follow me, soldiers: we'll devise a mean\nTo reconcile you all unto the king.","sourceLine":"4.8.67","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"2henryvi:4.8:17"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt","id":"2henryvi:4.8:18"}],"scene":8},{"id":"4.9","label":"Act 4 · SCENE IX. Kenilworth Castle.","act":4,"sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/2henryvi/full.html","blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Sound Trumpets. Enter KING HENRY VI, QUEEN MARGARET, and SOMERSET, on the terrace","id":"2henryvi:4.9:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Was ever king that joy'd an earthly throne,\nAnd could command no more content than I?\nNo sooner was I crept out of my cradle\nBut I was made a king, at nine months old.\nWas never subject long'd to be a king\nAs I do long and wish to be a subject.","sourceLine":"4.9.1","speaker":"KING HENRY VI","id":"2henryvi:4.9:2"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter BUCKINGHAM and CLIFFORD","id":"2henryvi:4.9:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Health and glad tidings to your majesty!","sourceLine":"4.9.7","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"2henryvi:4.9:4"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Why, Buckingham, is the traitor Cade surprised?\nOr is he but retired to make him strong?","sourceLine":"4.9.8","speaker":"KING HENRY VI","id":"2henryvi:4.9:5"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter below, multitudes, with halters about their necks","id":"2henryvi:4.9:6"},{"kind":"speech","text":"He is fled, my lord, and all his powers do yield;\nAnd humbly thus, with halters on their necks,\nExpect your highness' doom of life or death.","sourceLine":"4.9.10","speaker":"CLIFFORD","id":"2henryvi:4.9:7"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Then, heaven, set ope thy everlasting gates,\nTo entertain my vows of thanks and praise!\nSoldiers, this day have you redeemed your lives,\nAnd show'd how well you love your prince and country:\nContinue still in this so good a mind,\nAnd Henry, though he be infortunate,\nAssure yourselves, will never be unkind:\nAnd so, with thanks and pardon to you all,\nI do dismiss you to your several countries.","sourceLine":"4.9.13","speaker":"KING HENRY VI","id":"2henryvi:4.9:8"},{"kind":"speech","text":"God save the king! God save the king!","sourceLine":"4.9.22","speaker":"ALL","id":"2henryvi:4.9:9"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter a Messenger","id":"2henryvi:4.9:10"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Please it your grace to be advertised\nThe Duke of York is newly come from Ireland,\nAnd with a puissant and a mighty power\nOf gallowglasses and stout kerns\nIs marching hitherward in proud array,\nAnd still proclaimeth, as he comes along,\nHis arms are only to remove from thee\nThe Duke of Somerset, whom he terms traitor.","sourceLine":"4.9.23","speaker":"Messenger","id":"2henryvi:4.9:11"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Thus stands my state, 'twixt Cade and York distress'd.\nLike to a ship that, having 'scaped a tempest,\nIs straightway calm'd and boarded with a pirate:\nBut now is Cade driven back, his men dispersed;\nAnd now is York in arms to second him.\nI pray thee, Buckingham, go and meet him,\nAnd ask him what's the reason of these arms.\nTell him I'll send Duke Edmund to the Tower;\nAnd, Somerset, we'll commit thee thither,\nUntil his army be dismiss'd from him.","sourceLine":"4.9.31","speaker":"KING HENRY VI","id":"2henryvi:4.9:12"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My lord,\nI'll yield myself to prison willingly,\nOr unto death, to do my country good.","sourceLine":"4.9.41","speaker":"SOMERSET","id":"2henryvi:4.9:13"},{"kind":"speech","text":"In any case, be not too rough in terms;\nFor he is fierce and cannot brook hard language.","sourceLine":"4.9.44","speaker":"KING HENRY VI","id":"2henryvi:4.9:14"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I will, my lord; and doubt not so to deal\nAs all things shall redound unto your good.","sourceLine":"4.9.46","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"2henryvi:4.9:15"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Come, wife, let's in, and learn to govern better;\nFor yet may England curse my wretched reign.","sourceLine":"4.9.48","speaker":"KING HENRY VI","id":"2henryvi:4.9:16"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Flourish. Exeunt","id":"2henryvi:4.9:17"}],"scene":9},{"id":"4.10","label":"Act 4 · SCENE X. Kent. IDEN's garden.","act":4,"sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/2henryvi/full.html","blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter CADE","id":"2henryvi:4.10:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Fie on ambition! fie on myself, that have a sword,\nand yet am ready to famish! These five days have I\nhid me in these woods and durst not peep out, for\nall the country is laid for me; but now am I so\nhungry that if I might have a lease of my life for a\nthousand years I could stay no longer. Wherefore,\non a brick wall have I climbed into this garden, to\nsee if I can eat grass, or pick a sallet another\nwhile, which is not amiss to cool a man's stomach\nthis hot weather. And I think this word 'sallet'\nwas born to do me good: for many a time, but for a\nsallet, my brainpan had been cleft with a brown\nbill; and many a time, when I have been dry and\nbravely marching, it hath served me instead of a\nquart pot to drink in; and now the word 'sallet'\nmust serve me to feed on.","sourceLine":"4.10.1","speaker":"CADE","id":"2henryvi:4.10:2"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter IDEN","id":"2henryvi:4.10:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Lord, who would live turmoiled in the court,\nAnd may enjoy such quiet walks as these?\nThis small inheritance my father left me\nContenteth me, and worth a monarchy.\nI seek not to wax great by others' waning,\nOr gather wealth, I care not, with what envy:\nSufficeth that I have maintains my state\nAnd sends the poor well pleased from my gate.","sourceLine":"4.10.17","speaker":"IDEN","id":"2henryvi:4.10:4"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Here's the lord of the soil come to seize me for a\nstray, for entering his fee-simple without leave.\nAh, villain, thou wilt betray me, and get a thousand\ncrowns of the king carrying my head to him: but\nI'll make thee eat iron like an ostrich, and swallow\nmy sword like a great pin, ere thou and I part.","sourceLine":"4.10.25","speaker":"CADE","id":"2henryvi:4.10:5"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Why, rude companion, whatsoe'er thou be,\nI know thee not; why, then, should I betray thee?\nIs't not enough to break into my garden,\nAnd, like a thief, to come to rob my grounds,\nClimbing my walls in spite of me the owner,\nBut thou wilt brave me with these saucy terms?","sourceLine":"4.10.31","speaker":"IDEN","id":"2henryvi:4.10:6"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Brave thee! ay, by the best blood that ever was\nbroached, and beard thee too. Look on me well: I\nhave eat no meat these five days; yet, come thou and\nthy five men, and if I do not leave you all as dead\nas a doornail, I pray God I may never eat grass more.","sourceLine":"4.10.37","speaker":"CADE","id":"2henryvi:4.10:7"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Nay, it shall ne'er be said, while England stands,\nThat Alexander Iden, an esquire of Kent,\nTook odds to combat a poor famish'd man.\nOppose thy steadfast-gazing eyes to mine,\nSee if thou canst outface me with thy looks:\nSet limb to limb, and thou art far the lesser;\nThy hand is but a finger to my fist,\nThy leg a stick compared with this truncheon;\nMy foot shall fight with all the strength thou hast;\nAnd if mine arm be heaved in the air,\nThy grave is digg'd already in the earth.\nAs for words, whose greatness answers words,\nLet this my sword report what speech forbears.","sourceLine":"4.10.42","speaker":"IDEN","id":"2henryvi:4.10:8"},{"kind":"speech","text":"By my valour, the most complete champion that ever I\nheard! Steel, if thou turn the edge, or cut not out\nthe burly-boned clown in chines of beef ere thou\nsleep in thy sheath, I beseech God on my knees thou\nmayst be turned to hobnails.","sourceLine":"4.10.55","speaker":"CADE","id":"2henryvi:4.10:9"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Here they fight. CADE falls","id":"2henryvi:4.10:10"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O, I am slain! famine and no other hath slain me:\nlet ten thousand devils come against me, and give me\nbut the ten meals I have lost, and I'll defy them\nall. Wither, garden; and be henceforth a\nburying-place to all that do dwell in this house,\nbecause the unconquered soul of Cade is fled.","sourceLine":"4.10.60","speaker":"CADE","id":"2henryvi:4.10:11"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Is't Cade that I have slain, that monstrous traitor?\nSword, I will hollow thee for this thy deed,\nAnd hang thee o'er my tomb when I am dead:\nNe'er shall this blood be wiped from thy point;\nBut thou shalt wear it as a herald's coat,\nTo emblaze the honour that thy master got.","sourceLine":"4.10.66","speaker":"IDEN","id":"2henryvi:4.10:12"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Iden, farewell, and be proud of thy victory. Tell\nKent from me, she hath lost her best man, and exhort\nall the world to be cowards; for I, that never\nfeared any, am vanquished by famine, not by valour.","sourceLine":"4.10.72","speaker":"CADE","id":"2henryvi:4.10:13"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Dies","id":"2henryvi:4.10:14"},{"kind":"speech","text":"How much thou wrong'st me, heaven be my judge.\nDie, damned wretch, the curse of her that bare thee;\nAnd as I thrust thy body in with my sword,\nSo wish I, I might thrust thy soul to hell.\nHence will I drag thee headlong by the heels\nUnto a dunghill which shall be thy grave,\nAnd there cut off thy most ungracious head;\nWhich I will bear in triumph to the king,\nLeaving thy trunk for crows to feed upon.","sourceLine":"4.10.76","speaker":"IDEN","id":"2henryvi:4.10:15"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit","id":"2henryvi:4.10:16"}],"scene":10},{"id":"5.1","label":"Act 5 · SCENE I. Fields between Dartford and Blackheath.","act":5,"sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/2henryvi/full.html","blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter YORK, and his army of Irish, with drum and colours","id":"2henryvi:5.1:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"From Ireland thus comes York to claim his right,\nAnd pluck the crown from feeble Henry's head:\nRing, bells, aloud; burn, bonfires, clear and bright,\nTo entertain great England's lawful king.\nAh! sancta majestas, who would not buy thee dear?\nLet them obey that know not how to rule;\nThis hand was made to handle naught but gold.\nI cannot give due action to my words,\nExcept a sword or sceptre balance it:\nA sceptre shall it have, have I a soul,\nOn which I'll toss the flower-de-luce of France.","sourceLine":"5.1.1","speaker":"YORK","id":"2henryvi:5.1:2"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter BUCKINGHAM","id":"2henryvi:5.1:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Whom have we here? Buckingham, to disturb me?\nThe king hath sent him, sure: I must dissemble.","sourceLine":"5.1.12","speaker":"YORK","id":"2henryvi:5.1:4"},{"kind":"speech","text":"York, if thou meanest well, I greet thee well.","sourceLine":"5.1.14","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"2henryvi:5.1:5"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Humphrey of Buckingham, I accept thy greeting.\nArt thou a messenger, or come of pleasure?","sourceLine":"5.1.15","speaker":"YORK","id":"2henryvi:5.1:6"},{"kind":"speech","text":"A messenger from Henry, our dread liege,\nTo know the reason of these arms in peace;\nOr why thou, being a subject as I am,\nAgainst thy oath and true allegiance sworn,\nShould raise so great a power without his leave,\nOr dare to bring thy force so near the court.","sourceLine":"5.1.17","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"2henryvi:5.1:7"},{"kind":"speech","text":"[Aside] Scarce can I speak, my choler is so great:\nO, I could hew up rocks and fight with flint,\nI am so angry at these abject terms;\nAnd now, like Ajax Telamonius,\nOn sheep or oxen could I spend my fury.\nI am far better born than is the king,\nMore like a king, more kingly in my thoughts:\nBut I must make fair weather yet a while,\nTill Henry be more weak and I more strong,--\nBuckingham, I prithee, pardon me,\nThat I have given no answer all this while;\nMy mind was troubled with deep melancholy.\nThe cause why I have brought this army hither\nIs to remove proud Somerset from the king,\nSeditious to his grace and to the state.","sourceLine":"5.1.23","speaker":"YORK","id":"2henryvi:5.1:8"},{"kind":"speech","text":"That is too much presumption on thy part:\nBut if thy arms be to no other end,\nThe king hath yielded unto thy demand:\nThe Duke of Somerset is in the Tower.","sourceLine":"5.1.38","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"2henryvi:5.1:9"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Upon thine honour, is he prisoner?","sourceLine":"5.1.42","speaker":"YORK","id":"2henryvi:5.1:10"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Upon mine honour, he is prisoner.","sourceLine":"5.1.43","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"2henryvi:5.1:11"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Then, Buckingham, I do dismiss my powers.\nSoldiers, I thank you all; disperse yourselves;\nMeet me to-morrow in St. George's field,\nYou shall have pay and every thing you wish.\nAnd let my sovereign, virtuous Henry,\nCommand my eldest son, nay, all my sons,\nAs pledges of my fealty and love;\nI'll send them all as willing as I live:\nLands, goods, horse, armour, any thing I have,\nIs his to use, so Somerset may die.","sourceLine":"5.1.44","speaker":"YORK","id":"2henryvi:5.1:12"},{"kind":"speech","text":"York, I commend this kind submission:\nWe twain will go into his highness' tent.","sourceLine":"5.1.54","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"2henryvi:5.1:13"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter KING HENRY VI and Attendants","id":"2henryvi:5.1:14"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Buckingham, doth York intend no harm to us,\nThat thus he marcheth with thee arm in arm?","sourceLine":"5.1.56","speaker":"KING HENRY VI","id":"2henryvi:5.1:15"},{"kind":"speech","text":"In all submission and humility\nYork doth present himself unto your highness.","sourceLine":"5.1.58","speaker":"YORK","id":"2henryvi:5.1:16"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Then what intends these forces thou dost bring?","sourceLine":"5.1.60","speaker":"KING HENRY VI","id":"2henryvi:5.1:17"},{"kind":"speech","text":"To heave the traitor Somerset from hence,\nAnd fight against that monstrous rebel Cade,\nWho since I heard to be discomfited.","sourceLine":"5.1.61","speaker":"YORK","id":"2henryvi:5.1:18"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter IDEN, with CADE'S head","id":"2henryvi:5.1:19"},{"kind":"speech","text":"If one so rude and of so mean condition\nMay pass into the presence of a king,\nLo, I present your grace a traitor's head,\nThe head of Cade, whom I in combat slew.","sourceLine":"5.1.64","speaker":"IDEN","id":"2henryvi:5.1:20"},{"kind":"speech","text":"The head of Cade! Great God, how just art Thou!\nO, let me view his visage, being dead,\nThat living wrought me such exceeding trouble.\nTell me, my friend, art thou the man that slew him?","sourceLine":"5.1.68","speaker":"KING HENRY VI","id":"2henryvi:5.1:21"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I was, an't like your majesty.","sourceLine":"5.1.72","speaker":"IDEN","id":"2henryvi:5.1:22"},{"kind":"speech","text":"How art thou call'd? and what is thy degree?","sourceLine":"5.1.73","speaker":"KING HENRY VI","id":"2henryvi:5.1:23"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Alexander Iden, that's my name;\nA poor esquire of Kent, that loves his king.","sourceLine":"5.1.74","speaker":"IDEN","id":"2henryvi:5.1:24"},{"kind":"speech","text":"So please it you, my lord, 'twere not amiss\nHe were created knight for his good service.","sourceLine":"5.1.76","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"2henryvi:5.1:25"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Iden, kneel down.","sourceLine":"5.1.78","speaker":"KING HENRY VI","id":"2henryvi:5.1:26"},{"kind":"stage","text":"He kneels","id":"2henryvi:5.1:27"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Rise up a knight.\nWe give thee for reward a thousand marks,\nAnd will that thou henceforth attend on us.","sourceLine":"5.1.79","speaker":"KING HENRY VI","id":"2henryvi:5.1:28"},{"kind":"speech","text":"May Iden live to merit such a bounty.\nAnd never live but true unto his liege!","sourceLine":"5.1.82","speaker":"IDEN","id":"2henryvi:5.1:29"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Rises","id":"2henryvi:5.1:30"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter QUEEN MARGARET and SOMERSET","id":"2henryvi:5.1:31"},{"kind":"speech","text":"See, Buckingham, Somerset comes with the queen:\nGo, bid her hide him quickly from the duke.","sourceLine":"5.1.84","speaker":"KING HENRY VI","id":"2henryvi:5.1:32"},{"kind":"speech","text":"For thousand Yorks he shall not hide his head,\nBut boldly stand and front him to his face.","sourceLine":"5.1.86","speaker":"QUEEN MARGARET","id":"2henryvi:5.1:33"},{"kind":"speech","text":"How now! is Somerset at liberty?\nThen, York, unloose thy long-imprison'd thoughts,\nAnd let thy tongue be equal with thy heart.\nShall I endure the sight of Somerset?\nFalse king! why hast thou broken faith with me,\nKnowing how hardly I can brook abuse?\nKing did I call thee? no, thou art not king,\nNot fit to govern and rule multitudes,\nWhich darest not, no, nor canst not rule a traitor.\nThat head of thine doth not become a crown;\nThy hand is made to grasp a palmer's staff,\nAnd not to grace an awful princely sceptre.\nThat gold must round engirt these brows of mine,\nWhose smile and frown, like to Achilles' spear,\nIs able with the change to kill and cure.\nHere is a hand to hold a sceptre up\nAnd with the same to act controlling laws.\nGive place: by heaven, thou shalt rule no more\nO'er him whom heaven created for thy ruler.","sourceLine":"5.1.88","speaker":"YORK","id":"2henryvi:5.1:34"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O monstrous traitor! I arrest thee, York,\nOf capital treason 'gainst the king and crown;\nObey, audacious traitor; kneel for grace.","sourceLine":"5.1.107","speaker":"SOMERSET","id":"2henryvi:5.1:35"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Wouldst have me kneel? first let me ask of these,\nIf they can brook I bow a knee to man.\nSirrah, call in my sons to be my bail;","sourceLine":"5.1.110","speaker":"YORK","id":"2henryvi:5.1:36"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit Attendant","id":"2henryvi:5.1:37"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I know, ere they will have me go to ward,\nThey'll pawn their swords for my enfranchisement.","sourceLine":"5.1.113","speaker":"YORK","id":"2henryvi:5.1:38"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Call hither Clifford! bid him come amain,\nTo say if that the bastard boys of York\nShall be the surety for their traitor father.","sourceLine":"5.1.115","speaker":"QUEEN MARGARET","id":"2henryvi:5.1:39"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit BUCKINGHAM","id":"2henryvi:5.1:40"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O blood-besotted Neapolitan,\nOutcast of Naples, England's bloody scourge!\nThe sons of York, thy betters in their birth,\nShall be their father's bail; and bane to those\nThat for my surety will refuse the boys!","sourceLine":"5.1.118","speaker":"YORK","id":"2henryvi:5.1:41"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter EDWARD and RICHARD","id":"2henryvi:5.1:42"},{"kind":"speech","text":"See where they come: I'll warrant they'll\nmake it good.","sourceLine":"5.1.123","speaker":"YORK","id":"2henryvi:5.1:43"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter CLIFFORD and YOUNG CLIFFORD","id":"2henryvi:5.1:44"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And here comes Clifford to deny their bail.","sourceLine":"5.1.125","speaker":"QUEEN MARGARET","id":"2henryvi:5.1:45"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Health and all happiness to my lord the king!","sourceLine":"5.1.126","speaker":"CLIFFORD","id":"2henryvi:5.1:46"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Kneels","id":"2henryvi:5.1:47"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I thank thee, Clifford: say, what news with thee?\nNay, do not fright us with an angry look;\nWe are thy sovereign, Clifford, kneel again;\nFor thy mistaking so, we pardon thee.","sourceLine":"5.1.127","speaker":"YORK","id":"2henryvi:5.1:48"},{"kind":"speech","text":"This is my king, York, I do not mistake;\nBut thou mistakest me much to think I do:\nTo Bedlam with him! is the man grown mad?","sourceLine":"5.1.131","speaker":"CLIFFORD","id":"2henryvi:5.1:49"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ay, Clifford; a bedlam and ambitious humour\nMakes him oppose himself against his king.","sourceLine":"5.1.134","speaker":"KING HENRY VI","id":"2henryvi:5.1:50"},{"kind":"speech","text":"He is a traitor; let him to the Tower,\nAnd chop away that factious pate of his.","sourceLine":"5.1.136","speaker":"CLIFFORD","id":"2henryvi:5.1:51"},{"kind":"speech","text":"He is arrested, but will not obey;\nHis sons, he says, shall give their words for him.","sourceLine":"5.1.138","speaker":"QUEEN MARGARET","id":"2henryvi:5.1:52"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Will you not, sons?","sourceLine":"5.1.140","speaker":"YORK","id":"2henryvi:5.1:53"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ay, noble father, if our words will serve.","sourceLine":"5.1.141","speaker":"EDWARD","id":"2henryvi:5.1:54"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And if words will not, then our weapons shall.","sourceLine":"5.1.142","speaker":"RICHARD","id":"2henryvi:5.1:55"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Why, what a brood of traitors have we here!","sourceLine":"5.1.143","speaker":"CLIFFORD","id":"2henryvi:5.1:56"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Look in a glass, and call thy image so:\nI am thy king, and thou a false-heart traitor.\nCall hither to the stake my two brave bears,\nThat with the very shaking of their chains\nThey may astonish these fell-lurking curs:\nBid Salisbury and Warwick come to me.","sourceLine":"5.1.144","speaker":"YORK","id":"2henryvi:5.1:57"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter the WARWICK and SALISBURY","id":"2henryvi:5.1:58"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Are these thy bears? we'll bait thy bears to death.\nAnd manacle the bear-ward in their chains,\nIf thou darest bring them to the baiting place.","sourceLine":"5.1.150","speaker":"CLIFFORD","id":"2henryvi:5.1:59"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Oft have I seen a hot o'erweening cur\nRun back and bite, because he was withheld;\nWho, being suffer'd with the bear's fell paw,\nHath clapp'd his tail between his legs and cried:\nAnd such a piece of service will you do,\nIf you oppose yourselves to match Lord Warwick.","sourceLine":"5.1.153","speaker":"RICHARD","id":"2henryvi:5.1:60"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Hence, heap of wrath, foul indigested lump,\nAs crooked in thy manners as thy shape!","sourceLine":"5.1.159","speaker":"CLIFFORD","id":"2henryvi:5.1:61"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Nay, we shall heat you thoroughly anon.","sourceLine":"5.1.161","speaker":"YORK","id":"2henryvi:5.1:62"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Take heed, lest by your heat you burn yourselves.","sourceLine":"5.1.162","speaker":"CLIFFORD","id":"2henryvi:5.1:63"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Why, Warwick, hath thy knee forgot to bow?\nOld Salisbury, shame to thy silver hair,\nThou mad misleader of thy brain-sick son!\nWhat, wilt thou on thy death-bed play the ruffian,\nAnd seek for sorrow with thy spectacles?\nO, where is faith? O, where is loyalty?\nIf it be banish'd from the frosty head,\nWhere shall it find a harbour in the earth?\nWilt thou go dig a grave to find out war,\nAnd shame thine honourable age with blood?\nWhy art thou old, and want'st experience?\nOr wherefore dost abuse it, if thou hast it?\nFor shame! in duty bend thy knee to me\nThat bows unto the grave with mickle age.","sourceLine":"5.1.163","speaker":"KING HENRY VI","id":"2henryvi:5.1:64"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My lord, I have consider'd with myself\nThe title of this most renowned duke;\nAnd in my conscience do repute his grace\nThe rightful heir to England's royal seat.","sourceLine":"5.1.177","speaker":"SALISBURY","id":"2henryvi:5.1:65"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Hast thou not sworn allegiance unto me?","sourceLine":"5.1.181","speaker":"KING HENRY VI","id":"2henryvi:5.1:66"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I have.","sourceLine":"5.1.182","speaker":"SALISBURY","id":"2henryvi:5.1:67"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Canst thou dispense with heaven for such an oath?","sourceLine":"5.1.183","speaker":"KING HENRY VI","id":"2henryvi:5.1:68"},{"kind":"speech","text":"It is great sin to swear unto a sin,\nBut greater sin to keep a sinful oath.\nWho can be bound by any solemn vow\nTo do a murderous deed, to rob a man,\nTo force a spotless virgin's chastity,\nTo reave the orphan of his patrimony,\nTo wring the widow from her custom'd right,\nAnd have no other reason for this wrong\nBut that he was bound by a solemn oath?","sourceLine":"5.1.184","speaker":"SALISBURY","id":"2henryvi:5.1:69"},{"kind":"speech","text":"A subtle traitor needs no sophister.","sourceLine":"5.1.193","speaker":"QUEEN MARGARET","id":"2henryvi:5.1:70"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Call Buckingham, and bid him arm himself.","sourceLine":"5.1.194","speaker":"KING HENRY VI","id":"2henryvi:5.1:71"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Call Buckingham, and all the friends thou hast,\nI am resolved for death or dignity.","sourceLine":"5.1.195","speaker":"YORK","id":"2henryvi:5.1:72"},{"kind":"speech","text":"The first I warrant thee, if dreams prove true.","sourceLine":"5.1.197","speaker":"CLIFFORD","id":"2henryvi:5.1:73"},{"kind":"speech","text":"You were best to go to bed and dream again,\nTo keep thee from the tempest of the field.","sourceLine":"5.1.198","speaker":"WARWICK","id":"2henryvi:5.1:74"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I am resolved to bear a greater storm\nThan any thou canst conjure up to-day;\nAnd that I'll write upon thy burgonet,\nMight I but know thee by thy household badge.","sourceLine":"5.1.200","speaker":"CLIFFORD","id":"2henryvi:5.1:75"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Now, by my father's badge, old Nevil's crest,\nThe rampant bear chain'd to the ragged staff,\nThis day I'll wear aloft my burgonet,\nAs on a mountain top the cedar shows\nThat keeps his leaves in spite of any storm,\nEven to affright thee with the view thereof.","sourceLine":"5.1.204","speaker":"WARWICK","id":"2henryvi:5.1:76"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And from thy burgonet I'll rend thy bear\nAnd tread it under foot with all contempt,\nDespite the bear-ward that protects the bear.","sourceLine":"5.1.210","speaker":"CLIFFORD","id":"2henryvi:5.1:77"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And so to arms, victorious father,\nTo quell the rebels and their complices.","sourceLine":"5.1.213","speaker":"YOUNG CLIFFORD","id":"2henryvi:5.1:78"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Fie! charity, for shame! speak not in spite,\nFor you shall sup with Jesu Christ to-night.","sourceLine":"5.1.215","speaker":"RICHARD","id":"2henryvi:5.1:79"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Foul stigmatic, that's more than thou canst tell.","sourceLine":"5.1.217","speaker":"YOUNG CLIFFORD","id":"2henryvi:5.1:80"},{"kind":"speech","text":"If not in heaven, you'll surely sup in hell.","sourceLine":"5.1.218","speaker":"RICHARD","id":"2henryvi:5.1:81"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt severally","id":"2henryvi:5.1:82"}],"scene":1},{"id":"5.2","label":"Act 5 · SCENE II. Saint Alban's.","act":5,"sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/2henryvi/full.html","blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Alarums to the battle. Enter WARWICK","id":"2henryvi:5.2:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Clifford of Cumberland, 'tis Warwick calls:\nAnd if thou dost not hide thee from the bear,\nNow, when the angry trumpet sounds alarum\nAnd dead men's cries do fill the empty air,\nClifford, I say, come forth and fight with me:\nProud northern lord, Clifford of Cumberland,\nWarwick is hoarse with calling thee to arms.","sourceLine":"5.2.1","speaker":"WARWICK","id":"2henryvi:5.2:2"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter YORK","id":"2henryvi:5.2:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"How now, my noble lord? what, all afoot?","sourceLine":"5.2.8","speaker":"WARWICK","id":"2henryvi:5.2:4"},{"kind":"speech","text":"The deadly-handed Clifford slew my steed,\nBut match to match I have encounter'd him\nAnd made a prey for carrion kites and crows\nEven of the bonny beast he loved so well.","sourceLine":"5.2.9","speaker":"YORK","id":"2henryvi:5.2:5"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter CLIFFORD","id":"2henryvi:5.2:6"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Of one or both of us the time is come.","sourceLine":"5.2.13","speaker":"WARWICK","id":"2henryvi:5.2:7"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Hold, Warwick, seek thee out some other chase,\nFor I myself must hunt this deer to death.","sourceLine":"5.2.14","speaker":"YORK","id":"2henryvi:5.2:8"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Then, nobly, York; 'tis for a crown thou fight'st.\nAs I intend, Clifford, to thrive to-day,\nIt grieves my soul to leave thee unassail'd.","sourceLine":"5.2.16","speaker":"WARWICK","id":"2henryvi:5.2:9"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit","id":"2henryvi:5.2:10"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What seest thou in me, York? why dost thou pause?","sourceLine":"5.2.19","speaker":"CLIFFORD","id":"2henryvi:5.2:11"},{"kind":"speech","text":"With thy brave bearing should I be in love,\nBut that thou art so fast mine enemy.","sourceLine":"5.2.20","speaker":"YORK","id":"2henryvi:5.2:12"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Nor should thy prowess want praise and esteem,\nBut that 'tis shown ignobly and in treason.","sourceLine":"5.2.22","speaker":"CLIFFORD","id":"2henryvi:5.2:13"},{"kind":"speech","text":"So let it help me now against thy sword\nAs I in justice and true right express it.","sourceLine":"5.2.24","speaker":"YORK","id":"2henryvi:5.2:14"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My soul and body on the action both!","sourceLine":"5.2.26","speaker":"CLIFFORD","id":"2henryvi:5.2:15"},{"kind":"speech","text":"A dreadful lay! Address thee instantly.","sourceLine":"5.2.27","speaker":"YORK","id":"2henryvi:5.2:16"},{"kind":"stage","text":"They fight, and CLIFFORD falls","id":"2henryvi:5.2:17"},{"kind":"speech","text":"La fin couronne les oeuvres.","sourceLine":"5.2.28","speaker":"CLIFFORD","id":"2henryvi:5.2:18"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Dies","id":"2henryvi:5.2:19"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Thus war hath given thee peace, for thou art still.\nPeace with his soul, heaven, if it be thy will!","sourceLine":"5.2.29","speaker":"YORK","id":"2henryvi:5.2:20"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit","id":"2henryvi:5.2:21"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter YOUNG CLIFFORD","id":"2henryvi:5.2:22"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Shame and confusion! all is on the rout;\nFear frames disorder, and disorder wounds\nWhere it should guard. O war, thou son of hell,\nWhom angry heavens do make their minister\nThrow in the frozen bosoms of our part\nHot coals of vengeance! Let no soldier fly.\nHe that is truly dedicate to war\nHath no self-love, nor he that loves himself\nHath not essentially but by circumstance\nThe name of valour.","sourceLine":"5.2.31","speaker":"YOUNG CLIFFORD","id":"2henryvi:5.2:23"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Seeing his dead father","id":"2henryvi:5.2:24"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O, let the vile world end,\nAnd the premised flames of the last day\nKnit earth and heaven together!\nNow let the general trumpet blow his blast,\nParticularities and petty sounds\nTo cease! Wast thou ordain'd, dear father,\nTo lose thy youth in peace, and to achieve\nThe silver livery of advised age,\nAnd, in thy reverence and thy chair-days, thus\nTo die in ruffian battle? Even at this sight\nMy heart is turn'd to stone: and while 'tis mine,\nIt shall be stony. York not our old men spares;\nNo more will I their babes: tears virginal\nShall be to me even as the dew to fire,\nAnd beauty that the tyrant oft reclaims\nShall to my flaming wrath be oil and flax.\nHenceforth I will not have to do with pity:\nMeet I an infant of the house of York,\nInto as many gobbets will I cut it\nAs wild Medea young Absyrtus did:\nIn cruelty will I seek out my fame.\nCome, thou new ruin of old Clifford's house:\nAs did AEneas old Anchises bear,\nSo bear I thee upon my manly shoulders;\nBut then AEneas bare a living load,\nNothing so heavy as these woes of mine.","sourceLine":"5.2.41","speaker":"YOUNG CLIFFORD","id":"2henryvi:5.2:25"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit, bearing off his father","id":"2henryvi:5.2:26"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter RICHARD and SOMERSET to fight. SOMERSET is killed","id":"2henryvi:5.2:27"},{"kind":"speech","text":"So, lie thou there;\nFor underneath an alehouse' paltry sign,\nThe Castle in Saint Alban's, Somerset\nHath made the wizard famous in his death.\nSword, hold thy temper; heart, be wrathful still:\nPriests pray for enemies, but princes kill.","sourceLine":"5.2.67","speaker":"RICHARD","id":"2henryvi:5.2:28"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit","id":"2henryvi:5.2:29"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Fight: excursions. Enter KING HENRY VI, QUEEN MARGARET, and others","id":"2henryvi:5.2:30"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Away, my lord! you are slow; for shame, away!","sourceLine":"5.2.73","speaker":"QUEEN MARGARET","id":"2henryvi:5.2:31"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Can we outrun the heavens? good Margaret, stay.","sourceLine":"5.2.74","speaker":"KING HENRY VI","id":"2henryvi:5.2:32"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What are you made of? you'll nor fight nor fly:\nNow is it manhood, wisdom and defence,\nTo give the enemy way, and to secure us\nBy what we can, which can no more but fly.","sourceLine":"5.2.75","speaker":"QUEEN MARGARET","id":"2henryvi:5.2:33"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Alarum afar off","id":"2henryvi:5.2:34"},{"kind":"speech","text":"If you be ta'en, we then should see the bottom\nOf all our fortunes: but if we haply scape,\nAs well we may, if not through your neglect,\nWe shall to London get, where you are loved\nAnd where this breach now in our fortunes made\nMay readily be stopp'd.","sourceLine":"5.2.79","speaker":"QUEEN MARGARET","id":"2henryvi:5.2:35"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Re-enter YOUNG CLIFFORD","id":"2henryvi:5.2:36"},{"kind":"speech","text":"But that my heart's on future mischief set,\nI would speak blasphemy ere bid you fly:\nBut fly you must; uncurable discomfit\nReigns in the hearts of all our present parts.\nAway, for your relief! and we will live\nTo see their day and them our fortune give:\nAway, my lord, away!","sourceLine":"5.2.85","speaker":"YOUNG CLIFFORD","id":"2henryvi:5.2:37"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt","id":"2henryvi:5.2:38"}],"scene":2},{"id":"5.3","label":"Act 5 · SCENE III. Fields near St. Alban's.","act":5,"sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/2henryvi/full.html","blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Alarum. Retreat. Enter YORK, RICHARD, WARWICK, and Soldiers, with drum and colours","id":"2henryvi:5.3:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Of Salisbury, who can report of him,\nThat winter lion, who in rage forgets\nAged contusions and all brush of time,\nAnd, like a gallant in the brow of youth,\nRepairs him with occasion? This happy day\nIs not itself, nor have we won one foot,\nIf Salisbury be lost.","sourceLine":"5.3.1","speaker":"YORK","id":"2henryvi:5.3:2"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My noble father,\nThree times to-day I holp him to his horse,\nThree times bestrid him; thrice I led him off,\nPersuaded him from any further act:\nBut still, where danger was, still there I met him;\nAnd like rich hangings in a homely house,\nSo was his will in his old feeble body.\nBut, noble as he is, look where he comes.","sourceLine":"5.3.8","speaker":"RICHARD","id":"2henryvi:5.3:3"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter SALISBURY","id":"2henryvi:5.3:4"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Now, by my sword, well hast thou fought to-day;\nBy the mass, so did we all. I thank you, Richard:\nGod knows how long it is I have to live;\nAnd it hath pleased him that three times to-day\nYou have defended me from imminent death.\nWell, lords, we have not got that which we have:\n'Tis not enough our foes are this time fled,\nBeing opposites of such repairing nature.","sourceLine":"5.3.16","speaker":"SALISBURY","id":"2henryvi:5.3:5"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I know our safety is to follow them;\nFor, as I hear, the king is fled to London,\nTo call a present court of parliament.\nLet us pursue him ere the writs go forth.\nWhat says Lord Warwick? shall we after them?","sourceLine":"5.3.24","speaker":"YORK","id":"2henryvi:5.3:6"},{"kind":"speech","text":"After them! nay, before them, if we can.\nNow, by my faith, lords, 'twas a glorious day:\nSaint Alban's battle won by famous York\nShall be eternized in all age to come.\nSound drums and trumpets, and to London all:\nAnd more such days as these to us befall!","sourceLine":"5.3.29","speaker":"WARWICK","id":"2henryvi:5.3:7"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt","id":"2henryvi:5.3:8"}],"scene":3}],"sourceLineCount":3122,"sourceLabel":"MIT Shakespeare","sectionCount":24,"blockCount":1004,"wordCount":25476};
